export const LAENDER_REGIONEN = [
  { region: "Europe", laender: [
    { name: "Germany", iso: "DE", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The term \"German Angst\" has itself become a standing expression internationally—hardly any culture has institutionalized its own caution so consistently. This shows in daily life in a thousand places: in the world's highest insurance density, in DIN standards for almost every object, in the TÜV inspecting cars and elevators at fixed intervals, in meticulous waste separation. Savings behavior is also telling—Germans hoard cash and avoid the stock market far more than neighboring countries out of fear of loss. Even prosperity is defended over decades with the \"schwarze Null,\" the principle of incurring no new debt, no matter what. This collective vigilance is not German Angst as a cliché, but the lived motivation of \"security, structure, reliability\"—the Six in its unfiltered form.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Germany's Six shows up primarily in its social variant: safety isn't sought individually but produced through collectively binding systems—DIN standards, TÜV inspections, social insurance, a dense web of rules everyone submits to alike. Authority doesn't rest with a person but with the institution itself, trusted precisely because it applies to everyone. Alongside this, a clear self-preservation undertone is visible in private saving habits, the country's high household insurance density, and general caution with personal money. The sexual variant, which would show up as open confrontation or bold advance, recedes into the background in German culture overall—rule-conformity is preferred over open contests of strength." },
    { name: "France", iso: "FR", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Hardly any country has elevated feeling itself to an art form like France: In the café, people do not just drink coffee, but discuss the meaning of life for hours—philosophy is a mandatory subject until high school graduation, the \"bac philo.\" Fashion, from Coco Chanel to Haute Couture, understands itself not as functional clothing, but as an expression of the unmistakable. French cinema, \"cinéma d'auteur,\" to this day places the director's personal vision above mass taste. And when French people take to the streets with the \"Gilets Jaunes\" or other protests, it is almost always also about being heard and taken seriously as an individual—not just about concrete demands. The \"je ne sais quoi,\" the indescribable, is itself a national myth: the conviction that there is something that cannot be explained, only felt.", subtyp: "sx4", subtypText: "France likely leans toward sexual Four: a culture of seduction, grand passion, the \"coup de foudre\"—love and desire aren't muted but cultivated and celebrated, from literature to cinema. The intense, often dramatic pursuit of the one special connection isn't the exception here, it's the ideal. A social undertone shows in how deeply art and intellect are institutionalized as collective status—being recognized as an individualist and honored in salons, arts pages, and film festivals is a form of social achievement. Self-preservation Four, which would withdraw quietly instead, clearly recedes behind this publicly lived, often theatrical passion." },
    { name: "Portugal", iso: "PT", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "\"Saudade\" is not a literary concept, but a word that every child knows and uses in everyday life—the wistful longing for something that may never return. In Fado, sung in small, smoky venues from Lisbon to Coimbra, this feeling is celebrated anew every evening, often to the point of tears. The blue-and-white Azulejo tiles on countless house facades tell of the age of great discoveries—a lost greatness that is mourned to this day. Even the huge Portuguese diaspora, millions of people worldwide, holds onto this longing for the homeland with an intensity that is rarely named as literally as here. The poet Fernando Pessoa invented multiple literary identities for himself—a symbol of how deeply this culture struggles with longing, complexity, and melancholy.", subtyp: "se4", subtypText: "Portugal's Four appears mainly self-preservation-toned: \"saudade\" isn't loud, outward-facing drama but a quiet longing woven into daily life—carried while working, cooking, going on, humming in the background. It's a melancholy expressed through endurance rather than eruption. A social share is visible in the collective memory of the seafaring nation's lost greatness and in the worldwide diaspora that carries and tends this longing together. Sexual Four, with its open, competing intensity, barely surfaces in Portugal's comparatively reserved emotional culture." },
    { name: "Italy", iso: "IT", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "The Sunday family table, where three generations gather for hours, is not an exception, but a lived norm. \"La mamma\" cooks because food here is synonymous with love—a plate that is not filled to the brim is seen as a sign of a lack of care. Adult children often live with their parents well into their thirties, not out of a lack of alternatives, but because the family is felt to be a nest that one does not leave prematurely. The evening \"passeggiata,\" the shared stroll through the village or old town, serves solely to be seen and to greet others—a daily ritual of belonging. And the institution of godparents, the \"padrini,\" anchors every child in a network of additional care that extends beyond the nuclear family.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Italy shows up very clearly as social: care isn't confined to the nuclear family but organized across a wide net of relatives, neighbors, and godparents—the institution of the \"padrini\" anchors every child in several circles of care at once. The evening \"passeggiata\" is a public ritual of being seen and greeted, where belonging is confirmed in the social space rather than in a single pairing. A self-preservation undertone shows in the weight placed on shared meals and the family's material well-being—the overfilled plate as proof of love. Sexual Two, which seeks closeness through one exclusive bond, recedes behind this broad, communal web of care." },
    { name: "Spain", iso: "ES", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Spanish daily rhythm—late lunch, Siesta, dinner not until 10 PM—is an open refusal to submit to foreign norms of efficiency; one lives by one's own rules. In bullfighting and Flamenco, confrontation and passion are not disguised, but elevated to an art form—pain, pride, and anger are expressed with the whole body, not hidden. Regional identities like Catalan or Basque are defended with a vehemence that reaches as far as independence efforts—yielding is seen as weakness. The memory of the Civil War remains emotionally raw even now; monument disputes and exhumations are current topics, not a closed chapter. Even in everyday life, greetings are physically direct—two kisses, a firm handshake, eye contact that does not flinch.", subtyp: "sx8", subtypText: "Spain likely leans toward sexual Eight: bullfighting and flamenco aren't muted but physically immediate expressions of strength, pride, and confrontation—intensity is sought, not avoided. This directness also shows in everyday life, in unflinching eye contact, in close physical greetings. A strong social undertone appears in the regional independence movements—Catalan or Basque identity is defended collectively and vehemently against perceived outside control, a group Eight fighting for the collective. Self-preservation Eight, which would show up as quiet material security-seeking, clearly recedes behind this openly lived, physical, collective strength." },
    { name: "Greece", iso: "GR", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Greek word \"meraki\"—doing something with soul and devotion, without haste—describes a fundamental attitude that shows just as much in sitting for hours in the Kafenio as it does in the kitchen. Hospitality, \"philoxenia,\" has been a sacred duty since antiquity: Strangers are hosted even before one knows their name. Even the deep economic crisis after 2010, with mass unemployment and empty state coffers, did not lead to chaos or civil war, but was borne with remarkable collective composure. Island life, where ferry schedules are treated more as a guideline than a law, shapes a concept of time that refuses to be rushed. The Greek Orthodox Church, with its endless liturgies and emphasis on composure before God, further reinforces this fundamental cultural melody of harmony.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Greece shows up mainly as social: \"philoxenia,\" the sacred duty of hospitality toward strangers, is a deeply communal principle—harmony isn't sought for oneself but produced through the well-being and inclusion of others. The way the post-2010 economic crisis was collectively endured without chaos also speaks to a socially anchored calm. A self-preservation undertone shows in the relaxed island life, where ferry schedules are more guideline than law—a refusal to be rushed by outside timetables. Sexual Nine, which seeks harmony through one intense fusion, recedes behind this broad, communal ease." },
    { name: "Great Britain", iso: "GB", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Standing in line, the \"queue,\" is almost sacred—cutting in line is considered one of the greatest social sins because it violates distance and order between strangers. Dry, often subtle British humor functions as a shield: One keeps feelings at arm's length by wrapping them in irony. Talking about the weather is notorious—a safe, innocuous topic that skillfully bypasses genuine intimacy. The Oxbridge tutorial system, in which students pore over a text alone for weeks before discussing it with a tutor, celebrates independent, withdrawn thinking as the highest academic virtue. And daily tea at five is a ritual of the controlled pause—a distancing from the day in a clearly delineated, ritualized form.", subtyp: "so5", subtypText: "The UK likely shows a socially toned Five: the sacred \"queue\" is an implicit but binding rulebook that orders and protects distance between strangers—knowing the correct social form, not personal closeness, is what holds the group together. The Oxbridge tutorial system, where intellectual authority is earned through quiet solo work and then presented within a clearly regulated format, reinforces this picture of an institutionally anchored, detached observer role. A self-preservation share shows in the daily five o'clock tea ritual, a clearly bounded, personal retreat from the day. Sexual Five, which would show up as intense devotion to one single thing or person, recedes behind this reserved, socially ordered restraint." },
    { name: "Ireland", iso: "IE", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "The pub is not primarily a place for drinking, but the social living room of the nation—here people meet to tell stories, \"the craic,\" that untranslatable mix of gossip, wit, and shared joy. Traditional music sessions arise spontaneously when someone brings a fiddle or a Bodhrán, without planning, without a stage. The humor about their own, often difficult history—from the Great Famine to the colonial era—is characteristically Irish: People laugh at the heavy things instead of breaking under them. Millions of emigrants celebrate St. Patrick's Day worldwide with an exuberance that keeps identity alive across distance. And Irish literature, from Joyce to Wilde, is marked by sparkling wit that seeks lightness even in gravity.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Ireland shows a clearly social Seven: the pub as the \"nation's social living room,\" the spontaneous music sessions, \"the craic\"—all of this is joy that explicitly unfolds in the group, not alone. St. Patrick's Day, celebrated together by the diaspora worldwide, likewise shows how much identity and joy are kept alive here through shared, collective ritual. Humor about the country's own hard history is told and passed on together, not processed alone. Self-preservation or sexual variants, which would show up as individual enjoyment or exclusive one-on-one closeness, recede overall behind Ireland's markedly sociable, group-oriented joy." },
    { name: "Switzerland", iso: "CH", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "Direct democracy in Switzerland means that several times a year, precisely formulated ballot texts must be available for highly complex issues—inaccuracy here is not an option, but a constitutional problem. Trains run precisely to the minute, and delays of just a few minutes are discussed nationwide—punctuality is national pride. The federal system with 26 cantons, each having its own laws, demands a high degree of clean regulatory architecture for the whole thing to function. The banking sector, known for centuries for discretion and reliability, relies on the same principle: order and trust through dependability. Even mountain rescue is highly organized and disciplined—here precision literally matters over life and death.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Switzerland shows a clearly social One: direct democracy requires complex issues to be formulated collectively, precisely, and bindingly for everyone—rightness here isn't an individual claim but a shared standard to be met together. The federal system of 26 cantons, which must agree on a common rulebook, reinforces this picture of a collectively held order. A self-preservation undertone shows in the proverbial personal punctuality and the disciplined, precise handling of one's own affairs, as in banking. Sexual One, which would carry its principles outward with missionary intensity, recedes behind this more institutional, collectively coordinated love of order." },
    { name: "Austria", iso: "AT", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Viennese coffee house is an institution of undisturbed lingering: A single coffee entitles one to sit, read, and do nothing for hours—no one is rushed to leave. The \"Schmäh,\" that typical Austrian mixture of charm, irony, and evasiveness, is a tool with which conflicts are elegantly bypassed rather than fought out. Everlasting neutrality, a constitutional principle since 1955, is more than foreign policy—it is an expression of a national self-understanding to avoid getting dragged into confrontations wherever possible. Alpine slowness, deliberate \"nicht hudeln\" (not rushing), is considered a virtue in many places rather than sloth. And the waltz, danced in a steady three-quarter time, is a symbol of the Austrian preference for a harmonious, unagitated flow over abrupt breaks.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Austria likely shows a self-preservation-toned Nine: the Viennese coffeehouse as a place of undisturbed, personal lingering, the alpine slowness, the deliberate refusal to rush—personal, private coziness takes priority here over the group. The \"Schmäh,\" used to charmingly sidestep conflict, serves one's own inner peace more than active group harmony. A social share shows in perpetual neutrality as a collective self-image that has shaped Austrian identity outwardly since 1955. Sexual Nine, with its intense fusion into a single relationship, clearly recedes behind this cozy, more withdrawn basic stance." },
    { name: "Netherlands", iso: "NL", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The \"Poldermodell\"—the search for consensus between employers, trade unions, and the state until all sides can agree—has been the founding principle of Dutch politics for decades. This culture of consensus dates back centuries: Without joint, cooperative water management against the North Sea, the country in its current form would not exist at all. The liberal policy toward cannabis and sex work is less a breach of rules and more a pragmatic effort to defuse conflicts instead of criminalizing and escalating them. The bicycle as the preferred mode of transport creates a shared, calm public space where consideration is practiced routine. The much-cited Dutch directness serves not as a display of power, but aims to prevent misunderstandings from the outset—ultimately another service to harmony.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "The Dutch Nine shows up clearly as social: the \"polder model\" is literally an institution of collective consensus-seeking between employers, unions, and the state—harmony isn't sought individually but negotiated together until everyone can agree. The centuries-old necessity of banding together against the North Sea has deeply anchored this consensus culture. A self-preservation undertone shows in the pragmatic, undramatic handling of sensitive issues like cannabis or sex work—defusing practically rather than letting things escalate. The much-cited Dutch directness ultimately serves to avoid everyday misunderstandings, not confrontation. Sexual Nine, with its intense fusion, clearly recedes behind this broad, institutionalized consensus culture." },
    { name: "Sweden", iso: "SE", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Sweden is one of the few countries that has made its own social openness exportable: IKEA sells not just furniture, but a democratic lifestyle intended to be accessible to everyone. ABBA, a global pop music export power since the seventies, stands for carefree, mass-appealing joy of life. Generous parental leave regulations—over a year, split between both parents—deliberately create space for new life models. Midsummer celebrations, with dancing around the Maypole deep into the bright night, show a culture that takes festivities and collective exuberance seriously. And social willingness to experiment—from liberal family models to new housing arrangements—is celebrated here as progress rather than perceived as a threat.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Sweden shows a clearly social Seven: IKEA exports not just furniture but a democratic, accessible-to-everyone outlook on life, and ABBA stands for mass-appeal, shared joy. The generous parental leave, splittable between both parents, is a collective, institutionally secured expansion of life possibilities. Midsummer celebrations, enjoyed together deep into the bright night, show exuberance as a shared, social event. A sexual undertone shows in the openness to experimenting with new, individual family and living models, celebrated as personal progress. Self-preservation Seven, which would show up as quiet, private enjoyment, recedes behind this socially shared sense of new beginnings." },
    { name: "Norway", iso: "NO", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "\"Friluftsliv,\" outdoor life, is more than a hobby—it is a deeply rooted cultural need for withdrawal into one's own untouched stillness. Almost every family owns or rents a \"hytte,\" a simple mountain or coastal cabin without electricity and running water, to which people deliberately retreat. The massive sovereign wealth fund from oil profits is managed with striking restraint—no prestige projects, but a quietly growing reserve for future generations. With just five million people spread across a vast, rugged area, expanse and solitude here are not exceptions, but daily experiences. Reticence is not considered rude, but respectful restraint toward the other person.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Norway shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Five: \"friluftsliv\" is a deliberate, individual retreat into untouched silence, the \"hytte\" without electricity or running water a deeply personal place of simplicity. Taciturnity here isn't seen as coldness but as respectful, self-contained restraint. A social undertone shows in how the enormous oil fund is managed—restrained, without prestige projects, as a quietly growing, collectively held reserve for future generations. Sexual Five, with its intense, exclusive devotion, clearly recedes behind this expansive, inward-turned Norwegian solitude." },
    { name: "Finland", iso: "FI", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "The sauna is the central place of Finnish sociability—and characteristically, people mostly remain silent there rather than talk. The internet meme \"kalsarikänni,\" drinking at home alone in underwear with no intention of going out, was coined by Finns themselves and lovingly embraced—an avowal of their own introversion. Long, dark winters require a capacity for quiet, self-sufficient endurance, known in Finnish as \"sisu.\" Nokia, once the world leader in mobile phones, emerged from an engineering culture that prioritizes substance over self-promotion. Silence in Finland is not misunderstood as a pause in conversation that must be filled, but as a fully valued, pleasant part of communication.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Finland likewise shows a predominantly self-preservation-toned Five: \"kalsarikänni\"—drinking alone at home in your underwear with no intention of going out—is a fondly embraced declaration of one's own private introversion. \"Sisu,\" the quiet, self-sufficient endurance through the long dark winters, is a deeply personal strength, not a collective ritual. A social undertone shows in Finnish engineering culture, as at Nokia, which places substance and function over self-presentation—a kind of institutionalized restraint. Silence isn't felt as a gap that needs filling but as a full part of communication. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this consistently introverted basic stance." },
    { name: "Denmark", iso: "DK", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Hygge essentially means making things cozy for oneself and, above all, for others—candles are lit when guests arrive, not just for oneself. Denmark invests an above-average share of its social budget in childcare and eldercare—caring for the weakest in the community is a political consensus, not a point of contention. The bicycle-friendly urban planning of Copenhagen, with wide, safe bike lanes, is explicitly designed to train consideration for all road users. The \"Jantelov,\" the unwritten rule not to elevate oneself above others, ultimately serves to ensure that no one in the group feels uncomfortable. Care for the well-being of others is thus not a side effect here, but the actual core of the culture.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Denmark shows a clearly social Two: at its core, \"hygge\" means making things cozy above all for others—candles are lit when guests arrive. The above-average share of the social budget spent on childcare and elder care makes caring for society's weakest a political consensus, not a point of contention. \"Jantelov,\" not placing oneself above others, ultimately serves the well-being of the whole group. A self-preservation undertone shows in practical, everyday care—such as bicycle-friendly urban planning, which trains consideration concretely into daily life. Sexual Two, with its exclusive, intense devotion to a single person, recedes behind this broad, socially anchored culture of care." },
    { name: "Iceland", iso: "IS", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "With around 380,000 inhabitants on a volcanic island in the middle of the North Atlantic, isolation here is not a metaphor, but a geographical reality. The belief in elves and nature spirits, which many Icelanders still take seriously today, reflects a culture that trusts untouched, introverted nature more than loud civilization. The medieval sagas, passed down orally for generations before being written down, bear witness to a rich inner narrative tradition despite outer austerity. Geothermal energy makes the country virtually energy-independent—another expression of self-reliance rather than dependence on the outside. Hardly any other country has as many published books per capita—quiet, inward pursuit as a national pastime.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Iceland shows a particularly pronounced self-preservation Five: with roughly 380,000 people on a volcanic island in the North Atlantic, isolation here isn't a metaphor but lived daily reality—geothermal energy makes the country nearly self-sufficient on top of that. The still-held belief in elves and nature spirits reflects more trust in quiet, introverted nature than in loud civilization. Hardly any other country publishes as many books per capita—quiet, inward-turned occupation as a national pastime. A social undertone shows in the medieval sagas, passed down orally across generations as shared cultural heritage before being written down. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this deeply rooted, insular solitude." },
    { name: "Poland", iso: "PL", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Solidarity movement of the 1980s organized underground against communist state power—vigilance against state arbitrariness is deeply anchored in the collective memory. The Catholic Church was one of the few reliable safe havens during the communist era and enjoys enormous social influence to the present day. Christmas Eve, the \"Wigilia,\" with twelve traditional dishes and an empty chair for an unexpected guest, is the most important family ritual of the year—security in the tightest circle. Millions of Poles working abroad maintain close contact via weekly calls and home visits—attachment as a safeguard against uprooting. The experience of repeated partitions and occupations (Prussia, Russia, Austria, then Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union) has left a deep national need for security that continues to influence defense policy today.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Poland shows a predominantly social Six: the Solidarity movement organized as collective, underground resistance against state arbitrariness, and the Catholic Church offered one of the few reliable, communal safe havens during the communist era. The experience of repeated partitions and occupations has likewise left a deep, nationally shared need for security. A self-preservation undertone shows in Wigilia, the year's most important family ritual, with an empty chair kept for an unexpected guest—security within the closest, private circle. The millions of Poles working abroad, who stay closely connected through weekly calls and visits home, likewise show this bonding as personal security. Sexual Six, with its bold, open confrontation, recedes behind this institutionally and family-anchored vigilance." },
    { name: "Hungary", iso: "HU", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Hungarian belongs to none of the major European language families—this linguistic island state reinforces a sense of cultural uniqueness that is cultivated with pride. The Roma musical tradition, with its emotionally charged violin music, has deeply shaped Hungarian musical culture, from folk songs to Franz Liszt's rhapsodies. Budapest's thermal baths, above all the Széchenyi Bath, are places of quiet, introspective relaxation in the middle of the city—a ritual of retreat in company. The poet Sándor Petőfi, a symbolic figure of the 1848 revolution, inextricably linked political resistance with lyrical, emotional expression. This mix of linguistic independence, musical melancholy, and poetic depth makes Hungary a country that actively celebrates its distinctiveness.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Hungary shows mainly a socially toned Four: the linguistic uniqueness of Hungarian, which belongs to no major European language family, is cultivated as collective pride, celebrated publicly rather than carried quietly. The poet Sándor Petőfi inseparably fused political resistance with lyrical expression—a publicly visible, nationally significant bond of feeling and identity. A self-preservation undertone shows in Budapest's thermal baths, places of quiet, introspective recovery in the middle of the city—a personal retreat in company. The emotionally charged Roma musical tradition adds a further sexual coloring, but recedes overall behind this publicly celebrated cultural uniqueness." },
    { name: "Czechia", iso: "CZ", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Beer is ubiquitous in Czechia—but the pub culture is contemplative rather than boisterous: long, quiet conversations over low alcohol content, no loud chanting. Puppet theater, a UNESCO cultural heritage, allows serious and satirical topics to be negotiated from a safe, observant distance. Jaroslav Hašek's \"The Good Soldier Švejk\"—the story of a man who exposes the absurdity of power through apparent naivety instead of openly rebelling—is considered a national epic. Resistance against communism, such as Charter 77, was also marked by quiet, intellectual, often ironic dissent rather than loud uprising. Franz Kafka, Prague's most famous literary son, embodies the precise, distanced observation of bureaucratic absurdity from the outside like no other.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Czechia shows a predominantly self-preservation Five: despite ever-present beer, pub culture here is contemplative rather than boisterous—long, quiet conversations rather than loud celebration. Jaroslav Hašek's \"The Good Soldier Švejk,\" who undermines the absurdity of power through apparent naivety rather than open rebellion, and Kafka's detached observation of bureaucratic absurdity both show the same stance: close observation from a safe, personal distance. A social undertone shows in the quiet but collectively organized intellectual dissent of movements like Charter 77, and in puppet theater as a shared form of indirect critique. Sexual Five clearly recedes behind this consistently observant, unruffled basic stance." },
    { name: "Russia", iso: "RU", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "Russian bureaucracy is legendary for its flood of forms and stamp culture—a system based on meticulous, hierarchical order, regardless of who is currently in power. Military parades on Red Square, with precisely synchronized steps of thousands of soldiers, are an annual showcase of disciplined precision. Chess is a national sport and an educational subject—a game that places pure strategic control over chance. The Bolshoi Ballet stands for an aesthetic in which every movement is drilled to perfection over years. From the Tsars to Stalin to the present, a pattern runs through: a \"vertical of power\" in which order is enforced from above—the longing for strong, controlling leadership is historically remarkably constant.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Russia shows a pronouncedly social One: the legendary bureaucracy, with its flood of forms and stamps, rests on meticulous, hierarchical order that applies equally to everyone, regardless of who is in charge. Military parades with thousands of soldiers marching in exact synchrony, and the Bolshoi Ballet drilled to perfection, show the same logic: discipline as a collectively performed, public ideal. The historical \"vertical of power,\" from the tsars through Stalin to today, demands order enforced from above and upheld by everyone. A self-preservation undertone shows in chess as a national sport and school subject—strategic, individually learned control over chance. Sexual One, which would push its principles outward with personal, missionary intensity, recedes behind this collective-institutional power of order." },
    { name: "Ukraine", iso: "UA", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Holodomor, the Stalin-induced famine of the 1930s with millions of deaths, is an open collective pain commemorated publicly every year—a permanent memorial to vulnerability. The embroidered \"Vyshyvanka,\" traditional shirts with regional patterns, has been worn even more consciously since the war as a symbol of protection for one's own identity. Civilian volunteer networks organizing defense, supply, and reconstruction since 2014, and increasingly since 2022, show a cohesion arising directly from threat. Borscht, cooked together at the family table, is far more than a dish—it symbolizes the home that must be defended. The sunflower fields characterizing the Ukrainian landscape have become a global symbol of resilience against threat since the war.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Ukraine shows up mainly as a socially toned Six: the civilian volunteer networks that have organized defense, supply, and reconstruction since 2014 and especially since 2022 show safety as something produced together, collectively. The annually and publicly observed Holodomor remembrance day, and the sunflower fields that have become a worldwide symbol, likewise anchor this experience in the collective, shared memory of the whole society. A self-preservation undertone shows in borscht, cooked together at the family table—a very personal symbol of the home that must be defended—and in the embroidered \"vyshyvanka,\" worn as a deeply personal symbol of protecting one's own identity. Sexual Six, with its bold, open confrontation, recedes behind this resilience carried mainly through community and family." },
    { name: "Turkey", iso: "TR", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Istanbul, the only city in the world on two continents, has seen itself as a bridge for centuries—a status cultivated with visible pride. The Grand Bazaar, in operation for over 500 years, is a symbol of a deeply rooted trade mentality in which negotiation skills and business success enjoy high esteem. Massive construction activity over the past two decades, from skyscrapers to mega-airports, is deliberately staged by the government as a symbol of national advancement. Football clubs like Galatasaray or Fenerbahçe are far more than sports—their success is celebrated as proof of collective status, with a fan intensity that draws international attention. The teahouse culture, where deals are often concluded only after lengthy social banter, inextricably links relationship-building with economic success.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Turkey likely leans toward social Three: the Grand Bazaar and the massive, state-orchestrated construction boom present economic success as a publicly visible, nationally significant status symbol—Istanbul as a bridge between continents is maintained with visible, collective pride. Football clubs like Galatasaray or Fenerbahçe are far more than sport; their success is celebrated as collective proof of status. A sexual undertone shows in teahouse culture, where deals are closed only after personal, sociable relationship-building—success through the immediate, personal bond with one's negotiating partner. Self-preservation Three, which would show up as quiet, functional work, recedes behind this publicly staged, collective pride in success." },
    { name: "Serbia", iso: "RS", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Battle of Kosovo in 1389, a historic defeat against the Ottomans, is remembered even today as a founding myth of national pride and resistance—defeat is reinterpreted here as honor. Turbo-folk music, loud, defiant, and blunt, became the culture of defiance for an internationally isolated nation in the nineties. Sports fans, especially in football and basketball, are notorious for their intensity—emotions are not held back here, but lived out with full force. The centuries-long history of resistance against the Ottoman Empire and later against international isolation has shaped a strong, uncompromising narrative of self-assertion. Direct, often blunt communication is seen here as honest, not rude—restraint is read rather as weakness.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Serbia shows up mainly as a socially toned Eight: the memory of the Battle of Kosovo as a national founding myth, and the centuries-long narrative of self-assertion against foreign powers, are collective, uncompromising strength—a group Eight standing for the whole nation. Turbo-folk as the defiant culture of an internationally isolated nation reinforces this picture of a shared, resistant stance. A sexual undertone shows in the notorious intensity of sports fans, where emotions are lived out with full physical force and no restraint. Self-preservation Eight, which would show up as quiet material security-seeking, clearly recedes behind this loudly lived, collective and personal defiance." },
    { name: "Romania", iso: "RO", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Under Ceaușescu, the Securitate monitored virtually every area of life—it was estimated that one in three Romanians was some form of informant, leaving behind a deep mistrust even toward close acquaintances that lingers to this day. The orphanage crisis after 1989, when Western media discovered tens of thousands of neglected children in state homes, is a national trauma discussed to this day. Village communities and extended families are traditionally viewed as a more reliable protection than the state—a pattern showing up in strong neighborly help and close family cohesion. The Dracula legend surrounding Vlad Țepeș, marketed for tourism, involuntarily reflects a core cultural theme: the fear of threat and the necessity of defending oneself with total severity. Romanian is also the only Romance language in Eastern Europe, surrounded by Slavic neighbors—also generating a sense of a cultural island state that fosters vigilance.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Romania shows up mainly as a self-preservation-toned Six: after the experience of Securitate surveillance, village communities and extended families are still seen as more reliable protection than the state—safety is sought in the closest, personal circle, not in institutions. Close neighborly support and strong family cohesion are direct answers to a deep-seated, very personal distrust. A sexual undertone shows in the Dracula legend around Vlad Țepeș: fear of threat isn't avoided here but answered with uncompromising, almost defiant harshness—a counterphobic note. Social Six, which would show up more through collective institutions, recedes behind this private, family-anchored vigilance." },
    { name: "Croatia", iso: "HR", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Homeland War of the 1990s against the Yugoslav People's Army is a central point of reference for national identity—commemorations and war veterans enjoy high social status. The checkerboard pattern of the national flag and coat of arms is worn with visible pride, from football jerseys to public buildings. The Croatian national football team, reaching the World Cup final in 2018, is celebrated as a symbol that a small country can hold its own against major opponents. The Dalmatian coastal culture is proud and independent, with its own dialects and customs consciously set apart from the capital, Zagreb. A certain spirit of rivalry persists even now between coastal regions and continental Croatia—each region resolutely defending its own identity.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Croatia shows up mainly as a socially toned Eight: the Homeland War as the central reference point of national identity, the high social status of war veterans, and the checkerboard coat of arms worn with pride are collective symbols of jointly asserted strength. The national football team's success is explicitly celebrated as proof that a small country can hold its own against big opponents—a group Eight par excellence. A sexual undertone shows in the pronounced rivalry between regions, such as the proudly independent Dalmatian coastal culture, which deliberately sets itself apart from the capital—assertion on a local, almost personal level. Self-preservation Eight recedes behind this openly lived, collective and regional strength." },
    { name: "Slovenia", iso: "SI", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Over 60 percent of the land area is forested—making Slovenia one of the most forested countries in Europe, and these forests are valued as places of peace and retreat, not just as a resource. Mount Triglav, the country's highest peak, is so identity-defining that it is depicted on the flag—a symbol of quiet, sublime grandeur rather than loud self-promotion. With only around two million inhabitants, Slovenia maintains a modest, unagitated public sphere that tends to avoid rather than seek international attention. Beekeeping has an almost meditative cult status in Slovenia—a quietly concentrated craft to which a dedicated holiday is assigned. Politeness and restraint toward strangers are understood here as respect rather than coldness.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Slovenia shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Five: its vast forests are valued as a personal place of calm and retreat, not just a resource, and the almost meditative practice of beekeeping stands for quiet, inward, concentrated craft. The modest, unexcited public life, which avoids international attention rather than seeking it, reinforces this picture further. A social undertone shows in how Triglav, the highest mountain, appears as a quiet, elevated identity symbol even on the national flag—collective greatness, but quiet and restrained, not loudly displayed. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this consistently reserved Slovenian basic stance." },
    { name: "Bulgaria", iso: "BG", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Bulgarian head-shaking traditionally means \"yes,\" while nodding means \"no\"—a detail confusing to outsiders that makes a deeply rooted cultural independence visible. The Rose Valley around Kazanlak, a center of rose oil production for centuries, stands for a form of economy based on protected knowledge passed down through generations. Many children grow up with grandparents while parents work abroad out of economic necessity—a family safety net that must function across international borders. The communist past, featuring one of the most loyal Soviet satellite states, left behind a still-perceptible mistrust toward quick political promises. Situated between shifting major powers—Ottoman Empire, Russia, EU—a cautious, watchful fundamental attitude has imprinted itself as a survival strategy.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Bulgaria shows up mainly as a self-preservation-toned Six: many children grow up with their grandparents while parents work abroad out of economic necessity—a family safety net that must function across borders. The generations-old, protected knowledge of rose-oil production around Kazanlak shows the same logic: security through preserved, family-transmitted skill. A social undertone shows in the collective distrust of quick political promises rooted in the communist past, and in the cautious, wait-and-see basic stance toward shifting great powers. Sexual Six, with its bold confrontation, clearly recedes behind this family- and collectively-anchored caution." },
    { name: "Cyprus", iso: "CY", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Nicosia remains the last divided capital in Europe—a \"Green Line\" with a UN buffer zone runs right through the center of the city, a daily, visible reality since the 1974 division. Halloumi cheese, traditionally produced across the island, became a political issue itself when the EU protected its designation of origin—a symbol of how deeply division and identity intertwine. Older generations still talk of lost houses and villages on the other side of the island—a collective trauma passed down through generations. Positioned between the Ottoman Empire, British colonial rule, and the still-unresolved division, the island has rarely experienced stable, long-term security. Family cohesion and caution toward political promises are direct consequences of this repeated historical experience of threat.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Cyprus shows up mainly as a socially toned Six: the divided capital Nicosia with its \"Green Line\" is daily, collectively shared reality for the whole society, and the EU's protection of halloumi cheese shows how deeply division and national identity are intertwined. Between Ottoman rule, British colonial rule, and unresolved division, the island as a whole has rarely experienced stable security. A self-preservation undertone shows in how older generations still personally recount lost homes—a family-transmitted trauma—and in family cohesion as a direct, private answer to repeated threat. Sexual Six recedes behind this collective and family-level vigilance." },
    { name: "Slovakia", iso: "SK", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Traditional costumes and village festivals, such as the folklore festivals in Východná, are maintained with great seriousness—giving rural communities a sense of continuity and cohesion. The pastoral culture of the \"bača\" in the Tatra Mountains, complete with its own cheese-making and self-sufficiency, symbolizes a tradition of relying on oneself apart from state security. Following the separation from Czechia in 1993, the country had to reposition itself as an independent state—an experience favoring a cautious, step-by-step approach toward major changes. Family remains the central social safety net in rural regions, often more important than state institutions. Skepticism toward rapid political or economic upheavals remains clearly visible in surveys and voting behavior to the present day.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Slovakia shows up mainly as a self-preservation-toned Six: the shepherd culture of the \"bača\" in the Tatra mountains, with its own cheesemaking and self-sufficiency, symbolizes a tradition of fending for oneself outside state protection. In rural regions, family remains the central, personal social safety net, often more important than state institutions. A social undertone shows in the earnestly maintained village festivals and folk costumes, which give rural communities a collective sense of continuity, and in the shared skepticism toward rapid change that grew after the 1993 split from Czechoslovakia. Sexual Six recedes behind this family- and village-anchored caution." },
    { name: "Estonia", iso: "EE", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Estonia was the first country in the world to introduce digital \"e-Residency\"—one can run a company here without being physically present. This preference for distant interaction over personal proximity runs through all of daily life. The \"Laulupidu\" song festival, gathering over one hundred thousand people every five years for joint singing, is one of the rare moments of collective emotional outburst in an otherwise reserved culture. The sauna, mostly used alone or with immediate family, serves quiet cleansing rather than social conversation. With only 1.3 million inhabitants across a large area, physical distance between people in everyday life is taken for granted. Reticence is not considered unfriendly here, but a sign of sincerity—one speaks only what is truly meant.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Estonia shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Five: digital \"e-Residency,\" which makes physical presence unnecessary, and the sauna, mostly used alone or with the closest family for quiet cleansing, show a consistent preference for distance over sociable closeness. With only 1.3 million people across a large area, physical distance between people is everyday reality, and taciturnity is seen as a sign of sincerity, not unfriendliness. A social undertone shows in the \"Laulupidu\" song festival, where over a hundred thousand people gather to sing together every five years—one of the rare moments of collective emotional release in an otherwise reserved culture. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this distanced Estonian basic stance." },
    { name: "Latvia", iso: "LV", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "As in Estonia, the Song and Dance Festival held every five years in Riga is one of the few moments in which the otherwise reserved Latvian public shows itself collectively and loudly. Amber crafting, a traditional artisan trade along the Baltic coast, is quiet, meditative solo work passed down through generations. Frequent foreign rule—Swedish, Russian, German, Soviet—left behind a cautious, observant fundamental attitude toward those in power. The forests covering a large part of the country are traditionally valued as places of retreat where one can find peace undisturbed. Personal distance in daily life, even among acquaintances, is understood here as natural respect rather than coldness.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Latvia likewise shows a predominantly self-preservation-toned Five: amber crafting as quiet, meditative solo work, and forests valued as an undisturbed retreat, shape daily life alongside the frequent foreign rule that has left a cautious, watchful basic stance. Personal distance, even between acquaintances, is understood here as ordinary respect, not coldness. A social undertone shows, as in Estonia, in the song and dance festival held every five years in Riga—one of the few moments when the otherwise reserved Latvian public shows itself collectively and loudly. Sexual Five clearly recedes behind this watchful, distanced basic stance." },
    { name: "Lithuania", iso: "LT", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The 1989 \"Baltic Way,\" a 600-kilometer-long human chain through Lithuania, Latvia, and Estonia against Soviet occupation, is one of the most striking symbols of collective resistance against threat in European history. Basketball is a national sport and almost a substitute religion—the national team's success is seen as an expression of collective strength for a small, often threatened country. The partisan movement of the Forest Brothers, fighting Soviet occupation into the 1950s, is deeply anchored in the national memory. Family and village communities were historically considered the most reliable protection against recurring foreign rule—a pattern still echoing in strong family ties today. The Hill of Crosses shrine near Šiauliai, with hundreds of thousands of erected crosses despite Soviet destruction attempts, demonstrates a tenacious, quiet will to resist.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Lithuania shows up mainly as a socially toned Six: the 1989 \"Baltic Way,\" a 600-kilometer human chain against Soviet occupation, and the near-religious status of basketball as an expression of the collective strength of a small, often threatened country, show security as a jointly won, shared experience. A clear sexual undertone shows in the Forest Brothers partisan movement, which fought armed against Soviet occupation into the 1950s—not a wait-and-see but an actively combative, counterphobic response to threat. The Hill of Crosses, repeatedly restocked despite Soviet attempts to destroy it, likewise shows this tenacious, defiant will to resist. Self-preservation Six, which would withdraw more into the family, recedes behind this collectively combative stance." },
    { name: "Luxembourg", iso: "LU", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "As one of the most significant financial centers in Europe, Luxembourg thrives on precision, discretion, and regulatory reliability—errors in banking are existentially threatening to the entire country. Multilingualism—Luxembourgish, French, and German used side-by-side in daily life and administration—requires a high degree of structured order for communication to run smoothly. As the seat of several key EU institutions, such as the European Court of Justice, the country relies on permanent bureaucratic exactness. Despite its small size, Luxembourg boasts one of the highest per capita incomes in the world—a success based on stable, dependably long-term economic policy, not spectacular bets. Tidy, clean public spaces and high administrative efficiency shape daily life.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Luxembourg shows a clearly social One: as a major financial center, the country lives on precision, discretion, and regulatory reliability that must apply equally to all actors, and as host to several EU institutions it depends on permanent, collectively binding bureaucratic accuracy. The trilingualism lived daily requires a high degree of structured order that applies to everyone, so communication runs smoothly. A self-preservation undertone shows in the high per-capita income, built on stable, long-term reliable—rather than spectacular—personal and economic caution. Sexual One, which would carry its principles outward with missionary intensity, recedes behind this institutional, collectively coordinated love of order." },
    { name: "Belgium", iso: "BE", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "As the seat of the EU Commission and numerous international institutions, Brussels has consciously positioned itself as a political power center of Europe—a status actively cultivated and marketed. Belgian chocolate and Belgian beer are internationally protected marks of quality and export hits through which the small country becomes globally visible. The comic \"Tintin\" and the Belgian comic tradition as a whole have been turned into an internationally recognized cultural flagship. The historic ports of Antwerp and Zeebrugge turn Belgium into one of the most important trade hubs in Europe—economic activity as a national self-understanding, similar to the Ruhr area or North Rhine-Westphalia. The tension between economically strong, entrepreneurial Flanders and industrially shaped Wallonia shows how much economic status takes center stage both regionally and nationally.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Belgium shows a very clearly social Three: Brussels was deliberately positioned and actively marketed as Europe's political power center, Belgian chocolate and beer are internationally protected, deliberately exported hallmarks, and the comic \"Tintin\" was made into an internationally recognized cultural showcase—success here is consistently made visible to the outside world. The storied ports of Antwerp and Zeebrugge reinforce this picture of economic busyness as national self-image. A self-preservation undertone shows in the practical, unspectacular reliability of these trading hubs. Both self-preservation and sexual Three clearly recede behind this consistently, publicly staged, collective status-consciousness." },
    { name: "Vatican City", iso: "VA", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "As the smallest sovereign state in the world, the Vatican nonetheless claims moral authority over more than 1.3 billion Catholics worldwide — order and doctrine as its central instrument of power. Canon law (Codex Iuris Canonici), codified and refined over centuries, regulates doctrine, ritual, and hierarchy down to the smallest detail. Strict, unchanging liturgy and millennia-old ceremony run through daily life down to the smallest gesture. Historically, the Church saw itself as guardian of a single valid moral and doctrinal order, from the Inquisition to today's encyclicals. At the same time, the Vatican visibly wrestles internally with the tension between unchanging tradition and necessary reform — an inner judge that is never quite satisfied with itself.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "The Vatican shows a pronouncedly social One: its claimed moral authority over more than 1.3 billion Catholics worldwide, canon law codified over centuries, and an unchanging, millennia-old liturgy are order and doctrine as a central, collectively binding instrument of power. Historically, the Church saw itself as guardian of a single valid moral order that applied to everyone. A self-preservation undertone shows in the institution's internal struggle with itself, between unchanging tradition and necessary reform—an inner judge that is never quite satisfied with itself, a very personal, inward-facing facet of One's passion. Sexual One clearly recedes behind this institutional, collectively binding power of order." },
    { name: "Malta", iso: "MT", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "The Order of Malta, originally founded as the \"Knights Hospitaller of Saint John,\" devoted itself for centuries to caring for sick and poor pilgrims — care as the founding myth of statehood. The small island historically served as a refuge and crossroads between the cultures of the Mediterranean, open to trade, religious refugees, and travelers passing through. Famous Maltese hospitality, often described as \"insistently warm,\" still shows itself today in the dense web of mutual neighborly help in the villages. As an EU member, Malta disproportionately often takes on mediating and humanitarian roles, such as in Mediterranean sea rescue. Deeply rooted Catholic devotion, with over 350 churches on a tiny island, combines with an everyday life in which family and mutual care hold top priority.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Malta shows a clearly social Two: the Order of Malta devoted itself institutionally for centuries to caring for sick and poor pilgrims—care as the nation's founding myth—and as an EU member, Malta still disproportionately takes on humanitarian tasks such as Mediterranean sea rescue. Malta's famous, \"pushily warm\" hospitality shows itself in the dense network of mutual neighborly help in its villages. A self-preservation undertone shows in deeply rooted Catholic devotion and in family and mutual care holding top priority in private daily life. Sexual Two, with its exclusive devotion to a single person, recedes behind this broad culture of care lived both institutionally and among neighbors." },
    { name: "Belarus", iso: "BY", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Under Alexander Lukashenko, in power since 1994, Belarus has become one of the most tightly surveilled states in Europe — control as an answer to a deep fear of instability. Following the mass protests of 2020, an already dense security apparatus was expanded further, with thousands of arrests and some of the most repressive press laws on the continent. The historical experience of being a \"transit land\" between Russia and Western Europe — devastated in World War II — shapes a deep collective need for security. The close, near-total alignment with Russia is explicitly justified by the leadership as a protective alliance against Western influence. Suspicion toward independent institutions, a free press, and civic self-organization runs through the entire public sphere even today.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Belarus shows a pronouncedly social Six: the dense state surveillance apparatus, expanded after the 2020 mass protests, and the close alignment with Russia as an explicitly justified protective alliance, show safety as something produced from above, collectively and institutionally. The historical experience as a \"transit land\" between Russia and Western Europe has likewise shaped a deeply collective, shared need for security. A self-preservation undertone shows in the everyday, very personal distrust of independent institutions and free press that runs through the entire public sphere—caution even in the small, private circle. Sexual Six, with its bold, open confrontation of threat, clearly recedes behind this mainly institutional, collectively organized control." },
    { name: "Albania", iso: "AL", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Under the paranoid dictator Enver Hoxha, Albania built over 700,000 bunkers across the country between 1967 and 1986 — out of a deep, never-realized fear of invasion by either NATO or the Warsaw Pact. These bunkers, often standing amid neighborhoods, fields, and beaches, still visibly shape the Albanian landscape today as stone monuments to collective vigilance. The country's decades-long, near-total self-isolation from the outside world — more radical than in most other communist states — reveals extreme mistrust of outside influence. After communism collapsed in 1991, the country plunged into a pyramid-scheme financial disaster in 1997 that reinforced deep distrust of institutions all over again. Today's vigorous push for EU and NATO membership is explicitly framed as a need for lasting, dependable security after decades of isolation and mistrust.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Albania shows a pronouncedly social Six: the more than 700,000 bunkers built across the country under Enver Hoxha, and decades of radical self-isolation from the outside world, are collective, state-mandated vigilance in its clearest expression—security was elevated here into national doctrine. Today's energetic desire for EU and NATO membership is explicitly framed as a need for reliable, institutionally secured safety. A self-preservation undertone shows in the 1997 pyramid-scheme financial disaster, which cost millions of Albanians their personal savings and deepened a very concrete, individual distrust of institutions. Sexual Six clearly recedes behind this mainly collective, state-shaped vigilance." },
    { name: "North Macedonia", iso: "MK", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The decades-long name dispute with Greece — North Macedonia was officially unable to call itself \"Macedonia\" until 2019 — was ultimately resolved through a painstakingly negotiated compromise, not confrontation. Deeply divided ethnically between Slavic Macedonians and a large Albanian minority, the country avoided escalation after the 2001 armed conflict through the Ohrid Framework Agreement — power-sharing instead of civil war. The deliberate renaming to \"North Macedonia\" itself was a painful but pragmatic compromise made to finally open the door to EU and NATO membership. In the capital Skopje, different ethnic and religious groups live in dense proximity, often governed by tacitly established rules of coexistence rather than open confrontation. The small country deliberately navigates between eastern and western spheres of influence without clearly taking sides — balance as a foreign-policy principle in itself.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "North Macedonia shows a clearly socially toned Nine: the decades-long name dispute with Greece was resolved through a painstakingly negotiated compromise rather than confrontation, and the Ohrid Framework Agreement prevented escalation after 2001 through power-sharing rather than civil war—harmony here is deliberately produced institutionally. The country's foreign-policy balancing act between eastern and western spheres of influence likewise shows equilibrium as a conscious, collective principle. A self-preservation undertone shows in everyday life in the capital, Skopje, where different ethnic and religious groups live together according to tacit, personal rules of coexistence. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this mainly negotiated, institutional search for harmony." },
    { name: "Montenegro", iso: "ME", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The smallest country of former Yugoslavia peacefully separated from Serbia in 2006 through an overwhelmingly narrow referendum — separation through democratic consensus rather than force of arms. Montenegro traditionally maintains a remarkably relaxed, often almost casual attitude toward political tensions that have shaken other Balkan states far more severely. The Orthodox Church is deeply rooted in daily life without adopting the conflict-laden tone of religious disputes in other neighboring countries. As NATO's smallest member (since 2017), the country deliberately navigates between Russian cultural ties and Western integration without clearly picking a side. The relaxed Mediterranean coastal culture around the Bay of Kotor stands in striking contrast to the turbulent Balkan history surrounding it — a country that would rather sidestep the storm than seek it out.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Montenegro shows a more self-preservation-toned Nine: the traditionally relaxed, almost casual attitude toward political tension, and the easygoing Mediterranean coastal culture around the Bay of Kotor, show a personal, everyday kind of ease—a country that would rather sidestep the storm than seek it out. A social undertone shows in the peaceful 2006 separation from Serbia through a democratic referendum rather than force of arms, and in the deliberate foreign-policy balance between Russian cultural ties and Western NATO integration. Sexual Nine barely surfaces against this consistently relaxed, avoidant basic stance." },
    { name: "Bosnia and Herzegovina", iso: "BA", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Bosnian War (1992–1995), with the Srebrenica genocide as its deepest trauma, left behind a country still split into three ethno-religious blocs (Bosniaks, Serbs, Croats) to this day. The Dayton Peace Agreement created a highly complex, deliberately distrustful power-sharing structure with a rotating presidency, designed so that no single group could ever gain sole control. Checkpoints, divided school systems, and separated residential areas reveal a deeply entrenched need for protection against renewed escalation that persists to this day. International military presence (first NATO, then EU forces) remained in place for decades after the war ended — security from outside, because internal trust is lacking. Despite formal peace, deep collective vigilance toward the other ethnic groups remains, still shaping political decisions today.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Bosnia and Herzegovina shows a very pronouncedly social Six: the Dayton Peace Agreement created a highly complex, deliberately distrustful power-sharing structure with a rotating presidency so that no single group can ever gain sole control—institutionalized distrust as a principle of statehood. Checkpoints, divided school systems, and the international military presence that persisted for decades show safety as something that must be secured collectively and from outside. A self-preservation undertone shows in the separated residential areas, where families practice caution toward the other ethnic group in their immediate, personal everyday surroundings. Sexual Six clearly recedes behind this deeply institutionalized, collective vigilance." },
    { name: "Andorra", iso: "AD", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "As one of the last European microstates with a co-principality lasting until 1993 (shared rule by the French head of state and the Bishop of Urgell), Andorra has always preserved its sovereignty through skillful positioning between two great neighbors rather than confrontation. The country traditionally maintains no army of its own, relying instead on treaty-secured protection guarantees from France and Spain. Andorra's prosperity as a banking hub and tax haven rests on decades of carefully maintained discretion and regulatory compliance toward international oversight bodies. Its secluded location in the Pyrenees was historically used deliberately as protection from larger conflicts. Even the relatively late, cautious democratic opening (first constitution only in 1993) reflects a fundamentally deliberative, security-minded state culture.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Andorra shows a clearly social Six: the country maintains no army of its own and instead relies on treaty-secured protection guarantees from France and Spain—safety is deliberately produced institutionally and through alliance, not through its own strength. Andorra's prosperity as a banking center rests on decades of carefully maintained regulatory conformity toward international oversight bodies, and even the cautious democratic opening, only completed in 1993, shows a fundamentally deliberative state culture. A self-preservation undertone shows in the deliberately used, secluded location in the Pyrenees, historically serving as geographic protection from larger conflicts. Sexual Six clearly recedes behind this treaty- and rule-based security culture." },
    { name: "San Marino", iso: "SM", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "As the oldest surviving republic in the world (founded in 301 AD), San Marino owes its survival over 1,700 years to a strategy of steady neutrality and cautious alliance politics rather than military strength. Its location on a hard-to-access mountain (Monte Titano) served from the outset to protect it from larger powers. The country preserved its independence even during the Napoleonic wars and Italy's wars of unification through skillful diplomatic restraint. The traditionally rotating dual leadership, changing every six months (Capitani Reggenti), deliberately prevents the concentration of power in a single person — a structural distrust of one-man rule. San Marino's economy, heavily oriented toward tourism and financial services, is traditionally managed cautiously, favoring stability over risk.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "San Marino shows a pronouncedly social Six: the world's oldest republic owes its 1,700-year survival to a strategy of consistent neutrality and cautious alliance-building rather than military strength—and its dual leadership, rotating every six months, deliberately prevents power from ever concentrating in a single person, a structural, institutionalized distrust of one-man rule. A self-preservation undertone shows in the cautious, stability-over-risk management of the economy. Sexual Six, with its bold confrontation, clearly recedes behind this centuries-proven, institutionally anchored caution." },
    { name: "Monaco", iso: "MC", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "The tiny principality of Monaco has deliberately positioned itself as the epitome of glamour, wealth, and exclusive success — from the Formula 1 racetrack to the Monte Carlo Casino to the nearby annual red carpet of the film festival. With one of the highest per-capita incomes and the highest density of millionaires in the world, visible material success is cultivated as a central national image. The Grimaldi princely family has deliberately staged itself in the media for decades, from Princess Grace (Grace Kelly) to lavish weddings — success and status as a publicly visible family enterprise. Tax-free income deliberately attracts an international elite that uses Monaco as a stage for economic success. Monaco's harbor, lined with superyachts, explicitly serves to showcase wealth and status.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Monaco shows a very clearly social Three: the principality has deliberately positioned itself as the epitome of glamour and exclusive success—from Formula 1 to the casino to the nearby film festival—and visible material success is cultivated on purpose as national image. The Grimaldi princely family has staged itself media-savvily for decades as a publicly visible family enterprise of success and status. A sexual undertone shows in the deliberate display of allure and glamour, such as the harbor lined with superyachts or the iconic love story of Princess Gracia Patricia (Grace Kelly)—seduction as part of the public image. Self-preservation Three clearly recedes behind this consistently outward-facing consciousness of success and status." },
    { name: "Liechtenstein", iso: "LI", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "The principality of Liechtenstein is considered one of the most strictly regulated and simultaneously wealthiest countries in the world — economic success is achieved explicitly through meticulous rule-following rather than risk-taking. As the seat of numerous trusts and foundations, the country has deliberately cultivated its reputation for strict, correct administration, consistently improving it through comprehensive regulatory reforms since the 2000s after earlier international criticism of insufficient transparency. Its industry, above all precision engineering and dental technology, reflects a cultural inclination toward accuracy and flawless execution. The country has maintained no army of its own since 1868, consistently regulating interstate relations through treaties and orderly systems rather than confrontation. The orderly, almost seamless social safety net and low crime rate are understood by Liechtensteiners themselves as an expression of their disciplined state culture.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Liechtenstein shows a clearly social One: as one of the most strictly regulated countries in the world, economic success here is explicitly achieved through meticulous, universally applied rule-adherence rather than risk-taking, and the country has consistently managed interstate relations through treaties rather than confrontation since 1868. The orderly, almost seamless social safety net is likewise understood by Liechtensteiners themselves as an expression of a disciplined, collective state culture. A self-preservation undertone shows in the precision-engineering industry, which reflects a very personal, craftsmanlike inclination toward accuracy and flawless execution. Sexual One clearly recedes behind this institutional, collectively coordinated love of order." },
    { name: "Moldova", iso: "MD", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Moldova, wedged between Romania and Ukraine, has struggled since independence in 1991 to forge a distinct national identity that neither fully merges with Romania nor detaches from its Soviet past. The breakaway region of Transnistria, de facto independent since a 1992 war and backed by Russian troops, reinforces the feeling of a country wrestling with its own unresolved identity. As one of the poorest countries in Europe, Moldova carries a deep awareness of economic and cultural marginality, compounded by massive emigration of young people. The language question — Romanian or \"Moldovan\"? — was politically and identity-charged for decades without final resolution. The winemaking tradition and the unique underground wine city of Cricova are deliberately cultivated as a distinctive cultural hallmark setting the country apart from its larger neighbors.", subtyp: "se4", subtypText: "Moldova shows a more self-preservation-toned Four: as one of the poorest countries in Europe, it carries a deep, very personal awareness of economic and cultural marginality, reinforced by massive emigration of young people—a quiet struggle for its own survival and its own identity, wedged between larger neighbors. A social undertone shows in the deliberately cultivated winemaking tradition and the unique underground wine city of Cricova, positioned specifically as an unmistakable cultural distinguishing feature against Romania and Ukraine, as well as in the decades-long, politically charged language question. Sexual Four recedes behind this quiet, more inward-facing struggle for identity." },
    { name: "Kosovo", iso: "XK", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Kosovo, unilaterally declared independent from Serbia in 2008 but even now recognized by only about half of UN member states, carries a deep national identity substantially shaped by the experience of international non-recognition and historical persecution. The Kosovo War (1998–1999), with ethnic cleansing of the ethnic Albanian population majority and the subsequent NATO intervention, remains the central, generation-shaping trauma of the national narrative. Ongoing non-recognition by Serbia, Russia, China, and several EU states reinforces a collective sense of internationally incomplete, constantly-to-be-defended independence. The centuries-old myth of the Battle of Kosovo (1389), interpreted differently by Serbs and Albanians alike, exemplifies the identity-forming dispute over the same territory and the same history. The predominantly young, Albanian-speaking population maintains a pronounced awareness of cultural and political distinctiveness toward both Serbia and its larger Albanian neighbor.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Kosovo shows a pronouncedly socially toned Four: its national identity is significantly shaped by the experience of international non-recognition—to the present day only around half of UN member states recognize the country—and the centuries-old Battle of Kosovo myth is interpreted differently by Serbs and Albanians alike: a publicly contested struggle over identity and territory. The Kosovo War remains the central, generation-defining collective trauma of the national narrative. A sexual undertone shows in the predominantly young, Albanian-speaking population, which defends its cultural and political distinctiveness from both Serbia and its Albanian neighbor with palpable, passionate energy. Self-preservation Four recedes behind this publicly asserted, collective search for identity." },
    { name: "Greenland", iso: "GL", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Greenland, with around 56,000 inhabitants on the world's largest island, lives in a vastness and cold that makes withdrawal and quiet self-sufficiency almost a necessity rather than a choice. Denmark granted extensive autonomy in 2009, yet actual independence is being prepared by Greenlanders gradually, observantly, and on a scientifically grounded basis rather than declared abruptly — climate research and resource assessments shape political discourse more than grand speeches. Traditional Inuit culture, with its enormous practical knowledge of ice, weather, and hunting, was passed down orally over generations and is regarded as an independent body of knowledge alongside Western science. International climate researchers view Greenland as a key open-air laboratory for understanding global climate change, giving the country a peculiar role as both observed and self-observing knowledge center. The extreme darkness of the winter months favors an introverted culture of reading, reflection, and quiet contemplation.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Greenland shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Five: the enormous vastness and cold make retreat and quiet self-sufficiency almost a necessity rather than a choice, and the extreme darkness of the winter months favors an introverted, inward-turned culture of reading and quiet reflection. A social undertone shows in the step-by-step, scientifically grounded preparation for independence—climate research and resource assessments shape political discourse more than grand speeches—as well as in the traditional Inuit knowledge tradition, passed down orally across generations as shared heritage. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this consistently reserved, observant Greenlandic basic stance." },
    { name: "Faroe Islands", iso: "FO", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "The Faroe Islands, an autonomous part of the Kingdom of Denmark with its own parliament (Løgting) since 1948, regulate almost all internal affairs — except foreign and defense policy — according to a precisely balanced, clearly structured model of self-government. Traditional whaling (\"grindadráp\") is subject to strict, centuries-old rules governing procedure, participation, and fair distribution of the meat to the entire community — order and fairness are central to this contested practice. Sheep farming, so central that the country's name literally means \"sheep islands,\" still follows precisely regulated grazing rights established between families over generations. The extremely precise scheduling of ferries and helicopters between the 18 islands, often the only reliable connection in rough North Atlantic weather, requires highly disciplined, error-free logistics. The small, homogeneous population of around 54,000 maintains a strong awareness of rule-following and mutual reliability as the basis for living together in a confined space.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "The Faroe Islands show a very pronouncedly social One: the precisely balanced self-governance model, the strictly regulated, fair distribution of the whale hunt to the entire community, and the highly disciplined ferry logistics between the 18 islands show order and fairness as a collectively binding principle. The small, homogeneous population maintains a strong, shared consciousness of rule-adherence and mutual reliability as the basis of living together. A self-preservation undertone shows in the precisely regulated grazing rights of sheep farming, fixed between individual families across generations—personal, family-transmitted order. Sexual One clearly recedes behind this consistently collective-institutional love of order." },
  ]},
  { region: "North America", laender: [
    { name: "USA", iso: "US", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "The \"American Dream\" is not an empty phrase, but a lived promise: From rags to riches—self-made success stories like those of Oprah Winfrey or Steve Jobs are elevated to national legends. Job interviews, elevator pitches, LinkedIn profiles—the ability to present oneself and one's achievements convincingly is trained and rewarded from an early age. University prestige (Ivy League), house size, cars, and job titles function as visible status symbols by which success is immediately read. Motivational literature and coaching are a billion-dollar industry—the conviction that anyone can make it \"to the top\" with enough effort is deeply anchored. Even failure is culturally reinterpreted: Silicon Valley's \"fail fast\" mindset does not demand retreat after a setback, but an immediate new visible attempt.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "The United States shows a very pronouncedly social Three: the \"American Dream\" as a lived, collectively shared promise, status symbols like university prestige, house size, and job titles, and the billion-dollar motivation and coaching industry all present success as a publicly visible, socially anchored ideal. Silicon Valley's \"fail fast\" mindset—treating failure not as retreat but as an immediate, visible new attempt—is likewise a collectively shared success narrative. A sexual undertone shows in the personal self-presentation skills trained from a young age—the elevator pitch, the job interview, the LinkedIn profile—where the individual person becomes their own brand. Self-preservation Three, which would show up as quiet, functional work, recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated drive for success." },
    { name: "Canada", iso: "CA", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Saying \"sorry,\" even when bumped into oneself, is considered quintessential Canadian—a reflexive stepping back from one's own position to maintain distance rather than risk conflict. Across one of the most sparsely populated landmasses in the world—over nine million square kilometers for around 40 million people—physical distance between human beings is everyday reality, not an exception. Internationally, Canada consciously positions itself as a mediator and UN peacekeeping nation, observing and moderating rather than becoming entangled in conflict itself. Its own identity is often defined through demarcation from its loud southern neighbor, the USA, rather than through loud self-assertion of its own—restraint as a conscious national trademark. The vastness of the Canadian wilderness, from the Rocky Mountains to the Arctic, is culturally idealized as a place of quiet contemplation rather than conquest.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Canada shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Five: the reflexive \"sorry,\" even when one has just been bumped into, and the Canadian wilderness, culturally romanticized as a place of quiet contemplation rather than conquest, show a consistent need to maintain personal distance rather than risk conflict. Across one of the most sparsely populated areas on earth, physical distance between people is everyday, individually lived reality. A social undertone shows in the country's deliberate international positioning as a mediator and UN peacekeeping nation, and in a national identity defined more by contrast with its loud southern neighbor than by loud self-assertion of its own—restraint as a collective brand. Sexual Five barely surfaces against this consistently reserved Canadian basic stance." },
    { name: "Mexico", iso: "MX", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "\"No pasa nada\"—\"it's fine\"—is one of the most frequently used phrases in Mexican daily life, a formula with which tensions and conflicts are routinely downplayed. Día de los Muertos, the Day of the Dead, turns one of the biggest taboos—death—into a colorful, conciliatory family celebration instead of grief and fear. Despite drug wars, poverty, and political corruption, daily life for many Mexicans remains astonishingly unagitated—resilience shows itself here as composure, not as a fighting spirit. The Siesta culture and a relaxed handling of schedules (\"la hora mexicana\") express a priority for ease over efficiency. Family gatherings, often with dozens of relatives over many hours, serve less as active caretaking for one another than as simply dwelling and being together—connection as a state, not an action.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Mexico shows a clearly socially toned Nine: \"no pasa nada\"—one of the most commonly used phrases—routinely downplays tension, and Día de los Muertos turns death into a colorful, reconciling family festival instead of grief, showing harmony as an actively maintained, communal principle. Hours-long family gatherings with dozens of relatives, where simply being together matters more than actively caring for one another, likewise show connection as a shared state rather than an action. A self-preservation undertone shows in siesta culture and the relaxed \"hora mexicana\"—personal ease placed above efficiency. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently communal, easygoing way of life." },
    { name: "Bermuda", iso: "BM", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Bermuda, a British overseas territory in the North Atlantic, has become one of the wealthiest territories in the world despite its tiny size and lack of significant natural resources — chiefly through aggressive positioning as a global reinsurance and offshore finance center. The island deliberately advertises low tax rates and business-friendly regulation to attract international corporations and insurers, achieving a per-capita income that surpasses many sovereign industrialized nations. The pastel-colored houses, built to strict architectural code, and the immaculately maintained golf courses are deliberately presented as showcases of cultivated prosperity, not merely tourist scenery. The annual Bermuda Sailing Week and hosting the 2017 America's Cup were deliberately used to position the island internationally as an exclusive, successful location. Despite its status as a non-sovereign territory, Bermuda confidently presents itself as an independent actor on economic matters in international forums, with its own currency and its own presence at global economic conferences.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Bermuda shows a very clearly social Three: its aggressive positioning as a global reinsurance and offshore financial center, the pastel-colored, strictly regulated houses serving as a showcase of cultivated prosperity, and its deliberately cultivated presence on the international economic stage all present success as a consciously outward-staged, collective image. A sexual undertone shows in the use of exclusive, prestigious events like Bermuda Sailing Week and the 2017 America's Cup, through which the island deliberately generates admiration and international allure. Self-preservation Three, which would show up as quiet, practical reliability, recedes behind this consistently outward-facing image of success." },
  ]},
  { region: "Central America & Caribbean", laender: [
    { name: "Panama", iso: "PA", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Panama hats are traditionally made not in Panama, but in Ecuador—a small curiosity that nevertheless shows how much the country defines its identity through hospitality rather than its own product exports. The Panama Canal, as an international trade connection between oceans, shapes a self-understanding as a host and mediator between worlds. In direct personal experience—as Detlef himself reported—one encounters a remarkably warm, caring everyday culture that actively involves visitors rather than merely tolerating them. Family celebrations and neighborhood festivals are an integral part of the weekly rhythm, with strangers invited as a matter of course. The cuisine, characterized by \"Sancocho,\" a stew cooked together for many, symbolizes care as a shared, practical act.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Panama shows a clearly socially toned Two: the Panama Canal, as an international trade link, shapes a self-understanding as host and mediator between worlds, and a strikingly warm, caring everyday culture actively includes visitors rather than merely tolerating them—care as a communal, outward-facing principle. The cuisine, centered on \"sancocho,\" a stew cooked together for many, symbolizes care as a shared, practical act. A self-preservation undertone shows in family celebrations and neighborhood festivals firmly anchored in the weekly rhythm, where strangers are invited as a matter of course. Sexual Two, with its exclusive devotion to a single person, recedes behind this broad, hospitable culture of care." },
    { name: "Cuba", iso: "CU", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Despite decades of US embargo and a chronic shortage of basic goods, Cuban streets are filled with music—Son, Salsa, Rumba, played live on almost every corner of Havana. Old American classic cars from the 1950s are kept alive through improvisational skill—creativity in scarcity instead of resignation. The \"Casa de la Trova\" music venues are institutions where multiple generations dance together, not separated by age. Baseball enjoys cult status, with street games practically wherever there is space. Despite political isolation, Cuba exports doctors and dancers worldwide—a culture that views its joy of life and skill as its true wealth, if not material prosperity.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Cuba shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: despite decades of embargo and chronic shortages, the streets are filled with live music, the \"Casa de la Trova\" music houses bring several generations together to dance, and Cuba exports doctors and dancers around the world—zest for life here is consistently understood as a shared, public good, not a private pleasure. Baseball's cult status and its ubiquitous street games likewise show joy as a collectively lived practice. A self-preservation undertone shows in the resourceful upkeep of old 1950s American cars through improvisation—practical ingenuity in scarcity rather than resignation. Sexual Seven recedes behind this consistently communal zest for life." },
    { name: "Costa Rica", iso: "CR", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "In 1948, Costa Rica became one of the few countries in the world to abolish its military completely—a unique, constitutional renunciation of organized violence that remains in force even today. \"Pura Vida,\" literally \"pure life,\" is a greeting, life motto, and advertising slogan all at once—a deeply rooted attitude of relaxed contentment. The country invests an above-average amount in environmental protection and national parks; over a quarter of its land area is protected, representing a conscious, undramatic coexistence with nature rather than exploitation. Political stability and democracy for decades, in a region full of crises, demonstrate an inclination toward compromise over upheaval. Education and healthcare are traditionally prioritized highly—a quiet, unagitated path to prosperity instead of loud economic competition.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Costa Rica shows a pronouncedly socially toned Nine: the complete, constitutionally anchored abolition of the military in 1948, above-average environmental protection with over a quarter of the country's land under protection, and decades of stable democracy in a crisis-prone region all show peace and balance as a deliberately institutionalized, collective principle. High priority for education and healthcare likewise follows a quiet, unruffled path to prosperity rather than loud economic competition. A self-preservation undertone shows in \"Pura Vida\"—literally \"pure life\"—a deeply rooted, very personal attitude of relaxed contentment that runs through daily life as a greeting. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently balance- and coexistence-oriented basic stance." },
    { name: "Dominican Republic", iso: "DO", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Merengue and Bachata are not just music styles, but daily companions—in Colmados (small neighborhood shops), people dance to loud music while shopping on the side. Baseball is a national sport with religious significance, and talent is nurtured from a young age—the dream of success in the US Major League drives entire communities. Despite economic challenges, Dominican street culture is loud, colorful, and festive, with Carnival celebrations prepared months in advance. The national drink, rum, and the social drinking culture under the open sky underline a collective preference for shared, exuberant evenings. Even in times of economic hardship, daily life is shaped with striking joy of life and optimism rather than falling into retreat.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "The Dominican Republic shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: merengue and bachata as everyday companions, danced to loud music in neighborhood corner stores, and loud, colorful street culture with carnival celebrations prepared months in advance, show zest for life as a consistently shared, public celebration. The national drink, rum, and a sociable open-air drinking culture likewise underscore a collective preference for shared, exuberant evenings, even amid economic hardship. A sexual undertone shows in baseball, with its almost religious status, where the dream of personal success in the US Major Leagues drives entire communities with intense, idealizing energy. Self-preservation Seven clearly recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated zest for life." },
    { name: "Jamaica", iso: "JM", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Reggae and the Rastafari movement, world-famous through Bob Marley, combine spiritual depth with a carefree, freedom-loving attitude. \"No problem, mon\" is more than a tourist cliché—it describes a genuinely lived fundamental attitude of not taking difficulties dramatically. Despite its small size, Jamaica has produced an above-average number of world-class sprinters—Usain Bolt's casualness even in high-performance sports is almost symptomatic of the national self-presentation. Street parties, \"Dancehall\" parties, and sound system culture shape the nights in Kingston and beyond. Even economic difficulties and high crime rates in certain neighborhoods change little about the culturally prioritized attitude lived outwardly: Joy of life is actively defended, not sacrificed to reality.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Jamaica shows a clearly socially toned Seven: street festivals, dancehall parties, and sound system culture shape the nights in Kingston, and despite its small size, Jamaica has produced a disproportionate number of world-class sprinters—zest for life here is understood as a collectively shared, publicly celebrated, and actively defended good, even amid economic hardship. A sexual undertone shows in reggae and the Rastafari movement, which combine spiritual depth with a carefree, freedom-loving attitude toward life, as well as in the almost iconic personal casualness of exceptional athletes like Usain Bolt even at the highest level of competition. Self-preservation Seven recedes behind this consistently public, passionately lived zest for life." },
    { name: "Guatemala", iso: "GT", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "In the Maya-influenced highland villages around Chichicastenango, the communal \"Cofradía\" system—local religious brotherhoods supporting each other during festivals and times of need—remains an actively lived form of social security to this day. Hand-woven textiles, whose patterns vary from village to village, are passed down from mothers to daughters—a cross-generational craft of care. The weekly market is far more than trade: It is a social gathering place where news, worries, and help are exchanged. Despite poverty and political instability, the extended family remains the most reliable social safety net, often living under one roof across multiple generations. Hospitality toward strangers, even with very limited means, is seen as a matter-of-course duty, not an exception.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Guatemala shows a clearly socially toned Two: the communal \"cofradía\" system—local religious brotherhoods that support one another at festivals and in times of need—and the weekly market as a social meeting point for exchanging news, worries, and help, show care as an institutionalized, communal principle. Self-evident hospitality toward strangers, even with very limited means, is likewise considered a duty of the whole community. A self-preservation undertone shows in the extended family as the most reliable social safety net, often spanning several generations under one roof, and in handwoven textiles whose patterns are passed from mothers to daughters—a very personal, family-based craft of care. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad culture of care lived both communally and within the family." },
    { name: "Honduras", iso: "HN", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "As one of the countries with the highest crime rates in Latin America, Honduras has developed a deep familiarity with insecurity—neighborhood watches and family protection networks are practical daily responses. The extended family, often with three generations in the same household or neighborhood, serves as the primary social safety net, as state institutions are frequently experienced as unreliable. Migration to the USA, often at great personal risk, is supported and financed by entire families—a collective effort to achieve security for individuals. Religious communities, both Evangelical and Catholic, offer additional social support and guidance in uncertain times. Caution in public spaces, such as when going out after dark, is an everyday, necessary practice, not exaggerated anxiety.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Honduras shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Six: the extended family, often with three generations in the same household or neighborhood, serves as the primary, very personal social safety net, since state institutions are often experienced as unreliable, and neighborhood watches are practical, concrete everyday responses to insecurity. Migration to the US, often undertaken at great personal risk, is likewise jointly financed and carried by entire families—collective safety for the individual family member. A social undertone shows in religious communities, evangelical and Catholic alike, which offer additional institutional support and orientation. Sexual Six, with its bold confrontation of threat, clearly recedes behind this family- and neighborhood-anchored caution." },
    { name: "Puerto Rico", iso: "PR", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Reggaeton, today a global musical phenomenon, has its roots significantly in the streets of San Juan—music as a loud, physical expression of joy of life despite economic downturns and repeated hurricane disasters. After devastating Hurricane Maria in 2017, neighborhoods spontaneously organized street parties and communal cooking—celebrating as an active coping strategy, not as denial. Salsa dance nights in old town squares are weekly institutions open to young and old alike. The close, often complicated relationship with the USA as an unincorporated territory is processed by many Puerto Ricans with a mix of pride in their own culture and persistent optimism. Despite a heavy debt burden and emigration, the Caribbean island remains known for its unshakable festive mood.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Puerto Rico shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: reggaeton as a loud, physical expression of zest for life, the spontaneously organized street parties and communal cooking after Hurricane Maria as an active, shared coping strategy, and weekly salsa dance nights in old-town plazas all show joy consistently as a publicly celebrated, communal good. A self-preservation undertone shows in the persistent, very personal optimism with which many Puerto Ricans shape their daily lives despite heavy debt and emigration. Sexual Seven recedes behind this consistently communal festive spirit." },
    { name: "Trinidad and Tobago", iso: "TT", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "The Port of Spain Carnival is considered one of the most exuberant in the Caribbean—months of preparing costumes and steel pan orchestras culminate in days of street parties. The steel drum, crafted from old oil drums, is a genuine local invention and a matter of national pride—a musical instrument that created creativity out of scarcity. Calypso and Soca music traditionally serve political satire as well—social tensions are sung and danced about, not merely discussed. The multiethnic society, composed of African, Indian, and other roots, celebrates its diversity equally through shared, exuberant festivals like Diwali and Carnival. The informal, spontaneous approach to time and plans—\"Trini time\"—is culturally understood as an expression of joy of life rather than a problem.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Trinidad and Tobago shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: the Port of Spain Carnival with months of communal preparation, the multiethnic society that celebrates its diversity through shared festivals like Diwali and Carnival alike, and calypso/soca as sung, danced political satire all show zest for life consistently as a publicly shared, communal good. The relaxed \"Trini time\" is likewise culturally understood as a collective expression of joy, not a problem. A self-preservation undertone shows in the steel drum, crafted from old oil barrels—practical, personal ingenuity born of scarcity. Sexual Seven recedes behind this consistently communally celebrated zest for life." },
    { name: "Haiti", iso: "HT", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Despite one of the most traumatic histories in the Western Hemisphere—slavery, violent colonial rule, devastating earthquakes, and political instability—a spiritual daily life seeking to reconcile opposites has developed in Haiti: Vodou belief, often misunderstood, is fundamentally a practice of bringing ancestors, spirits, and the living into harmony. Communal \"Konbit\" work groups, where neighbors take turns working together in the fields, are a centuries-old principle of collective, undramatic cooperation. Despite extreme poverty, Haitian art—naive painting in vibrant colors—is internationally known for its life-affirming, often peaceful imagery. Music and dance accompany both religious and secular occasions as a means of balance. The remarkable ability to patiently rebuild daily life after every disaster, rather than remaining in despair, shapes the collective self-image to this day.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Haiti shows a clearly socially toned Nine: the communal \"konbit\" work groups, in which neighbors take turns working the fields together, and Vodou belief, which at its core brings ancestors, spirits, and the living into harmony, show a centuries-old principle of collective, undramatic cooperation and balance. Music and dance accompany religious and secular occasions alike as a communal means of balance. A self-preservation undertone shows in the remarkable, very personal ability to patiently rebuild daily life after every disaster rather than remaining in despair. Sexual Nine recedes behind this consistently communal, patiently lived balance." },
    { name: "El Salvador", iso: "SV", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "After decades of holding the world's highest murder rate, driven by brutal street gangs (MS-13, Barrio 18), President Nayib Bukele imposed a nationwide state of emergency starting in 2022 and arrested over 80,000 suspected gang members. The purpose-built mega-prison CECOT, with capacity for 40,000 inmates, was deliberately staged as an intimidating symbol of state power. Bukele himself jokingly refers to himself publicly as \"the world's coolest dictator\" — power is not hidden, but put on display. Despite (or because of) the harsh crackdown, public approval ratings exceed 80 percent — security through unconditional toughness is widely embraced. Criticism of human rights violations is openly and confidently dismissed by the government as the price of restored public order.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "El Salvador shows a pronouncedly social Eight: the arrest of over 80,000 suspected gang members, the purpose-built mega-prison CECOT as a deliberately staged symbol of state power, and approval ratings above 80 percent all show strength as a collectively willed, publicly celebrated principle—safety through unconditional, jointly embraced harshness. The open, confident dismissal of international criticism as the price of restored public order is likewise a collectively shared statement. A sexual undertone shows in President Bukele's personal, provocative self-staging, jokingly calling himself \"the world's coolest dictator\" in public—power as an individual, showcased personality. Self-preservation Eight recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated demonstration of power." },
    { name: "Nicaragua", iso: "NI", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Daniel Ortega, once leader of the Sandinista revolution against the Somoza dictatorship, has since his return to power in 2007 built an increasingly authoritarian system of control himself — the revolutionary became the new strongman. After the 2018 protests, brutally crushed with hundreds killed, the government consolidated power by systematically eliminating any serious opposition. Numerous opposition figures, priests, and journalists have been imprisoned, forced into exile, or stripped of citizenship — control over the national narrative as the top priority. The tight concentration of power within the presidential family, with his wife serving as vice president, reveals a system that places personal power above institutional continuity. Direct confrontation with international criticism, such as withdrawing from the Organization of American States, is confidently staged as proof of sovereignty rather than felt as isolation.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Nicaragua shows a pronouncedly social Eight: the systematic elimination of any serious opposition following the 2018 protests, the brutal crackdown with hundreds of deaths, and the imprisonment, denaturalization, or exile of numerous opposition figures, priests, and journalists show power as a collectively-institutionally enforced system of control. A sexual undertone shows in the direct, confrontational rejection of international criticism—such as withdrawal from the Organization of American States—confidently staged as personal proof of sovereignty, as well as in the tight concentration of power within the presidential family itself, with the wife serving as vice president. Self-preservation Eight recedes behind this consistently confrontational, institutionally and personally enforced exercise of power." },
    { name: "Belize", iso: "BZ", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Belize, the only English-speaking country in Central America, maintains an exceptionally diverse but largely low-conflict society of Creoles, Maya, Garifuna, Mestizos, and Mennonites. The national motto \"Sub Umbra Floreo\" (Under the Shade I Flourish) reflects a cultural baseline of existing in balance with larger forces rather than fighting against them. The huge barrier reef, the second largest in the world, is cultivated as a shared national natural heritage that creates identity across ethnic groups. Despite a long-standing border dispute with Guatemala, Belize consistently pursues the path of international mediation and diplomatic patience rather than military escalation. The relaxed \"Belizean go slow\" mentality, often noticed by visitors, is understood by residents themselves as a deliberate counter-model to the pace of neighboring countries.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Belize shows a clearly socially toned Nine: its exceptionally diverse yet largely low-conflict society of Creoles, Maya, Garifuna, Mestizos, and Mennonites, the jointly maintained Barrier Reef as a national, cross-ethnic natural heritage, and the consistent pursuit of international mediation over military escalation in the border dispute with Guatemala all show balance as a collectively lived core principle—existing in harmony with larger forces rather than fighting them. A self-preservation undertone shows in the relaxed \"Belizean go slow\" mentality, understood by residents themselves as a deliberate, personal counterpoint to the pace of neighboring countries. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently coexistence-oriented basic stance." },
    { name: "Barbados", iso: "BB", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Barbados, the first Caribbean island to remove the British queen as head of state and become a republic in 2021, deliberately presents itself as a progressive frontrunner in the region. The island produced Rihanna, one of the most successful pop stars in the world, celebrated as a national hero and ambassador — visible global success as a source of national pride. Barbados is considered one of the most economically developed countries in the Caribbean, with high literacy and a stable financial sector. Prime Minister Mia Mottley positions the country very visibly on the international stage as a leader in climate negotiations and economic reform (the \"Bridgetown Initiative\"). The historical rise from a former sugarcane colonial territory to one of the most stable democratic island states in the Caribbean is cultivated as a national success story.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Barbados shows a very pronouncedly social Three: becoming a republic in 2021, Prime Minister Mia Mottley's highly visible international positioning in climate negotiations and economic reforms, and the celebrated global success of Rihanna as a national hero all present success as a consciously outward-staged, collective status symbol. The historical rise from a colonial sugar territory to a stable democratic Caribbean frontrunner is likewise cultivated as a national success narrative. A self-preservation undertone shows in the practical economic substance behind it—high literacy and a stable financial sector that materially supports the public success. Sexual Three recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated, collective consciousness of success." },
    { name: "Grenada", iso: "GD", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Grenada experienced one of the Caribbean's most dramatic violations of sovereignty in 1983, when the US intervened militarily after a bloody intra-party coup — a trauma that has shaped a vigilant awareness of small-state vulnerability toward great powers even now. Since then, the country has pursued a deliberately cautious foreign policy that prioritizes close, protective relationships with regional alliances (CARICOM, the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States) over going it alone. After the devastating destruction wrought by Hurricane Ivan in 2004, which damaged over 90% of buildings, Grenada invested specifically in building codes and early warning systems to reduce risk. The nutmeg economy, known as the \"Spice Isle,\" is deliberately diversified and safeguarded to avoid being existentially hit by a weather event again. The small population maintains a strong, protective sense of community through church congregations and neighborhood networks, especially in times of crisis.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Grenada shows a pronouncedly social Six: its deliberately cautious foreign policy, which prioritizes close, protective ties to regional alliances like CARICOM over going it alone, and its targeted investments in building codes and early-warning systems following Hurricane Ivan, show security as an institutionally and collectively organized principle—a direct response to the trauma of the 1983 US intervention. The deliberate diversification of the nutmeg economy likewise serves collective protection against future shocks. A self-preservation undertone shows in the strong, protective sense of community through church congregations and neighborhood networks, especially in times of crisis—security in the close, personal circle. Sexual Six recedes behind this institutionally and neighborhood-anchored caution." },
    { name: "Saint Lucia", iso: "LC", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Saint Lucia, known for its exceptionally hospitable tourism culture, deliberately positions itself as an island of personal care and warm attention to visitors — from family-run boutique resorts to personally guided island tours. The traditional \"Jounen Kwéyòl\" (Creole Day), an annual community festival, celebrates togetherness and mutual care among residents across economic or political differences. As the birthplace of two Nobel laureates (Derek Walcott, literature; Arthur Lewis, economics), Saint Lucia proudly maintains a culture in which personal mentorship of young talent is understood as a national concern. Healthcare and social welfare, closely organized through church congregations and family networks, traditionally emphasize mutual personal support over state anonymity. The national self-image as the \"friendliest island in the Caribbean\" is actively cultivated by Saint Lucians themselves and understood as a source of both tourism and social success.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Saint Lucia shows a clearly socially toned Two: the traditional \"Jounen Kwéyòl,\" an annual community festival celebrating togetherness across economic and political differences, and the actively cultivated national self-image as the \"friendliest island in the Caribbean,\" show care as a deliberately cultivated, collective hallmark. The culture of personal mentorship and support for young talent—as birthplace of two Nobel laureates—is likewise understood as a national concern. A self-preservation undertone shows in family-run boutique resorts, personally guided island tours, and healthcare and social welfare organized closely through church congregations and family associations, emphasizing personal support over state anonymity. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad culture of care lived both publicly and within the family." },
    { name: "Dominica", iso: "DM", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Dominica, known as the \"Nature Island of the Caribbean,\" has deliberately committed itself to gentle harmony with its lush rainforest and volcanic landscape rather than aggressive tourism development like many neighboring islands. After near-total destruction by Hurricane Maria in 2017, the government announced the ambitious goal of becoming the world's first climate-resilient country — a deliberate response of balance with the forces of nature rather than pure rebuilding logic. The remaining indigenous Kalinago population, one of the last pre-Columbian communities in the Caribbean, lives in its own territory in comparatively peaceful coexistence with the majority population. Low population density and the deliberate avoidance of mass tourism (few sandy beaches, instead hiking trails and hot springs) reflect a cultural choice for balance over economic maximization. Dominica is one of the first countries to offer a \"citizenship by investment\" program, deliberately used to create economic balance without losing its own identity.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Dominica shows a very pronouncedly socially toned Nine: its deliberate choice against aggressive tourism development in favor of gentle harmony with its rainforest and volcanic landscape, the ambitious goal of becoming the world's first climate-resilient country after Hurricane Maria, and peaceful coexistence with the remaining indigenous Kalinago population all show balance as a deliberately institutionalized, collective principle. Its \"citizenship by investment\" program is likewise deliberately used to create economic balance without losing its own identity. A self-preservation undertone shows in forgoing mass tourism in favor of hiking trails and hot springs—personal, quiet retreat into nature rather than economic maximization. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently balance-oriented basic stance." },
    { name: "Antigua and Barbuda", iso: "AG", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Antigua and Barbuda deliberately advertises \"365 beaches — one for every day of the year\" as the tourism trademark of a culture of enjoyment, variety of experiences, and carefree island living. The famous English Harbour and the annual Antigua Sailing Week are considered international gathering points for sailing and leisure enthusiasts, with the country deliberately marketing itself as a stage for adventure and joie de vivre. The carnival-like celebration culture, especially the colorful Antigua Carnival, celebrates music, dance, and communal enjoyment as a central cultural element. Cricket as the national sport, with legends like Viv Richards, is followed with demonstrative passion and convivial enthusiasm rather than sober competitive rigor. The country consistently positions itself touristically as a place of boundless, varied vacation experiences, from diving to historical exploration, rather than as a quiet retreat.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Antigua and Barbuda shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: its marketing as \"365 beaches—one for every day of the year,\" the carnival-like festive culture of music, dance, and communal pleasure, and cricket as the national sport, followed with demonstrative, sociable passion, all show zest for life consistently as a publicly celebrated, communal good. International gathering points like English Harbour and Antigua Sailing Week likewise deliberately position the country as a communal stage for adventure. A sexual undertone shows in the marketing of boundless, varied experiences—from diving to historical exploration—as intense, personal experience-seeking. Self-preservation Seven recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated culture of experience and celebration." },
    { name: "Saint Kitts and Nevis", iso: "KN", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "St. Kitts and Nevis, the smallest sovereign state in the Americas, has pursued a deliberately cautious policy of close regional alliances (OECS, CARICOM) rather than unilateral foreign policy since independence in 1983, in order to safeguard its own small size. The country pioneered \"citizenship by investment\" (since 1984), a program deliberately designed to create additional financial security against economic vulnerability as a small state. The historic Brimstone Hill Fortress, once one of the most heavily fortified sites in the Caribbean, still symbolizes the centuries-old awareness of small island states' military vulnerability today. Nevis was granted the constitutional right in 1998 to secede from St. Kitts via referendum — a deliberately created legal safeguard against future conflicts between the two islands. The economy, once heavily dependent on sugarcane, was deliberately broadly diversified into tourism, offshore finance, and the aforementioned investment programs after the industry's collapse in 2005, to avoid renewed economic vulnerability.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "St. Kitts and Nevis shows a pronouncedly social Six: its deliberately cautious policy of close regional alliances (OECS, CARICOM) since independence, its pioneering role in the \"citizenship by investment\" program for financial security, and the economy's deliberate broad diversification after the collapse of sugar all show safety as an institutionally organized, collective principle against the country's own vulnerability as a small state. The historic Brimstone Hill fortress likewise still symbolizes this centuries-old, collective awareness of military vulnerability. A self-preservation undertone shows in Nevis's constitutionally anchored right, since 1998, to secede from St. Kitts by referendum—a very personal legal safeguard tied to its own, smaller identity. Sexual Six recedes behind this consistently institutionally anchored caution." },
    { name: "Bahamas", iso: "BS", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "The Bahamas, made up of over 700 islands, have deliberately positioned themselves for decades as the epitome of carefree vacation enjoyment — from the cruise-ship hub of Nassau to exclusive resort islands to leisure attractions like swimming with pigs or sharks. The historical reputation as a former pirate and smuggler's paradise is deliberately romanticized touristically as a symbol of unrestrained, adventurous freedom. The economy, almost entirely oriented toward tourism and offshore financial services, consistently favors light-hearted, experience-oriented consumption over industrial production. The Junkanoo parade, a colorful, loud street festival at Christmas and New Year, celebrates music, costumes, and exuberant festive spirit as a central cultural event. The Bahamas consistently market themselves internationally through sensory appeal — turquoise water, sunsets, party scene — rather than cultural depth or political themes.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "The Bahamas show a very pronouncedly social Seven: their deliberate positioning as the epitome of carefree vacation pleasure, the Junkanoo parade as a colorful, loud communal street festival, and consistent international marketing through turquoise water, sunsets, and party scenes all show zest for life consistently as a publicly staged, communal experience. A sexual undertone shows in the deliberate, touristic romanticizing of the historical pirate and smuggler reputation as a symbol of unrestrained, adventurous, personal freedom. Self-preservation Seven, which would show up as quiet, functional commerce, recedes behind this consistently publicly celebrated culture of experience." },
  ]},
  { region: "South America", laender: [
    { name: "Brazil", iso: "BR", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "The Rio Carnival, with months of preparation by samba schools and millions of participants, is not a tourist show, but the central cultural event of the year. Football in Brazil is understood not merely as a sport, but as \"joga bonito,\" the beautiful game—creativity and joy in play count more than pure efficiency. The beaches of Copacabana and Ipanema are everyday social gathering spots where life happens outside and physically, not behind closed doors. Despite enormous social inequality, Brazilian music—Samba, Bossa Nova, Funk Carioca—is characterized by a lightness that comments on grievances with humor rather than accusation. \"Jeitinho brasileiro,\" the art of finding creative detours around rules and obstacles, is considered an admired national trait—variety of options over rigid structure.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Brazil shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: the Rio Carnival, with months of communal preparation by the samba schools and millions of participants, and the beaches as everyday, public social meeting points, show zest for life consistently as a shared, public celebration. Brazilian music—samba, bossa nova, funk carioca—which comments on grievances with humor rather than accusation despite enormous social inequality, likewise shows lightness as a collective coping strategy. A sexual undertone shows in \"jeitinho brasileiro,\" the art of finding creative, personal workarounds around rules and obstacles—charm and ingenuity in dealing with the individual rather than rigid structure. Self-preservation Seven recedes behind this consistently publicly lived zest for life." },
    { name: "Argentina", iso: "AR", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Tango, originating in the port neighborhoods of Buenos Aires, is a dance of longing and loss—\"tres minutos de melancolía bailada,\" three minutes of danced melancholy, as singer Enrique Santos Discépolo phrased it. National poet Jorge Luis Borges wove time, identity, and loss into a literature of extraordinary introspective depth. Football icon Diego Maradona is revered to the present day not just as an athlete, but as a tragic, deeply human figure—fallibility and greatness inextricably entwined. The economic crises of recent decades, with repeated loss of middle-class wealth, have left behind a collective sense of shared, often proudly carried tragedy. Cafés in Buenos Aires, many over a hundred years old, are places for long, melancholic conversations about lost greatness and what could have been.", subtyp: "sx4", subtypText: "Argentina shows a pronouncedly sexually toned Four: the tango, \"three minutes of danced melancholy,\" is an intense, physically close dance of longing and loss between two people, and the still-ongoing, almost passionate veneration of Diego Maradona as a tragic, deeply human figure shows intense, personal identification rather than distanced admiration. A self-preservation undertone shows in the introspective depth of Jorge Luis Borges's literature and in the century-old cafés of Buenos Aires, places of quiet, personal conversation about lost greatness. Social Four, which would show up more as collectively shared, public mourning, recedes behind this consistently intense, personal melancholy." },
    { name: "Colombia", iso: "CO", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Colombian hospitality is shown in spontaneously inviting even strangers to meals—doors and refrigerators are traditionally open to visitors. Despite decades of civil war with the FARC and the drug conflict of the nineties, the internationally perceived warmth of the people has barely changed—cities like Medellín, once symbols of violence, are today considered models for community-led urban renewal through neighborhood projects. Music, from Cumbia to Vallenato, is mostly played communally at family celebrations rather than as solo performances. \"Sobremesa\"—lingering long at the table after a meal to talk—is lived care for one another in the form of shared time. Even after the 2016 peace agreement, rebuilding trust between former conflicting parties remains a central social project, carried by civil society engagement.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Colombia shows a clearly socially toned Two: spontaneous hospitality, where doors and refrigerators are open even to strangers, music played communally at family celebrations, and the rebuilding of trust after the 2016 peace agreement, carried by civil-society engagement, all show care as a broad, societal principle. A self-preservation undertone shows in \"sobremesa,\" lingering long at the table after a meal—care lived in the close, personal circle, as shared time rather than grand gesture. Sexual Two, with its exclusive devotion to a single person, recedes behind this broad, societally lived warmth." },
    { name: "Chile", iso: "CL", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "Chile is considered economically and institutionally one of the most stable and well-organized countries in Latin America—a reputation consciously cultivated over decades through disciplined fiscal policy. The country was the first in the region to introduce an independent, rule-based pension system and strict central bank independence. Chilean wine production follows rigorous, internationally recognized quality standards that are consistently maintained. The education protest movement of the 2010s, though conflict-ridden, resulted in an orderly, lengthy constitutional process rather than permanent chaos—even upheaval proceeded in a structured manner. In regional comparison, Chile is seen as the country with the highest demand for adherence to rules, reliability, and orderly institutions.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Chile shows a very pronouncedly social One: the independent, rule-based pension system, strict central bank independence, and the orderly, protracted constitutional reform following the education protest movement rather than lasting chaos, all show order as a deliberately institutionalized, collective principle—even the upheaval proceeded in a structured way. The regional reputation as the country with the highest standard of rule-adherence and orderly institutions is likewise disciplinedly cultivated over decades. A self-preservation undertone shows in Chilean wine production, which follows strict, internationally recognized quality standards—personal, craftsmanlike precision. Sexual One clearly recedes behind this consistently institutionally anchored love of order." },
    { name: "Peru", iso: "PE", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Machu Picchu, hidden high in the Andes and barely noticed by the outside world for centuries, is an apt symbol for a country that kept its greatest treasure withdrawn and in secret for a long time. Quechua culture, with a spiritual connection to the mountains (Apus) and the earth (Pachamama) grown over millennia, maintains introverted, ritualistic forms of nature worship far from loud public displays. Peruvian cuisine, celebrated globally today, developed over centuries in relative isolation from Indigenous, Spanish, and Asian influences before being presented to the world. Andean communities at high altitudes often live physically isolated from urban centers, with their own independent supply systems. Retreat, patience, and a deep, quiet rather than loud relationship to their own cultural heritage shape the national character.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Peru shows a very clearly self-preservation-toned Five: Machu Picchu, barely noticed by the outside world for centuries, the introverted, ritual forms of Quechua nature veneration far from public display, and Andean communities, often physically isolated with their own independent supply systems, all show retreat and quiet preservation as a consistent cultural principle. A social undertone shows in Peruvian cuisine, which developed over centuries in relative isolation before being deliberately and carefully presented to the world—a shared cultural treasure, eventually offered. Sexual Five clearly recedes behind this consistently withdrawn, patient Peruvian basic stance." },
    { name: "Venezuela", iso: "VE", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Venezuela has won more pageant crowns than any other country in the world—beauty contests enjoy a social standing that goes far beyond entertainment and is seen as a path to social advancement. Plastic surgery is socially normalized and discussed openly, often understood as a legitimate effort to increase one's own visibility and expressiveness. Despite severe economic crisis and mass emigration in recent years, a cultural prioritization of physical appearance and self-presentation remarkably persists. The former oil economy created a self-image of economic success and status over decades that continues to echo today, even as reality has drastically changed. However, this assessment remains more uncertain than for other countries, as the political crisis overlays many traditional cultural patterns.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Venezuela likely shows a socially toned Three: the internationally unmatched number of beauty-pageant crowns, socially regarded as a path to upward mobility, and the long-lingering self-image of economic success from the oil era show success as a publicly visible, collectively shared status symbol. A sexual undertone shows in socially normalized plastic surgery, openly understood as a legitimate, very personal way to increase one's own visibility and expressive presence. Self-preservation Three recedes behind this consistently appearance- and public-image-oriented basic stance. As with the main type, this assessment remains less certain than for other countries, since the ongoing political crisis overlays many traditional cultural patterns." },
    { name: "Ecuador", iso: "EC", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "In markets like Otavalo, bargaining is not just business, but social exchange—sellers and buyers often maintain personal relationships for years. The Indigenous Kichwa culture of the Andes practices \"Minga,\" communal voluntary labor for the village, where every family helps out without expectation of payment. Host families often welcome visitors, including strangers, with a natural warmth that goes beyond mere politeness. The Andean region and the Amazon basin rely on tight family networks that stand by each other during harvest, illness, and celebrations. Even political instability in recent decades has barely altered this deeply rooted impulse for mutual care in daily life.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Ecuador shows a clearly socially toned Two: \"minga,\" communal voluntary work for the village in which every family helps without pay, the years-long personal relationships between vendors and customers at markets like Otavalo, and the matter-of-course welcoming of guest families, even strangers, all show care as a broad, communal principle. A self-preservation undertone shows in the close family networks of the Andean and Amazon regions, which support one another through harvest, illness, and festivals—care in the close, personal circle. Sexual Two recedes behind this consistently communally lived culture of care." },
    { name: "Bolivia", iso: "BO", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "With Lake Titicaca and the Altiplano highlands over 3,800 meters above sea level, a significant portion of the population lives in one of the most isolated inhabited regions in the world. Aymara and Quechua cultures preserve independent spiritual and agricultural traditions, often largely independent of state structures in La Paz. \"Cholita\" wrestling in El Alto combines traditional Indigenous attire with modern entertainment—an expression of independent cultural reinterpretation away from external trends. Bolivia's historical isolation, lacking sea access since the loss of its coastal region in 1904, reinforces a self-understanding as an introverted landlocked country. Traditional knowledge about agriculture at extreme altitudes is mostly passed down within the community rather than marketed externally.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Bolivia shows a very clearly self-preservation-toned Five: a significant share of the population living in one of the most remote inhabited regions in the world, historical isolation as a landlocked country since 1904, and traditional knowledge of agriculture at extreme altitude, mostly kept within the community rather than marketed outward, all show retreat and self-sufficiency as a consistent cultural principle. A social undertone shows in the independent spiritual and agricultural traditions of the Aymara and Quechua cultures, maintained largely independent of state structures but within their own community. Sexual Five clearly recedes behind this consistently introverted, inward-turned Bolivian basic stance." },
    { name: "Uruguay", iso: "UY", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Uruguay was the first country in Latin America to legalize same-sex marriage and cannabis consumption—not out of provocation, but from a pragmatic effort to defuse social conflicts instead of criminalizing them. Former President José Mujica, who donated most of his salary and lived in a simple farmhouse, became an international symbol for unagitated, modest politics. Compared to neighboring Argentina and Brazil, its political history progressed remarkably undramatically, with stable democracy since the 1980s. Football, a national passion like everywhere in the region, is celebrated in Uruguay remarkably peacefully and without the violent excesses seen in some neighboring countries. The \"Mate\" tea drinking ritual, shared together from one vessel among many, symbolizes a relaxed, undramatic sociability in daily life.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Uruguay shows a very pronouncedly socially toned Nine: the pragmatic legalization of same-sex marriage and cannabis to defuse social conflicts rather than criminalize them, the strikingly undramatic political history with stable democracy since the 1980s, and remarkably peacefully celebrated football fervor without the violent excesses seen in some neighboring countries, all show balance as a consistently collective, societal principle. Sharing mate tea from one shared vessel likewise symbolizes relaxed, undramatic sociability in daily life. A self-preservation undertone shows in former President José Mujica, who donated most of his salary and lived in a simple farmhouse—personal, unruffled modesty as a lived principle. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently societally lived ease." },
    { name: "Paraguay", iso: "PY", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Following the devastating War of the Triple Alliance in the 19th century, which cost the lives of an estimated half of the male population, and a long dictatorship under Stroessner in the 20th century, caution toward political promises is deeply anchored. The Guaraní language is actively preserved alongside Spanish—a commitment to holding onto their own identity as protection against external assimilation. Extended families, often across multiple generations in the same household, remain the most important social safety net, more essential than state institutions. Agricultural communities in the hinterland still live off subsistence farming and mutual neighborly help in many places rather than market integration. Political restraint and a watchful attitude toward rapid social changes continue to shape the national character today.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Paraguay shows a clearly self-preservation-toned Six: extended families, often spanning several generations in the same household, remain the most important, very personal social security system, more important than state institutions, and rural communities in the countryside often live by subsistence farming and mutual neighborly help rather than market integration. A social undertone shows in the deeply rooted, collectively shared distrust of political promises following the devastating War of the Triple Alliance and the long Stroessner dictatorship, as well as in actively maintaining the Guaraní language alongside Spanish as collective protection of identity. Sexual Six recedes behind this consistently family- and historically-anchored caution." },
    { name: "Suriname", iso: "SR", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Suriname is one of the most ethnically diverse countries in the world — descendants of African slaves, Indian and Javanese indentured laborers, indigenous peoples, Chinese, and Dutch all live together in a small space without this diversity erupting into open conflict. The capital Paramaribo, with mosques, synagogues, Hindu temples, and churches in immediate proximity, is considered a lived example of religious coexistence and is a UNESCO World Heritage site not least because of this peaceful closeness. The informal national motto \"Wan pipel\" (One People) is deliberately cultivated as a unifying, undramatic offer of identity across all population groups. Despite a short, bloody civil war in the 1980s, the country quickly returned to an everyday life of pragmatic adaptation and balance between population groups. The dense, largely undeveloped rainforests in the country's interior, covering more than ninety percent of its area, reflect a cultural baseline of avoiding conflict rather than seeking it.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Suriname shows a very pronouncedly socially toned Nine: its extraordinary ethnic diversity living together without open conflict, the capital Paramaribo with mosques, synagogues, Hindu temples, and churches in immediate proximity as a lived example of religious coexistence, and the national motto \"wan pipel\" (one people) as a deliberately cultivated, unifying offer of identity, all show balance as a consistently collective, societal principle. A self-preservation undertone shows in the dense, barely developed rainforests covering over ninety percent of the country's area—a cultural baseline of personally avoiding conflict rather than seeking it. Sexual Nine clearly recedes behind this consistently coexistence-oriented basic stance." },
    { name: "Guyana", iso: "GY", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Guyana, one of the most sparsely populated countries on Earth with vast, largely unexplored rainforest areas, has made an international name for itself primarily through scientific and ecological research projects rather than political or economic displays of power. The country hosts extraordinary biodiversity that is increasingly being systematically mapped and studied — knowledge about its own, largely undeveloped territory as national capital. The extreme ethnic and linguistic diversity (descendants of Indian, African, indigenous, Chinese, and Portuguese immigrants) is managed rather reservedly and analytically rather than openly celebrated. The decades-long border dispute with Venezuela over the oil-rich Essequibo region, argued meticulously with historical documents and maps, is deliberately pursued by Guyana through international courts and fact-based legal argumentation rather than confrontation. Guyana's predominantly introverted, unexcited public culture stands in noticeable contrast to its louder, more extroverted Caribbean neighbors.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Guyana shows a very clearly self-preservation-toned Five: its international reputation built mainly on scientific and ecological research projects rather than political or economic displays of power, the reserved, analytical rather than openly celebrated management of extreme ethnic diversity, and the predominantly introverted, unagitated public culture—in striking contrast to its louder Caribbean neighbors—all show retreat and quiet knowledge as a consistent cultural principle. A social undertone shows in the decades-long border dispute with Venezuela over the Essequibo region, deliberately pursued through international courts and fact-based legal argumentation rather than confrontation—institutional, matter-of-fact distance rather than open conflict. Sexual Five clearly recedes behind this consistently introverted Guyanese basic stance." },
  ]},
  { region: "Africa", laender: [
    { name: "Morocco", iso: "MA", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "In Morocco, the rule applies: A guest entering a house must be invited for tea at least three times before leaving—declining the first offer is part of the ritual, but in the end, no one goes without hospitality. Traditional Riad architecture, featuring inner courtyards instead of windows facing the street, symbolizes that the center of the house and care is directed inward, toward the people within. In the souks of Marrakech and Fès, haggling is a social act of relationship-building, not just price negotiation—agreeing too quickly is almost considered rude because it lacks conversation. Families frequently take in distant relatives or neighbors in need permanently, without this being viewed as an extraordinary gesture. Travelers repeatedly report being spontaneously invited to meals—a lived norm, not an exception for special occasions.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Morocco shows a clearly social Two: the ritual of the threefold tea invitation, communal haggling as relationship-building, and taking in distant relatives or needy neighbors show care as a publicly lived norm bound to hospitality rules, not a private exception. A self-preservation undertone appears in Riad architecture, which consistently directs the house's center inward toward its inhabitants—care begins in the innermost, protected circle. Sexual Two, which would show as exclusive devotion to individuals, recedes behind this broad, ritualized hospitality toward practically every visitor." },
    { name: "Egypt", iso: "EG", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "It is not the pyramids, but the daily lives of its people that reveal the core Egyptian pattern: The extended family, often with three generations under one roof or in the same neighborhood, serves as the primary safeguard against economic and political uncertainty. The expression \"Inshallah\"—\"God willing\"—accompanies almost every statement about the future, expressing a cautious restraint toward firm commitments. After decades of political upheaval, from Nasser to Sadat, Mubarak, and the 2011 Arab Spring, mistrust toward quick political promises remains deeply anchored. Neighborhood \"Baltagiya\" networks for informal self-defense historically arose as a response to unreliable state security. Religion, whether Islamic or Coptic Christian, offers many Egyptians an additional, dependable framework of structure and community in uncertain times.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Egypt shows a pronouncedly self-preservation Six: the extended family as the most important safeguard against economic and political uncertainty, distrust of political promises, and informal neighborhood safety networks like the \"Baltagiya\" aim directly at concrete, material protection of one's own environment. A social share appears in religion and community, which offer additional support and structure—whether Islamic or Coptic Christian, always as a collective framework. Sexual Six, which would show as defiant confrontation with authority, recedes behind this cautious, family-anchored security strategy." },
    { name: "South Africa", iso: "ZA", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Following decades of apartheid, with its systematic separation and oppression of the Black majority, South Africa consciously chose the path of the \"Truth and Reconciliation Commission\" after 1994 instead of retribution—publicly speaking out about injustice as a path to peace, not revenge. \"Ubuntu\"—I am because we are—is more than a catchphrase: It describes a lived philosophy in which individual well-being is thought of as inextricably linked to that of the community. Nelson Mandela, imprisoned for 27 years, did not become an avenging angel upon his release, but a symbol of reconciliatory leadership—a national role model whose influence endures even today. The \"Rainbow Nation,\" featuring eleven official languages, consciously attempts to hold diversity together rather than suppress it. Despite ongoing social tensions and crime, public discourse remains noticeably marked by the search for a shared path rather than division.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "South Africa shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: \"Ubuntu\"—I am because we are—makes individual well-being explicitly dependent on the well-being of the community, and the \"Rainbow Nation\" with eleven official languages consciously seeks cohesion amid diversity rather than retreat into subgroups. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which favored publicly voicing injustice over silent withdrawal, shows the same priority on collective harmony. A self-preservation undertone lies in the pragmatic clinging to shared national daily life despite crime and tension. Sexual Nine, which would show as exclusive fusion with individuals, recedes behind this broad national reconciliation project." },
    { name: "Nigeria", iso: "NG", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Nollywood, Nigeria's film industry, produces the second-most feature films in the world after Bollywood—an explosive, entrepreneurial growth despite limited resources and infrastructure. Afrobeats stars like Burna Boy or Wizkid fill international stadiums today—musical success is celebrated as national pride and proof of global competitiveness. The \"hustle\" mentality, an informal entrepreneurial spirit in an often challenging economic environment, is visible everywhere in daily life, from street vendors to tech startups in Lagos. Nigerian fashion, represented internationally by designers like Lisa Folawiyo, combines traditional patterns with a confident, modern presentation of status. Educational success is pursued with great emphasis in Nigerian families—a university degree or studies abroad are considered proof of advancement and celebrated accordingly.", subtyp: "se3", subtypText: "Nigeria shows a strongly self-preservation Three: the \"hustle\" mentality, the informal entrepreneurial spirit from street vendors to tech start-ups in Lagos, aims directly at economic survival and advancement under difficult material conditions. The family emphasis on educational success and a university degree as visible proof of advancement reinforces this picture of status earned through hard, concrete achievement. A social share shows in Nollywood and the internationally celebrated Afrobeats stars, whose success counts as collective national pride. Sexual Three, which seeks individual attractiveness and competition for one person, recedes behind this broadly economically driven culture of advancement." },
    { name: "Kenya", iso: "KE", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "\"Harambee\"—Swahili for \"let us pull together\"—was not only the national motto after independence, but to this day describes local self-help groups that pool money for neighbors' school fees, weddings, or medical emergencies. Kenyan hospitality manifests in spontaneously sharing Chai (tea) with any visitor, regardless of status. Marathon runners from the Rift Valley traditionally train in groups, openly sharing knowledge and training plans with one another rather than withholding advantages from competitors. \"Harambee\" building projects, in which entire villages construct schools or wells together, remain common today, even without state funding. Extended family networks often span multiple cities and countries, with a clear duty of mutual financial support within the broader family.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Kenya shows a clearly social Two: \"Harambee\"—let us pull together—as both national motto and lived practice of local self-help groups is the institutionalization of mutual care at the community level, visible in jointly financed schools and wells. The open sharing of training knowledge among Rift Valley marathon runners instead of competitive secrecy confirms this pattern of collective rather than individual advancement. A self-preservation share shows in the extended family networks with clear mutual financial support obligations across cities and countries. Sexual Two, which seeks exclusive devotion to individuals, recedes behind this broad, institutionalized community care." },
    { name: "Ethiopia", iso: "ET", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "As the only African country never permanently colonized (apart from a brief Italian occupation), Ethiopia has preserved a deeply rooted self-understanding of cultural independence—possessing its own script (Ge'ez), its own calendar, and even its own system of keeping time. The Ethiopian Orthodox Church, one of the oldest Christian traditions in the world, practices long, contemplative liturgies in relative isolation from Western church influences. The traditional coffee ceremony, where beans are roasted in front of guests and prepared over several hours, is a ritual of slow, introspective sociability, not rush. The highland topography, with many isolated mountain villages, has preserved distinct, mutually independent local cultures for centuries. Ethiopia's restraint toward rapid external influence is evident to this day in a deliberate cultivation of its own original traditions.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Ethiopia shows a clearly self-preservation Five: never having been permanently colonized, its own script, its own calendar, and its own time-reckoning show a need to preserve one's own reservoir of resources and knowledge independent of outside access. The isolated highland villages with self-contained, mutually independent cultures and the contemplative liturgy of the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, shielded from Western influence, reinforce this picture of withdrawal and self-sufficiency. A social share shows in the coffee ceremony as a slow, shared ritual of sociability. Sexual Five, which would show as intense fusion with a few trusted individuals, recedes behind this broad cultural reserve." },
    { name: "Senegal", iso: "SN", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "\"Teranga,\" the Senegalese word for hospitality, is so central that Senegal officially refers to itself as the \"Land of Teranga\"—hospitality as a national brand identity. A Senegalese proverb states that a guest brings blessings into the home—visitors are treated accordingly with the best available, even if it is little. Sharing meals from a single large bowl, where all family members and guests eat together using their right hand, is a daily, bonding ritual. Music, especially the Sabar drums, accompanies almost every social event and actively invites the shared participation of all present. Sufism, the predominant form of Islam in Senegal, emphasizes community and spiritual leadership that cares for the well-being of its followers.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Senegal shows a very pronouncedly social Two: \"Teranga\" is hospitality elevated to official national self-identity, visible in shared eating from one bowl and Sabar drums that actively invite everyone present to join in. Sufism as the predominant form of Islam, which explicitly organizes spiritual leadership around the well-being of the community of followers, reinforces this pattern of institutionalized care. A self-preservation undertone shows in the proverb that a guest brings blessing into the house—hosting even with scarce means as a safeguard of one's own standing. Sexual Two recedes behind this public, ritualized hospitality for every visitor." },
    { name: "Ghana", iso: "GH", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "In 1957, Ghana became the first sub-Saharan colony to gain independence, celebrating this even now with a distinct, carefree pride rather than heavy symbolism. Highlife and later Afrobeats music, with danceable rhythms, accompany practically every social occasion, from funerals to weddings. Ghanaian funerals are infamous for their elaborate, sometimes multi-day festivities featuring music and dancing—here, even death is reinterpreted into a celebration of life. \"Fantasy Coffins,\" artfully sculpted caskets shaped like cars, animals, or tools, demonstrate a culture that extracts creativity and lightness even from grave occasions. Street markets and local community festivals are loud, colorful, and welcoming—sociability as the underlying tone of daily life, despite economic challenges.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Ghana shows a pronouncedly social Seven: highlife and Afrobeats music accompany practically every social occasion, and even funerals are reinterpreted as multi-day, communal celebrations with music and dance—zest for life is consistently celebrated publicly and collectively, not enjoyed privately. The \"Fantasy Coffins\" and the loud, colorful street-market culture confirm that creativity and lightness are deliberately shared with others. A self-preservation share shows in the carefree pride in the independence gained in 1957 as the earliest sub-Saharan colony, celebrated without heavy symbolism. Sexual Seven, with its search for the one intense experience, recedes behind this broad, everyday sociability." },
    { name: "Algeria", iso: "DZ", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Algerian War of Independence against France (1954–1962), with an estimated one million deaths, ranks among the bloodiest anti-colonial conflicts in history and remains a central point of reference for national pride to the present day. The \"Front de Libération Nationale\" is celebrated in textbooks and public commemorations as heroic resistance, never glossed over or downplayed. Even after independence, Algerian history remained marked by confrontation, such as during the bloody civil war of the 1990s against Islamist groups. The \"Hirak\" protest movement of 2019, which forced long-time President Bouteflika to resign, demonstrated once again a readiness for resolute, collective confrontation with state power. Directness and a robust, unyielding demeanor are viewed in Algerian society as strengths rather than problems.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Algeria shows a pronouncedly social Eight: the War of Independence with over a million deaths is celebrated as collective, heroic resistance in schoolbooks, and the 2019 \"Hirak\" protest movement again showed organized, communal confrontation with state power rather than individual struggle. The civil war of the 1990s, too, was experienced and processed as a collective confrontation of society with Islamist groups. A self-preservation undertone shows in the still-robust, uncompromising everyday stance as a survival strategy. Sexual Eight, which would show as intense one-on-one confrontation, recedes behind this broad, historically anchored culture of collective resistance." },
    { name: "Tunisia", iso: "TN", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Tunisian hospitality is closely related to the Moroccan tradition: Visitors are welcomed with mint tea and pastries before the actual reason for the visit is even discussed. In the medinas of Tunis or Sousse, personal relationships between merchants and regular customers have grown over years, featuring conversations that extend far beyond a sale. Family celebrations, especially weddings, often last for days and involve the entire extended neighborhood. As the birthplace of the Arab Spring in 2011, Tunisia simultaneously showed a remarkable capacity for a comparatively peaceful, negotiated political transition instead of lingering violence. The emphasis on education and social cohesion, even during economically difficult times, reflects a culture that holds care for one another in high regard.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Tunisia shows a socially shaped Two: the personal bonds grown over years between merchants and regular customers in the medinas, and multi-day wedding celebrations that involve the entire neighborhood, show care as a communally organized, recurring ritual. The comparatively peaceful, negotiated political transition after the Arab Spring of 2011 confirms a culture that prefers collective cohesion to confrontation. A self-preservation share shows in the welcoming ritual with mint tea and pastries before any actual reason for the visit is discussed—care begins in the immediate, domestic setting. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, socially anchored culture of care." },
    { name: "Uganda", iso: "UG", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Once named the \"Pearl of Africa\" by Winston Churchill, Uganda combines a difficult history under Idi Amin with a strikingly joyful present-day culture. Music and dance, from traditional Kiganda rhythms to modern Afrobeat, accompany virtually every social gathering, from church services to market squares. The youthful population structure—one of the youngest in the world—shapes an energetic, optimistic street culture in cities like Kampala. Local comedy shows and improvised street theater are popular, often free forms of entertainment that flourish despite economic constraints. Sociability in the form of shared meals, often centered around Matoke (plantains), serves as a daily, unifying ritual across all social classes.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Uganda shows a socially colored Seven: music and dance accompany practically every gathering from church services to marketplaces, and the youthful, energetic street culture in Kampala shows optimism as a shared, public mood rather than individual enjoyment. Local comedy shows and improvised street theater as popular, often free entertainment confirm this pattern of collective lightness despite a difficult history under Idi Amin. A self-preservation share shows in shared meals with matoke as a connecting everyday ritual across all social classes. Sexual Seven recedes behind this broad, publicly lived zest for life." },
    { name: "Tanzania", iso: "TZ", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Julius Nyerere, Tanzania's first president, developed an explicit political program with \"Ujamaa,\" advocating family hood and collective economics over competition—an ideal of harmony that shaped national politics for decades. Zanzibar and the mainland united in 1964 to form one of the few permanently peaceful state unions in Africa, avoiding major ongoing conflicts. Swahili, promoted as a shared national language across dozens of ethnic groups, deliberately served the goal of creating unity rather than ethnic division. Compared to many neighboring countries, Tanzania remained spared from major civil wars or genocides—a remarkable degree of political stability. Daily life in rural regions remains shaped even today by communal farming and a relaxed, non-confrontational tone.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Tanzania shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: \"Ujamaa\" was an explicit political program of family hood and collective economics instead of competition, and Swahili, promoted as a national language across dozens of ethnicities, deliberately served the goal of creating unity rather than ethnic division. The peaceful union of Zanzibar and the mainland in 1964 and the largely avoided major civil wars confirm this priority on collective harmony. A self-preservation undertone shows in communal agriculture and the relaxed, low-confrontation everyday life of rural regions. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, politically and linguistically anchored ideal of unity." },
    { name: "Zimbabwe", iso: "ZW", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Following years of political and economic turmoil—hyperinflation that reached astronomical proportions in 2008 alongside ongoing political repression—a deep need for family and community protection has developed in Zimbabwe. Extended family networks, often spread across city and countryside, support each other financially and practically when state institutions fail. Church communities, whether traditional Christian or charismatic new movements, offer additional social support and predictability in uncertain times. The experience of seeing savings rendered worthless virtually overnight by hyperinflation has left behind a deep mistrust toward official economic promises. Caution, frugality, and building informal reserves—such as holding livestock or goods in kind rather than cash—remain widespread precautionary strategies today.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Zimbabwe shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the experience of the 2008 hyperinflation, which made savings worthless virtually overnight, produced concrete precautionary strategies such as building reserves in the form of livestock or goods instead of cash—security through material rather than monetary value. The extended family networks that support each other financially and practically when state institutions fail reinforce this picture of practical self-provision. A social share shows in church communities that offer additional social support and predictability. Sexual Six, which would show as defiant confrontation, recedes behind this cautious, materially anchored security strategy." },
    { name: "Ivory Coast", iso: "CI", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "Coupé-Décalé, a music and dance style invented in Paris by Ivorian migrants that is now popular across West Africa, embodies a culture that exports festive spirit and pride even from afar. Street parties (maquis) featuring grilled fish and loud music are an integral part of evening life in Abidjan, regardless of the country's economic ups and downs. Despite two civil wars in the 2000s and 2010s, Ivorian party culture recovered remarkably fast—music and dance serve as collective ways to look forward again. Football enjoys immense status, triggering spontaneous street celebrations with every victory of the national team, the \"Elephants.\" Traditional hospitality toward travelers merges with a pronounced preference for loud, social gatherings over withdrawn silence.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Ivory Coast shows a very pronouncedly social Seven: Coupé-Décalé, invented in Paris by Ivorian migrants and now popular across West Africa, along with street parties (maquis) with grilled fish and loud music, show high spirits as a collectively exported, public baseline. The spontaneous street celebrations after every win by the \"Elephants\" and the remarkably fast recovery of the celebration culture after two civil wars confirm music and dance as a shared way of looking forward together. A self-preservation undertone shows in traditional hospitality toward travelers. Sexual Seven recedes behind this broad, loud communal culture." },
    { name: "Cameroon", iso: "CM", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "With over 250 ethnic groups and languages, Cameroon is one of the most culturally diverse countries in Africa—a diversity traditionally held together by close family and village care networks rather than enforced standardization. The \"Njangi,\" a traditional rotating savings system in which group members take turns receiving financial support, remains a widespread form of practical solidarity to this day. Shared meals, often centered around Ndolé or other traditional dishes, are deliberately shared with neighbors and extended family, not just immediate household members. Traditional chiefdoms (fondoms), particularly in the western part of the country, preserve social care structures older than the modern state. Christian and Muslim communities provide additional, often cross-border support networks for families in need.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Cameroon shows a socially shaped Two: the \"Njangi,\" a traditional rotating savings system with financial support distributed in turn, and the more than 250 ethnic groups held together by family and village care networks rather than forced uniformity, show care as an institutionalized community system. The traditional chiefdoms (Fondoms), which preserve social care structures older than the modern state, reinforce this pattern. A self-preservation share shows in the deliberate sharing of meals with neighbors and extended family beyond the innermost circle. Sexual Two recedes behind these broad, systematically organized structures of solidarity." },
    { name: "DR Congo", iso: "CD", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Under Belgian colonial rule, particularly during the personal reign of King Leopold II, millions of people were killed or mutilated through forced labor in the rubber trade within the so-called Congo Free State—one of the most brutal colonial histories of all, whose trauma endures in the collective memory. Since the 1990s, the country has experienced several of the deadliest armed conflicts since World War II, with millions killed in the Congo Wars. Despite this history, one of Africa's most vibrant music cultures developed here—Rumba Congolaise and Soukous are considered precursors to many African pop styles—yet it often arose precisely as a coping mechanism in the face of ongoing insecurity. Family and village communities remain the most reliable, and often only, functioning protective authority in large parts of the country. Vigilance regarding armed groups and everyday caution are simple necessities for survival in many regions today.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "DR Congo shows a clearly self-preservation Six: vigilance toward armed groups and everyday caution remain a plain survival necessity across much of the country, and family and village communities remain the most reliable, often only functioning protective institution amid the trauma of the colonial era and repeated wars. A social share shows in the vibrant, communally carried music culture—Rumba Congolaise and Soukous—which often emerged precisely as a collective coping attempt amid ongoing insecurity. Sexual Six, which would show as defiant confrontation, recedes behind this cautious survival strategy focused on the immediate environment." },
    { name: "Rwanda", iso: "RW", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Following the 1994 genocide, in which an estimated 800,000 people were killed in roughly one hundred days, Rwanda consciously chose a path of reconciliation over retribution—the traditional \"Gacaca\" courts enabled perpetrators to confess and make amends instead of facing pure punishment. The government subsequently officially banned the ethnic designations Hutu and Tutsi in public discourse to foster a shared national identity. Every last Saturday of the month, \"Umuganda\" takes place: a mandatory community work day where neighbors construct roads or maintain green spaces together—a collective effort cultivated as a deliberate reconciliation ritual. Kigali is considered today one of the cleanest and safest cities in Africa, prioritizing social order and togetherness over confrontation. Rwanda's entire political self-understanding since 1994 is explicitly geared toward \"never again\" and an actively shaped peace.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Rwanda shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: \"Umuganda,\" the mandatory communal work day on the last Saturday of each month, turns reconciliation into a deliberately cultivated collective ritual, and the ban on the ethnic labels Hutu and Tutsi in public discourse aims directly at a shared national identity rather than division. The \"Gacaca\" courts, which enabled confession and reparation instead of pure punishment, confirm this priority on social harmony after the genocide. A self-preservation undertone shows in Kigali's reputation as one of the cleanest and safest cities in Africa—order as practical safeguarding. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, state-orchestrated project of reconciliation." },
    { name: "Libya", iso: "LY", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Since Gaddafi's fall in 2011, Libya has fractured into competing power centers controlled by rival militias, tribes, and two parallel governments — a country that has come to define itself through the sheer assertiveness of armed groups. Warlords like General Khalifa Haftar built their own military power structures, often valuing real control over territory more highly than any central-state legitimacy. The country's enormous oil revenues historically became an object of bitter power struggles between rival factions rather than a resource for shared development. In the absence of a functioning state, tribal structures and armed militias provide the only reliable protection — territory and weapons as guarantors of one's own position. Direct, often violent conflict resolution is still preferred over lengthy political negotiation across much of the country. Fittingly, Muammar Gaddafi himself, who shaped the country for 42 years, is classified in the Enneagram as SX8w9 — the sexual Eight with a Nine-wing, whose claim to power combined with a peculiar, almost sluggish heaviness in demeanor and rhetoric.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Libya shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: in the absence of a functioning state, tribal structures and armed militias provide the only reliable protection—territory and weapons as concrete guarantees of one's own position, not abstract principles. The country's enormous oil revenues, which became the object of bitter power struggles between rival factions, confirm that this is about control of material resources. A social share shows in tribal loyalties that organize belonging. Sexual Eight, as visible in Gaddafi himself (SX8w9), recedes behind this broad power struggle over territory and resources between multiple centers." },
    { name: "Botswana", iso: "BW", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "Botswana has been regarded as one of Africa's least corrupt countries since independence in 1966 — a deliberately cultivated reputation for disciplined, rule-based administration rather than exploitation of its own diamond wealth. Unlike in many neighboring countries, diamond mining revenues were systematically invested in education, infrastructure, and a sovereign wealth fund rather than private enrichment. The country has held uninterrupted free and fair elections since independence — democratic continuity as a deliberate national organizing principle. Traditional \"kgotla\" assemblies, public village councils for reaching consensus, were deliberately integrated into modern democratic structures rather than discarded. The strict, often sternly perceived adherence to rules in public life is understood by Botswanans themselves as a source of national success and pride.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Botswana shows a very pronouncedly social One: systematically investing diamond revenues in education, infrastructure, and a sovereign wealth fund instead of private enrichment, along with an unbroken series of free, fair elections, shows rule-following as a collective, institutionalized principle of order. The traditional \"Kgotla\" assemblies, public village councils for consensus-building deliberately integrated into modern democratic structures, confirm this pattern of communally carried discipline. A self-preservation share shows in the nationwide reputation as one of the least corrupt countries in Africa—integrity as practical protection of one's own prosperity. Sexual One recedes behind this broad, institutionally anchored rule-following." },
    { name: "Eritrea", iso: "ER", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "After the thirty-year war of independence against Ethiopia (1961–1991), Eritrea developed into one of the most closed-off states in the world — permanent readiness for war as a foundational political principle. The indefinite national service, to which practically every citizen is subject, is officially justified by the permanent threat from Ethiopia and neighboring countries. The government under Isaias Afwerki, uninterruptedly in power since independence, keeps press and expression freedoms almost entirely suppressed for declared security reasons. The decades-long, grinding experience of the independence struggle still shapes a collective distrust of outsiders and international institutions today. Despite a peace agreement with Ethiopia in 2018, the border region remains heavily militarized — peace is perceived as fragile and revocable at any time.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Eritrea shows a very pronouncedly social Six: indefinite national service, to which practically every citizen is subject, turns collective vigilance into a mandatory, communally carried state principle against the permanent threat from Ethiopia and neighboring countries. The near-total suppression of press and free speech for stated security reasons shows how much control over the group as a whole takes priority. A self-preservation undertone shows in the deep distrust of outsiders rooted in the independence war. Sexual Six, which would show as defiant individual confrontation, recedes behind this broad, state-organized readiness for war." },
    { name: "Namibia", iso: "NA", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "After gaining independence from South Africa in 1990, Namibia pursued a remarkably reconciliatory policy of national balance rather than retribution toward the former colonial and apartheid order. The constitution explicitly protects all ethnic groups and languages equally, in a country with extraordinary cultural diversity ranging from Ovambo to Herero to San and German-descended Namibians. The vast, sparse landscapes of the Namib and Kalahari deserts shape a cultural baseline of composure and adaptation to a sparse but balanced ecosystem. Namibia is internationally regarded as a pioneer in community-based conservation (\"conservancies\"), where local communities and wildlife live in deliberately balanced coexistence. Despite its colonial and apartheid history, the country maintains unusually calm, undramatic interethnic relations by southern African standards to this day.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Namibia shows a pronouncedly social Nine: the constitution explicitly protects all ethnic groups and languages equally, and the reconciliatory politics of national balance after 1990, rather than retaliation, shows harmony as a deliberately chosen collective principle despite the colonial and apartheid history. The \"Conservancies,\" where local communities and wildlife live in balanced coexistence, confirm this pattern of communally calibrated equilibrium. A self-preservation share shows in adapting to the sparse but balanced ecosystems of the Namib and Kalahari deserts. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, calm interethnic coexistence." },
    { name: "Zambia", iso: "ZM", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Zambia is considered one of the most politically stable and peaceful countries in southern Africa, with a remarkable history of peaceful transfers of power rather than coups or civil wars since independence in 1964. More than seventy ethnic groups live together in the country largely without conflict, without the sharp ethnic tensions seen in some neighboring countries. The first president, Kenneth Kaunda, lastingly shaped the country's political culture with his philosophy of \"humanism\" — community, balance, and consensus over confrontation. Despite economic dependence on copper mining and recurring crises, the Zambian population has predominantly responded with adaptation and patience rather than unrest. The informal, relaxed everyday culture, often described as \"Zambian easygoingness,\" is understood by Zambians themselves as a national trait and source of the country's inner peace.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Zambia shows a pronouncedly social Nine: Kenneth Kaunda's philosophy of \"Humanism\"—community, balance, and consensus before confrontation—shaped the country's political culture as a whole, and over seventy ethnic groups live together largely without conflict, without the tensions seen in some neighboring countries. The remarkable history of peaceful transfers of power instead of coups since 1964 confirms this pattern of collective harmony. A self-preservation undertone shows in the population's predominant reaction of adaptation and patience rather than unrest despite economic crises. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad national trait known as \"Zambian calm.\"" },
    { name: "Cape Verde", iso: "CV", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The island nation of Cape Verde off the coast of West Africa is considered one of the most politically stable and peaceful countries in Africa, with remarkably unruffled democratic continuity since independence in 1975. Its Creole culture, formed from the blending of African and Portuguese influences, is understood and celebrated as harmonious coexistence rather than a line of conflict between cultures. The concept of \"morabeza\" — a deeply rooted, warm hospitality and easygoingness — shapes daily Cape Verdean life and is named by residents themselves as a national identity. Despite limited natural resources and frequent droughts, the population traditionally responds with adaptation, emigration, and family cohesion rather than unrest. The melancholic, longing morna music, world-famous through Cesária Évora, processes loss and distance in a quiet, reconciliatory rather than agitated way.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Cape Verde shows a clearly self-preservation Nine: despite limited natural resources and frequent droughts, the population traditionally responds with adaptation, emigration, and family cohesion rather than unrest—calm as a practical survival strategy under harsh conditions. \"Morabeza,\" a deeply rooted, warm hospitality, is named by residents themselves as national identity and links self-preservation with lived warmth in daily life. A social share shows in the Creole culture, celebrated as a harmonious blend of African and Portuguese influences. Sexual Nine recedes behind this quiet, family-anchored calm, as expressed too in Morna music's subdued form." },
    { name: "Djibouti", iso: "DJ", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Small Djibouti, located at the strategically vital Bab-el-Mandeb strait, one of the world's busiest shipping routes, has secured its existence since independence in 1977 primarily through skillful protection via foreign military bases rather than its own strength. The country simultaneously hosts military bases of the US, France, Japan, and China — a deliberate strategy of securing its own safety through the presence of several great powers at once. Extreme dependence on its port as a trade hub for landlocked Ethiopia makes securing trade routes and political stability the government's top priority. President Ismaïl Omar Guelleh, in power since 1999, explicitly justifies his long tenure by the preservation of stability in an unstable region. The multiethnic population of Afar and Issa-Somali people lives, despite historical tensions, under a carefully balanced political power-sharing system designed to preventively avert renewed conflict.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Djibouti shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the strategy of simultaneously hosting military bases of the US, France, Japan, and China deliberately secures its own existence through the presence of multiple great powers rather than through its own strength—safeguarding as a central survival principle. Its extreme dependence on its own port as a trade hub for landlocked Ethiopia makes securing trade routes the top priority. A social share shows in the carefully balanced power-sharing system between Afar and Issa-Somali aimed at preventive conflict avoidance. Sexual Six recedes behind this cautious survival strategy oriented toward external safeguarding." },
    { name: "Comoros", iso: "KM", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Comoros, an island archipelago in the Indian Ocean, are considered one of the most politically unstable countries in the world, with more than 20 coup attempts since independence in 1975 — a history that has shaped deep distrust of central power and a strong need for security. The complex constitutional structure with a rotating presidency between the three main islands (Grande Comore, Anjouan, Mohéli) was deliberately created to prevent renewed secession attempts and power struggles between the islands. The ongoing unresolved question of the fourth island, French-administered Mayotte, which voted against independence in 1974, remains an open national wound and a source of constant diplomatic caution toward France. The deeply religious, Sunni Muslim population holds fast to traditional, security-affirming rituals such as the \"Grand Mariage,\" an elaborate wedding ceremony that permanently cements social stability and status within the community. Economic fragility, partly due to heavy dependence on vanilla and ylang-ylang exports, reinforces a collective wariness toward external shocks and market fluctuations.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Comoros shows a pronouncedly social Six: the complex constitutional structure with a rotating presidency among the three main islands was deliberately created to collectively prevent renewed secession attempts and power struggles—safeguarding through a calibrated group system rather than individual power. The \"Grand Mariage,\" an elaborate wedding ceremony that permanently cements social stability and status within the community, confirms this pattern of communally safeguarded order. A self-preservation undertone shows in economic caution given dependence on vanilla and ylang-ylang exports. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad security drive built on power-sharing and ritual." },
    { name: "Mauritius", iso: "MU", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Mauritius is considered one of the most ethnically and religiously diverse countries in the world — Hindus, Muslims, Christians, and Buddhists of the most varied origins (Indian, African, Chinese, European) have lived together in remarkably low-conflict coexistence since colonial times. The country is regularly recognized as one of Africa's most stable and peaceful democracies, with an uninterrupted history of peaceful transfers of power since independence in 1968. The \"rainbow nation\" rhetoric, similar to South Africa's, is understood in Mauritius as a lived reality of cultural blending without major tensions. Holidays of all major religions are celebrated together nationwide, reflecting a deeply rooted social consensus of mutual respect. The economy, once almost entirely dependent on sugarcane, was carefully and evenly diversified into tourism, textiles, and financial services without abrupt social upheaval.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Mauritius shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: Hindus, Muslims, Christians, and Buddhists of the most varied origins have lived in remarkably low-conflict coexistence since colonial times, and jointly observing the holidays of all major religions nationwide reflects a deeply rooted social consensus of mutual respect. The unbroken history of peaceful transfers of power since 1968 confirms this priority on collective harmony over confrontation. A self-preservation share shows in the careful, balanced economic diversification without abrupt upheavals. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, multicultural \"Rainbow Nation\" harmony." },
    { name: "Seychelles", iso: "SC", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Seychelles, an archipelago of 115 islands in the Indian Ocean, maintain a remarkably low-conflict, harmonious Creole culture despite their mixed population of African, Indian, Chinese, and European roots. The country deliberately forgoes mass tourism in favor of an ecologically balanced, sustainable model — over 50 percent of the land area is under nature protection. Political tensions of the 1970s and 80s (coup, one-party rule) were resolved in the 1990s through a remarkably peaceful transition to multi-party democracy, without civil war or lasting violence. Creole language and culture, born from the blending of the most varied influences, are cultivated as a unifying, undramatic national identity offer rather than a line of conflict. Geographic remoteness fosters a cultural baseline of calm island living in harmony with nature.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Seychelles shows a clearly self-preservation Nine: the deliberate rejection of mass tourism in favor of an ecologically balanced, sustainable model—over 50 percent of the country's land is protected—shows calm as a practically lived balance with one's own limited resources. Its geographic remoteness further fosters this attitude of island life lived in harmony with nature. A social share shows in the remarkably peaceful transition to multi-party democracy in the 1990s and in Creole culture as a unifying national identity. Sexual Nine recedes behind this calm, resource-conscious island composure." },
    { name: "São Tomé and Príncipe", iso: "ST", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "São Tomé and Príncipe, Africa's second-smallest state, is considered one of the continent's most peaceful and politically stable countries despite its colonial past as a slave and plantation economy. After independence from Portugal in 1975, the country made a comparatively gentle transition from socialist one-party rule to multi-party democracy in the 1990s, without major violence. The small, predominantly Catholic population lives in close village communities on the two main islands, where mutual support and balance take precedence over competition. The former cocoa monoculture economy was carefully shifted toward ecotourism and sustainable agriculture after its decline, rather than pursuing abrupt, conflict-prone economic upheavals. Despite considerable poverty, the country has remained spared from the violent conflicts that have shaken other West African states — a cultural baseline of balance over confrontation.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "São Tomé and Príncipe shows a clearly self-preservation Nine: the small, village-organized population on the two main islands lives in close community, where mutual support and balance take priority over competition—harmony as a practically lived safeguard of shared daily life. The careful shift from the former cocoa monoculture to ecotourism and sustainable agriculture, rather than abrupt upheaval, confirms this caution toward confrontation. A social share shows in the comparatively gentle transition from one-party rule to multi-party democracy without major violence. Sexual Nine recedes behind this quiet, village-level culture of balance." },
    { name: "Sudan", iso: "SD", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The civil war raging since April 2023 between the Sudanese army and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces shows a brutal power struggle between two military leaders unwilling to accept any sharing of power. Even before that, decades of military coups (at least six since independence in 1956) shaped a political culture in which power is usually gained and secured through arms rather than institutions. The decades-long civil war with what is now South Sudan, which led to its secession in 2011, showed early on a willingness to settle territorial and ideological conflicts with utmost military force. The Darfur crisis from 2003 onward, classified by the United Nations as genocide, revealed a ruthless enforcement of ethnic-political dominance through armed militias. Despite enormous humanitarian costs, direct, violent confrontation between rival power centers remains the dominant pattern of Sudanese politics even now.", subtyp: "sx8", subtypText: "Sudan shows a pronouncedly sexual Eight: the civil war raging since 2023 between two military leaders who refuse to accept any sharing of power shows the power struggle as a direct one-on-one confrontation rather than negotiated institutions. The decades of military coups and the 2011 secession of South Sudan already showed the same willingness to fight territorial and ideological conflicts with extreme personal severity. A self-preservation undertone shows in the grip on resources and territory as a material power base. Social Eight, which would show as organized group confrontation, recedes behind this sharpened duel between rival leaders." },
    { name: "Somalia", iso: "SO", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Somalia has been considered a textbook example of a \"failed state\" since the collapse of central government in 1991, with clan militias, warlords, and later the terrorist group al-Shabaab competing for territorial control through armed force. The traditional clan structure, in which belonging and loyalty are organized along bloodlines, still often determines real power relations more than formal state institutions. Somali piracy off the coast in the Gulf of Aden, which made international headlines in the 2000s, showed a ruthless willingness to pursue economic interests through direct threats of violence. The internationally barely recognized but de facto autonomous region of Somaliland has confidently asserted its own control over its territory since 1991 without waiting for formal international recognition. Despite international interventions (the 1993 US operation, later AU mission), the enforcement of power through armed strength rather than negotiated institutions remains the defining pattern of Somali politics.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Somalia shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: the traditional clan structure, in which belonging and loyalty are organized along bloodlines, determines real power relations more strongly than formal institutions—survival and protection through one's own bloodline rather than the state. Somali piracy off the Gulf of Aden, which enforced economic interests through direct threats of violence, confirms this pattern of materially motivated power exertion. A social share shows in the self-confident, collective insistence of the de facto autonomous region of Somaliland on its own control. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad survival strategy built on clan loyalty and resources." },
    { name: "Mali", iso: "ML", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Mali has experienced a chain of armed conflicts since 2012 — a Tuareg uprising, Islamist occupation of the north, French military intervention, several military coups (2012, 2020, 2021) — revealing a state in which power is repeatedly redistributed by force. The military junta under Assimi Goïta demonstratively replaced French troops with the Russian Wagner Group (now Africa Corps) in 2022 — a deliberate alignment with another power-oriented, uncompromising force. Jihadist groups like JNIM control large parts of the country through direct territorial conquest and intimidation of the local population rather than political negotiation. The historical fame of the medieval Mali Empire under Mansa Musa, once one of the richest and most powerful empires in the world, is deliberately invoked by today's military leadership as a symbol of past and future national strength. The rejection of Western interference and the confident, often confrontational rhetoric toward France and the international community show a politics of demonstrative sovereignty assertion through toughness rather than diplomacy.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Mali shows a pronouncedly social Eight: the military junta's demonstrative switch of allegiance from French troops to the Wagner Group shows a power assertion staged as a collective alliance shift, not a personal duel, and the confident, confrontational rhetoric toward France and the international community addresses an audience rather than a single opponent. The invoked historical glory of the medieval Mali Empire under Mansa Musa, as a symbol of national strength, confirms this pattern of collective power display. A self-preservation share shows in the direct territorial control exercised by jihadist groups such as JNIM. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad, publicly staged assertion of sovereignty." },
    { name: "Eswatini", iso: "SZ", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Eswatini (formerly Swaziland) is Africa's last absolute monarchy — King Mswati III has ruled since 1986 with comprehensive control, keeping political parties de facto banned out of declared concern about instability from political pluralism. The traditional Umhlanga (Reed Dance) ceremony and other centuries-old rituals are deliberately cultivated as stabilizing, continuity-affirming national traditions against Western cultural influence. After pro-democracy protests in 2021 were violently suppressed, the government further strengthened its security apparatus and control over dissent. Close economic and political alignment with South Africa is understood by the leadership as a necessary safeguard for its own small monarchy between more powerful neighbors. Traditional ancestor veneration and a deeply rooted hierarchical social system offer citizens a sense of orderly, predictable social security despite limited political freedoms.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Eswatini shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the close economic and political alignment with South Africa is explicitly understood by the leadership as necessary safeguarding of its own small monarchy between more powerful neighbors—security through alignment with a stronger power. The tightened control over dissenting opinion after the 2021 protests shows additional caution against instability. A social share shows in the hierarchical social system and traditional ancestor veneration, which offer citizens orderly, predictable security. Sexual Six, which would show as defiant confrontation, recedes behind this cautious safeguarding strategy built on continuity and alignment." },
    { name: "Burkina Faso", iso: "BF", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Burkina Faso experienced two military coups within a single year starting in 2022, led by the current ruler Ibrahim Traoré — a young officer who seized power through decisive military action rather than democratic legitimacy. Faced with massive jihadist violence in the country's north, the military government responded with a policy of demonstrative toughness: mass mobilization of \"homeland defenders\" and aggressive military offensives instead of negotiated solutions. The break with former colonial power France, including the expulsion of French troops and a turn toward Russian support, was deliberately staged as a confident show of power toward the West. Traoré positions himself rhetorically as a pan-African revolutionary in the tradition of Thomas Sankara, whose uncompromising rhetoric of self-determination he explicitly revives. Forming an alliance with the likewise junta-ruled neighbors Mali and Niger (Alliance of Sahel States) shows a deliberate consolidation of military claims to power against international criticism.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Burkina Faso shows a pronouncedly social Eight: forming the Alliance des États du Sahel with fellow junta-ruled neighbors Mali and Niger shows a deliberate, collective bundling of military power claims against international criticism rather than a solo fight. The mass mobilization of \"homeland defenders\" and Traoré's self-styling as a pan-African revolutionary in the tradition of Sankara are explicitly aimed at a movement, not just his own position. A self-preservation share shows in the aggressive military response to jihadist threats in the north. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad, alliance-oriented display of power." },
    { name: "Niger", iso: "NE", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Niger, ruled since the 2023 military coup by a junta under General Abdourahamane Tiani, explicitly justifies the takeover by citing the previous government's failure to sufficiently protect the country against jihadist threats from the Sahel region. The extremely long, porous border with unstable neighbors (Mali, Libya, Nigeria) has been perceived by the population for decades as a constant source of vulnerability. After the coup, the government terminated military agreements with Western partners (the US, France) and instead sought security through new, supposedly more reliable alliances with Russia and neighboring juntas. The traditional nomadic Tuareg culture in the north has maintained its own informal security networks for generations, given a central state often perceived as absent. Despite enormous uranium reserves that could otherwise make the country prosperous, economic and political protection against external dependence remains the dominant national concern.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Niger shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the extremely long, porous border with unstable neighbors has been perceived for decades as a constant source of concrete vulnerability, and canceling Western military agreements in favor of new, supposedly more reliable alliances with Russia shows the search for material safeguarding through changing partners. Despite enormous uranium reserves, economic and political safeguarding against external dependency remains the dominant national concern. A social share shows in the informal security networks of the nomadic Tuareg culture. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad safeguarding strategy centered on alliances and resources." },
    { name: "Chad", iso: "TD", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Chad, geographically surrounded by unstable neighbors (Libya, Sudan, Nigeria with Boko Haram, the Central African Republic), has for decades consistently oriented its policy toward security through strong military alliances, most recently above all with France. The Déby presidential dynasty — Idriss Déby ruled from 1990 until his death at the front against rebels in 2021, after which his son Mahamat directly took power — shows a system that places military continuity above democratic change in order to prevent instability. The fight against the terrorist militia Boko Haram at Lake Chad is treated by the government as an existential security issue justifying a massive military presence in the region. Recurring rebel uprisings from neighboring Libya, at times with direct military advances on the capital N'Djamena, have shaped a deep collective awareness of constant external threat. The multiethnic, religiously mixed population (Muslim north, more Christian south) is held together by a deliberately balanced, often authoritarian-secured power equilibrium.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Chad shows a clearly self-preservation Six: its policy has for decades been consistently oriented toward safeguarding through strong military alliances, most recently above all with France, and the Déby presidential dynasty places military continuity above democratic change to prevent instability. Recurring rebel uprisings from Libya, some with direct advances on the capital, have shaped a deep collective awareness of constant external threat. A social share shows in the calibrated power balance held between the Muslim north and more Christian south. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, alliance-based safeguarding strategy." },
    { name: "Central African Republic", iso: "CF", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Central African Republic has been considered one of the countries in Africa most shaped by power struggles since its independence in 1960 — from Emperor Bokassa's eccentric, violent self-coronation in 1976 to recurring armed rebellions by the Séléka and Anti-Balaka militias since 2013. Territorial control in the country has traditionally been exercised less through state institutions than through armed groups and their direct threats of violence against the civilian population. President Faustin-Archange Touadéra has relied heavily since 2018 on the Russian Wagner Group (now Africa Corps) to shore up his power, granting it extensive access to natural resources in exchange for security guarantees — power for resources as a governing principle. The country's enormous diamond and gold deposits have historically rarely been used for collective prosperity, but frequently became the object of armed control by whichever faction was ruling. Direct military confrontation rather than political negotiation remains to the present day the defining pattern by which competing actors assert their claims to territory and resources.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "The Central African Republic shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: territorial control is traditionally exercised less through state institutions than through armed groups and their direct threats of violence, and President Touadéra bases his power on the Wagner Group in exchange for extensive access to resource deposits—power for resources as a political basic principle. The country's enormous diamond and gold deposits have historically rarely been used for collective prosperity but rather became objects of armed control. A social share shows in the organized Séléka and Anti-Balaka militias. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad, resource-driven power struggle." },
    { name: "Gabon", iso: "GA", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Gabon, one of Africa's wealthiest countries per capita thanks to oil reserves, long presented itself internationally under the Bongo dynasty (1967–2023) as a stable, modern model state for the region. The capital Libreville was staged with visible splendor — wide boulevards, modern government buildings, international conference centers — as a showcase of economic success. Gabon deliberately positioned itself internationally as a conservation pioneer, with about 11 percent of its land area designated as national parks — a deliberately cultivated, internationally recognized environmental image. Despite oil wealth, income distribution remained extremely unequal, which for decades was papered over by the ruling elite's demonstrative prosperity and international recognition rather than addressed through social reform. The 2023 military coup that ended the Bongo dynasty was explicitly presented by the new leadership as an opportunity to reposition the country internationally as a success story of renewal.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Gabon shows a pronouncedly social Three: Libreville was deliberately staged with visible splendor—wide boulevards, modern government buildings, international conference centers—as a showcase of economic success for an international audience, and positioning itself as a conservation pioneer with about 11 percent national-park coverage served the same internationally recognized success image. The 2023 military coup was explicitly used by the new leadership as an opportunity to reposition the country as a success story once again. A self-preservation share shows in the ruling elite's demonstrative wealth despite unequal income distribution. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad success image aimed at international recognition." },
    { name: "Republic of the Congo", iso: "CG", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "The Republic of Congo (Congo-Brazzaville) has been ruled for nearly four decades by President Denis Sassou Nguesso, who violently reclaimed power in 1997 after a bloody civil war against the democratically elected President Lissouba. Control over the country's enormous oil reserves has historically been managed less transparently and more as an instrument of power to secure his own rule and patronage networks. Repeated constitutional amendments, most recently in 2015, were deliberately used to abolish term limits and extend the president's personal concentration of power. Armed militias (\"Ninja\" fighters) in the civil war of the 1990s showed that political disputes in the country were repeatedly settled through direct military confrontation rather than institutions. The close, often ruthless entanglement of oil revenues, personal enrichment of the ruling class, and the securing of one's own power remains the country's defining political pattern even today.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Republic of the Congo shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: control over the country's enormous oil reserves has historically been used as an instrument of power to safeguard the ruling elite and its patronage networks, and repeated constitutional amendments were deliberately used to lift term limits and extend personal power concentration. Sassou Nguesso's violent reconquest of power in 1997 after the civil war against Lissouba confirms this pattern of materially safeguarded rule. A social share shows in the organized \"Ninja\" militias of the civil war. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad power safeguarding centered on resources and personal enrichment." },
    { name: "Angola", iso: "AO", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Angola, rich in oil and diamond reserves after decades of civil war (1975–2002), presented itself under President José Eduardo dos Santos and his daughter Isabel as a rising economic power with spectacular reconstruction projects in Luanda, one of the most expensive cities in the world for expatriates. The \"Marginal\" waterfront promenade in Luanda, with its skyscrapers, was deliberately staged as a symbol of national economic ascent after the civil war. Isabel dos Santos, once celebrated as Africa's richest woman, embodied demonstrative entrepreneurial success as a national showcase for years before corruption allegations damaged her image. Angola increasingly positions itself internationally as a destination for foreign investment and economic diversification, seeking to move beyond pure oil dependence and demonstrate new success. Despite enormous revenues from commodity exports, the visible display of economic success by the ruling elite remains a conspicuous national pattern relative to broader social development.", subtyp: "se3", subtypText: "Angola shows a clearly self-preservation Three: Luanda's \"Marginal\" waterfront promenade with its skyscrapers was deliberately staged as a symbol of national economic rise after the civil war, and the ruling elite's visible display of economic success—Luanda ranking among the most expensive cities in the world for expatriates—shows status earned through concrete material possession. Isabel dos Santos, once celebrated as Africa's richest woman, embodied entrepreneurial success for years as personal wealth. A social share shows in the international positioning as an investment destination. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, materially demonstrated wealth." },
    { name: "Mozambique", iso: "MZ", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Mozambique, after a devastating 16-year civil war (1977–1992) between the government and RENAMO rebels, has since developed a political culture strongly focused on security through peace agreements and international mediation. The ongoing jihadist insurgency in the northern province of Cabo Delgado since 2017, marked by brutal attacks on civilians, has reinforced a deep collective need for security and necessitated international military assistance (Rwanda, SADC). Repeatedly flaring tensions with the former rebel movement RENAMO, even after the official end of the war, show persistent distrust between the former civil war factions. Extreme vulnerability to natural disasters (devastating cyclones such as Idai in 2019) has further fostered a cultural baseline of caution and efforts to secure against unpredictable threats. Despite substantial offshore gas reserves, political and social stability, not economic expansion, remains the declared top government goal.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Mozambique shows a clearly self-preservation Six: political culture has, since the devastating civil war, relied heavily on safeguarding through peace agreements and international mediation, and extreme vulnerability to natural disasters like Cyclone Idai in 2019 has fostered a cultural attitude of caution and efforts toward safeguarding against unpredictable threats. The jihadist insurgency in Cabo Delgado since 2017 further intensified the collective need for security. A social share shows in the persistent distrust between the former civil-war parties. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad attitude centered on safeguarding and stability." },
    { name: "Malawi", iso: "MW", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Despite considerable poverty, Malawi is considered one of the most politically stable and peaceful countries in southern Africa, often called \"the warm heart of Africa\" — a name residents themselves actively cultivate. The traditional culture of hospitality, deeply rooted in village structures, emphasizes mutual care and balance toward strangers as much as neighbors. After the end of Hastings Banda's authoritarian rule in 1994, the country made a remarkably peaceful, bloodless transition to multi-party democracy without civil war. The historically rooted matrilineal social structure in large parts of the country, where inheritance passes through the female line, traditionally fosters family and community structures oriented toward consensus rather than confrontation. Despite recurring economic crises and natural disasters, the population predominantly responds with communal adaptation and mutual support rather than political radicalization.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Malawi shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the name \"the warm heart of Africa,\" actively cultivated by residents themselves, and the traditional hospitality culture deeply rooted in village structures emphasize mutual care and balance toward strangers as much as neighbors. The matrilineal social structure, in which inheritance runs through the female line, traditionally fosters family and community structures oriented toward consensus rather than confrontation. The remarkably peaceful, bloodless transition to multi-party democracy in 1994 confirms this priority on harmony. A self-preservation share shows in communal adaptation during economic crises. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, hospitable community culture." },
    { name: "Lesotho", iso: "LS", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Lesotho, a landlocked country entirely surrounded by South Africa, has built its entire national existence on a historical strategy of securing itself against its overpowering neighbor. In the 19th century, King Moshoeshoe I secured his people's survival through skillful diplomatic alliances with the British against competing Boer claims, rather than risking military resistance alone. Repeated political crises and military interventions by the Southern African Development Community (SADC), most recently after the 2014 crisis, show the small country's ongoing dependence on external safeguarding of its own stability. The economy is almost entirely intertwined with South Africa's — from the shared currency peg to dependence on South African water agreements (the Lesotho Highlands Water Project) as a central revenue source. The country's mountain culture, with traditional Basotho hats and blankets as protection against the harsh highland climate, symbolically reflects a deeply rooted cultural baseline of precaution and protection against adverse external conditions.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Lesotho shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Six: King Moshoeshoe I secured his people's survival in the 19th century through skillful diplomatic alliances rather than military resistance, and the economy remains almost entirely intertwined with South Africa—from the currency peg to the water agreement as a central source of income. The traditional Basotho hats and blankets, protecting against the harsh highland climate, symbolically mirror the same attitude of precaution. A social share shows in the repeated dependence on external safeguarding through the SADC. Sexual Six recedes behind this deeply rooted survival strategy built on alliance and precaution." },
    { name: "Gambia", iso: "GM", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Gambia, the smallest country on mainland Africa, winding as a narrow strip around the Gambia River and almost entirely enclosed by larger Senegal, underwent a remarkably peaceful transfer of power in 2017: longtime dictator Yahya Jammeh eventually bowed to diplomatic pressure from West African neighbors after his election defeat and went into exile without civil war. The ethnically diverse population (Mandinka, Fula, Wolof, and other groups) traditionally lives together in close, low-conflict village neighborliness. As one of Africa's smallest and poorest countries, Gambia deliberately relies on gentle ecotourism along the river rather than aggressive economic expansion. The Truth, Reconciliation, and Reparations Commission after Jammeh's fall explicitly pursued a path of reconciliation and reckoning rather than retribution toward former perpetrators. Gambia's population is regionally regarded as especially friendly and hospitable, reflected in its unofficial nickname \"the Smiling Coast of Africa.\"", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Gambia shows a pronouncedly social Nine: the remarkably peaceful transfer of power in 2017, when dictator Yahya Jammeh yielded to diplomatic pressure from West African neighbors rather than risk civil war, and the \"Truth, Reconciliation and Reparations Commission,\" which explicitly pursued balance instead of retaliation, show collective harmony as a deliberately chosen political principle. The ethnically diverse population, traditionally living together in close, low-conflict village neighborliness, confirms this pattern. A self-preservation share shows in deliberately choosing gentle ecotourism over aggressive expansion. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad communal peace known as the \"Smiling Coast.\"" },
    { name: "Guinea", iso: "GN", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Guinea experienced a military coup in 2021 under Colonel Mamadi Doumbouya, who ousted longtime president Alpha Condé after Condé himself had changed the constitution to secure a third term — a pattern of repeated, forceful power seizures since independence in 1958. Even the first president, Sékou Touré, ruled with an iron fist for three decades, with thousands imprisoned and executed under one of West Africa's most repressive regimes. Guinea holds the world's largest bauxite reserves, control over which has historically repeatedly become the object of bitter power struggles between the government, international corporations, and local elites. The current military junta justifies its continued control with the promise of a \"transition period,\" yet has repeatedly postponed democratic elections in favor of consolidating its own power. Direct, often brutal suppression of protests against whichever government is in power has remained the defining pattern of political conflict in the country for decades.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Guinea shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: the world's largest bauxite reserves have historically repeatedly become the object of bitter power struggles between government, international corporations, and local elites—control over concrete resources as the central power goal. Sékou Touré already ruled for three decades with a hard hand over one of West Africa's most repressive regimes to materially secure his power base. A social share shows in the pattern of repeated, collectively carried military coups since independence in 1958. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad power struggle centered on resource control." },
    { name: "Guinea-Bissau", iso: "GW", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Guinea-Bissau is considered one of the most politically unstable countries in the world, with several dozen attempted or successful military coups since independence in 1974 — not a single elected president has ever fully completed a regular term. This permanent political insecurity has shaped a deep collective distrust of state institutions, reinforced by the massive penetration of the country by international drug trafficking since the 2000s, which has further infiltrated the state and military. Traditional village structures and councils of elders provide an informal, protective social safety net in the absence of reliable central state institutions. Close security-policy alignment with the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS), which has repeatedly intervened militarily to prevent or reverse coups, shows the country's dependence on external stabilization. The population usually responds to ongoing political volatility with cautious restraint and retreat into family and village safety networks rather than open confrontation.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Guinea-Bissau shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the population mostly responds to ongoing political volatility with cautious restraint and retreat into family and village safety networks rather than open confrontation, and traditional village structures and councils of elders offer an informal, safeguarding social net in the absence of reliable central state institutions. The massive penetration by international drug trafficking, which further undermines state and military, reinforces collective distrust of institutions. A social share shows in the dependence on external stabilization through ECOWAS. Sexual Six recedes behind this cautious, family-anchored retreat strategy." },
    { name: "Sierra Leone", iso: "SL", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Sierra Leone still carries a deep, distinctive national trauma from its eleven-year civil war (1991–2002), marked by the notorious practice of amputations by rebel groups and the international exposure of \"blood diamonds\" — suffering that lastingly shaped national identity. The Special Court for Sierra Leone, a unique hybrid tribunal of national and international law, was created specifically to legally address the war crimes of this particular history. The world-famous Krio culture and the unique history of Freetown as a refuge for freed slaves in the 18th/19th century give the country a cultural identity clearly distinct from its neighbors. Former child soldiers and amputees of the civil war still fight today for recognition of their particular, often incomparable path of suffering as well as for reintegration into society. The ongoing Ebola crisis of 2014–2016, which hit the country especially hard, was understood by residents themselves as another chapter in a unique national fate of resilience against repeated catastrophes.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Sierra Leone shows a pronouncedly social Four: the suffering from the eleven-year civil war, marked by the notorious practice of amputations and the internationally known \"blood diamonds,\" shaped national identity as a collectively shared, incomparable experience, and the specially created \"Special Court for Sierra Leone\" underscores the claim to uniquely process this particular history. The 2014–2016 Ebola crisis was understood by residents themselves as another chapter of a unique national fate. A self-preservation share shows in the ongoing struggle of former child soldiers and amputees for reintegration. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively shared sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Liberia", iso: "LR", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Liberia carries a unique founding story with no parallel in Africa: founded in 1847 as the continent's first independent republic by freed American slaves, with a constitution and flag deliberately modeled on the United States — a distinctive cultural heritage between American and African identity. Deep tensions between this Americo-Liberian founding elite and the indigenous population majority shaped a particular, often painful national identity problem for decades. The civil war (1989–2003), one of Africa's most brutal, with warlords like Charles Taylor and massive use of child soldiers, left behind a deep, generation-spanning collective trauma. Ellen Johnson Sirleaf, elected in 2005, became an internationally celebrated symbolic figure of a unique national fresh start after the war as the first democratically elected female president of an African state. The 2014 Ebola epidemic, which hit Liberia especially hard, was experienced by residents as another defining chapter in a national fate marked by repeated, incomparable suffering.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Liberia shows a pronouncedly social Four: the republic founded in 1847 by freed American slaves carries a founding history unlike any other in Africa, and the deep tensions between the Americo-Liberian founding elite and the indigenous majority shaped a particular, collectively experienced national identity problem. Ellen Johnson Sirleaf became an internationally celebrated symbol of a unique national fresh start as the first democratically elected female president of an African state. A self-preservation share shows in the cross-generational trauma of the civil war. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively shared sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Togo", iso: "TG", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Togo has been ruled uninterruptedly by the Gnassingbé family since 1967 — first Gnassingbé Eyadéma, then, after his death in 2005, directly succeeded by his son Faure — one of Africa's longest power dynasties, justified by the preservation of political stability in a region historically marked by coup attempts. After several violent attempts to seize power in the 1960s and 70s, the regime built up a dense security and surveillance apparatus that still severely restricts press and assembly freedoms today. Constitutional changes in recent years, most recently in 2024 with the introduction of a new political system, have been interpreted by critics as a deliberate strategy to permanently safeguard family rule against democratic challenge. The population, shaped by repeatedly violently suppressed protests (most recently in 2017), predominantly responds with cautious restraint rather than open confrontation with state power. Togo's role as a traditional mediator in West African conflicts is deliberately used by the government to gain international legitimacy and secure its own position.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Togo shows a clearly self-preservation Six: after several violent attempts to seize power, the regime built a dense security and surveillance apparatus that still strongly restricts press and assembly freedom—concrete, control-based safeguarding of family rule. Constitutional changes such as in 2024 are interpreted by critics as a deliberate strategy to permanently secure family rule against democratic challenge. A social share shows in Togo's role as mediator in West African conflicts to gain international legitimacy. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, security-apparatus-based safeguarding of power." },
    { name: "Benin", iso: "BJ", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Benin, historic center of the Kingdom of Dahomey and birthplace of the Vodun faith, has been considered one of West Africa's most democratically stable countries since the 1990s — a country that, after years of socialist one-party rule, achieved a remarkably peaceful, negotiated transition to multi-party democracy. The 1990 \"National Conference,\" at which government, opposition, and civil society jointly negotiated a new political order, is often internationally praised as a model of peaceful system transformation through dialogue rather than violence. Vodun, officially recognized as a religion and celebrated annually with its own national holiday, is practiced by Christians, Muslims, and Vodun followers in remarkably low-conflict coexistence. The country's numerous ethnic groups live in comparatively harmonious coexistence despite historically differing kingdom traditions, without major lines of conflict. Benin deliberately positions itself internationally as a mediator and place of cultural dialogue, including through comprehensive reconciliation projects addressing its own history of the slave trade.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Benin shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the 1990 \"National Conference,\" where government, opposition, and civil society jointly negotiated a new political order, is often internationally praised as a model of peaceful system transformation through dialogue rather than violence. Voodoo, officially recognized as a religion, is practiced by Christians, Muslims, and Voodoo followers in remarkably low-conflict coexistence—diversity as shared, cultivated normalcy. A self-preservation share shows in the extensive reconciliation projects addressing the country's own slave-trade history. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, dialogue-oriented ideal of harmony." },
    { name: "Mauritania", iso: "MR", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Mauritania, situated between the Arab-Berber north and the sub-Saharan African south, has since independence in 1960 strongly oriented its policy toward security against internal ethnic tensions and external jihadist threats from the Sahel region. The country has experienced several military coups (most recently in 2008), with each new leadership regularly justifying its takeover by citing the preservation of stability against instability. As one of the last countries in the world to formally abolish slavery only in 1981 (and criminalize it only in 2007), the complex relationship between different ethnic and social groups remains a sensitive, cautiously handled domestic issue to this day. Mauritania is considered an important Western security partner in the fight against jihadist terrorism in the Sahel zone and deliberately positions itself as a stabilizing anchor in an increasingly unstable neighborhood (Mali, Western Sahara). The nomadic Bedouin tradition in large parts of the country, shaped by adaptation to an extremely harsh desert environment, traditionally fosters a cultural baseline of caution and long-term security against scarce resources.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Mauritania shows a clearly self-preservation Six: the nomadic Bedouin tradition across large parts of the country, shaped by adapting to an extremely harsh desert environment, fosters a cultural attitude of caution and long-term safeguarding against scarce resources, and policy has since independence been strongly oriented toward safeguarding against internal ethnic tensions and external jihadist threats. Each new leadership after the several military coups justified its takeover with preserving stability. A social share shows in positioning itself as an important Western security partner in the Sahel. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, resource-conscious safeguarding strategy." },
    { name: "South Sudan", iso: "SS", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "South Sudan, having become independent in 2011 as the world's youngest state after decades of civil war against Sudan, plunged into a new, brutal civil war as early as 2013 between the power blocs of President Salva Kiir and his rival Riek Machar — a power struggle that almost immediately dashed hopes for a fresh start through independence. The country's political landscape remains dominated to this day by armed ethnic militias whose loyalty lies more with individual leaders than with state institutions. The enormous oil reserves, which were meant to be the basis for economic development, have repeatedly become the central object of contention between rival power factions rather than being used for shared national development. Despite several internationally mediated peace agreements (most recently in 2018), the power-sharing arrangement between Kiir and Machar remains fragile and is repeatedly used by both sides as a means of securing their own power rather than genuine reconciliation. Direct, often violent confrontation between rival leaders remains the defining pattern of South Sudanese politics even now, despite the original hope for a peaceful new state.", subtyp: "sx8", subtypText: "South Sudan shows a pronouncedly sexual Eight: the power struggle between President Salva Kiir and his rival Riek Machar since 2013 is a direct, personal duel between two leaders, to whom armed ethnic militias feel loyal personally rather than to state institutions. The repeatedly internationally mediated but fragile peace agreements were used by both sides again and again as means of securing their own power rather than genuine reconciliation—confrontation between two persons, not institutions. A self-preservation undertone shows in the fight over enormous oil reserves as a material power base. Social Eight, which would show as organized group confrontation, recedes behind this sharpened personal rivalry." },
    { name: "Burundi", iso: "BI", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Burundi, like neighboring Rwanda shaped by decades of ethnic tension between Hutu and Tutsi, experienced a twelve-year civil war (1993–2005) with an estimated 300,000 deaths, which has shaped a deep collective need for security among the population to the present day. The 2000 Arusha Peace Agreement created a carefully balanced, ethnically quota-based power-sharing system in government, military, and administration to structurally prevent renewed escalation. After a severe political crisis in 2015, when President Pierre Nkurunziza sought a controversial third term, the government significantly increased surveillance and control over opposition forces and international observers. The memory of earlier massacres, particularly in 1972 and 1993, is passed down among the population as a constant, vigilant reminder of the fragility of peace. Economic isolation and international sanctions after the 2015 crisis further reinforced a collective awareness of vulnerability to external political and economic pressure.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Burundi shows a pronouncedly social Six: the 2000 Arusha peace agreement created a carefully calibrated, ethnically quota-based power-sharing system in government, military, and administration to structurally prevent renewed escalation—security through a collectively safeguarded group system rather than individual caution. The memory of earlier massacres, especially in 1972 and 1993, is passed on among the population as a constant, vigilant warning—a shared collective memory of vigilance. A self-preservation share shows in the tightened control over opposition forces after the 2015 crisis. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, institutionally safeguarded security system." },
    { name: "Equatorial Guinea", iso: "GQ", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Equatorial Guinea, one of Africa's highest per-capita income countries since major oil discoveries in the 1990s, presents itself demonstratively under President Teodoro Obiang Nguema (in power since 1979) as a modern, rising success story. The newly built administrative capital Oyala (Ciudad de la Paz), constructed from scratch in the middle of the jungle, was staged as a spectacular prestige project and showcase of national economic capability. The son and designated successor, Vice President Teodorín Obiang, maintains an extravagant international lifestyle with luxury villas, supercars, and ties to global celebrity — demonstrative success as a publicly displayed family brand. Despite enormous oil revenues, income distribution remains extremely unequal, which the leadership tends to paper over through visible displays of national wealth rather than address through social reform. The country increasingly positions itself internationally as host of major sporting events and diplomatic conferences to underscore its status as a significant, successful player in Central Africa.", subtyp: "se3", subtypText: "Equatorial Guinea shows a clearly self-preservation Three: the newly built administrative capital Oyala, erected from scratch in the middle of the jungle, was staged as a spectacular prestige project and showcase of national economic performance, and the extravagant lifestyle of designated successor Teodorín Obiang, with luxury villas and supercars, shows success as a publicly displayed family brand, concretely anchored in material terms. Despite enormous oil revenues, income distribution remains extremely unequal, which is masked by visible wealth rather than addressed through reforms. A social share shows in the international positioning as host of major sporting events. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, materially demonstrated family success." },
    { name: "Madagascar", iso: "MG", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Madagascar, separated from Africa some 88 million years ago, developed through its geographic isolation a unique flora and fauna (over 90 percent endemic, including the famous lemurs) unlike anywhere else in the world. Malagasy culture itself is a distinctive amalgam of Austronesian (not African) roots, a Malayo-Polynesian language, and African as well as Arab influences — a cultural identity clearly distinct from any African neighbor. The \"famadihana\" custom, in which the remains of the deceased are ritually exhumed, wrapped in new cloth, and reburied, shows a unique, deeply rooted relationship to ancestors and death found nowhere else in this form. Recurring political crises and several unconstitutional transfers of power since independence in 1960 are experienced by many Malagasy as an expression of their own, often misunderstood political special path, far removed from continental African patterns. The island's extreme biodiversity and ecological uniqueness is understood by the international community and residents alike as a national distinguishing feature of global significance.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Madagascar shows a pronouncedly social Four: Malagasy culture, a distinctive amalgam of Austronesian roots, Malayo-Polynesian language, and African and Arab influences, is deliberately understood as a cultural identity clearly distinct from any African neighbor—uniqueness as a collective self-understanding, not individual distancing. The island's extreme biodiversity and ecological uniqueness are cultivated equally by residents and the international community as a national distinguishing feature. A self-preservation share shows in the \"Famadihana\" custom, which cultivates a uniquely village-lived relationship to ancestors and death. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively carried cultural distinctiveness." },
  ]},
  { region: "Middle East", laender: [
    { name: "Israel", iso: "IL", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Almost every adult Israeli has completed military service and remains ready for deployment in the reserves for decades—a collective awareness of a permanent state of threat that permeates daily life, from school trips to bomb shelters in nearly every residential building. The \"Sabra\" mentality—named after the prickly pear cactus fruit, tough on the outside, soft inside—describes a cultural approach to caution: gruff upon first contact, but fiercely loyal within the inner circle. The Holocaust, as a collective trauma, is commemorated annually with nationwide memorial ceremonies and a minute of silence during which traffic literally comes to a standstill. Security checks, from shopping malls to bus stops, are everyday, taken-for-granted routine rather than a state of emergency. Despite frequent regional conflicts, Israel invests an above-average amount in civilian early-warning systems and shelters—precaution as a foundational state principle.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Israel shows a very pronouncedly social Six: nearly every adult Israeli has completed military service and remains reserve-ready for decades—collective vigilance as a shared, institutionalized civic duty, not individual caution. Nationwide Holocaust memorial ceremonies, during which traffic literally comes to a complete stop, confirm this pattern of communally celebrated remembrance and vigilance. A self-preservation share shows in security checks and shelters as everyday, practical precaution in nearly every residential building. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, collectively carried security culture." },
    { name: "Saudi Arabia", iso: "SA", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "The five daily prayer times structure all public life—shops close promptly, and traffic reorganizes immediately afterward. Sharia forms the foundation of the legal system, with clear, unalterable rules of conduct for nearly every area of life, from clothing to business transactions. Mecca, as the holiest site in Islam, welcomes millions of pilgrims annually during the Hajj—a highly organized, meticulously planned logistical event. Vision 2030, the ambitious state reform program, is implemented with the same structured, top-down precision as traditional religious order was enforced. Public order, gender segregation in many spaces, and a pronounced system of social control shape everyday societal life even today, despite recent relaxations.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Saudi Arabia shows a very pronouncedly social One: the five daily prayer times structure the entire public day—shops close punctually, traffic reorganizes immediately afterward—and Sharia, with clear, unchanging rules of conduct, forms the basis of collectively binding order for nearly every area of life. The highly organized, meticulously planned Hajj, which receives millions of pilgrims annually, shows the same precision in service of the community. A self-preservation share shows in the pronounced system of social control in immediate daily life. Sexual One recedes behind this broad, top-down enforced public order." },
    { name: "Iran", iso: "IR", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Nowruz, the Persian New Year celebrated for over 3,000 years, is cultivated with visible pride as a distinct, pre-Islamic cultural root—a conscious adherence to a Persian rather than purely Arab-Islamic identity. The poets Hafez and Rumi enjoy a social status akin to national heroes—their verses are quoted in daily life, used for fortune-telling, and memorized by heart. Despite international sanctions and economic pressure, Iranian society demonstrates remarkable resilience and a refusal to bow to external coercion—spanning from the art scene to protest movements like \"Woman, Life, Freedom.\" Historic pride in the Persian Empire, one of the world's oldest advanced civilizations, is consciously wielded against Arab and Western influences. Direct, often confrontational political rhetoric, both from state authorities and protest movements, reveals a culture that does not easily submit.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Iran shows a pronouncedly social Eight: the Nowruz festival, celebrated for over 3,000 years, is cultivated with visible, collective pride as a distinctly Persian rather than purely Arab-Islamic identity, and historical pride in the Persian Empire is deliberately positioned against Arab and Western influences—national assertion as a shared principle of resistance. Both state rhetoric and protest movements such as \"Woman, Life, Freedom\" show the same refusal to bend to outside pressure. A self-preservation share shows in the country's remarkable economic resilience despite sanctions. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad, collective will to assert itself." },
    { name: "Lebanon", iso: "LB", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Beirut was once hailed as the \"Paris of the Middle East\"—a reputation as a cosmopolitan commercial and cultural hub that many Lebanese actively strive to keep alive despite repeated crises. The Lebanese diaspora, larger than the population residing within the country itself, is globally renowned for entrepreneurial success spanning trade empires to the financial sector. Despite civil war (1975–1990), economic collapse, and the 2020 Beirut port explosion, the Lebanese business world holds fast to a remarkably entrepreneurial, achievement-oriented self-image. The vast number of banks and financial service providers in such a small country historically underscores Beirut's role as a regional financial hub. Education and academic success command immense social status—a university degree, often earned abroad, serves as both a status symbol and a career promise.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Lebanon shows a pronouncedly social Three: Beirut's reputation as the \"Paris of the Middle East,\" which many Lebanese actively keep alive despite repeated crises, and the enormous social status of education and academic success—a university degree as a status symbol—show success as a collectively recognized, publicly visible good. The Lebanese diaspora, known worldwide for entrepreneurial success, reinforces this image of a nationally shared self-image of advancement. A self-preservation share shows in the business world's persistence in entrepreneurial thinking despite economic collapse. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, socially anchored image of success." },
    { name: "Jordan", iso: "JO", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "Hospitality is almost sacred in Jordanian Bedouin culture: Traditionally, even a stranger entering a tent must be entertained and provided for for three days before even being asked about their purpose. The shared \"Mansaf\" dish, eaten from a giant communal platter using the right hand, is a symbol of shared hospitality at celebrations and important occasions. Over decades, Jordan has taken in millions of refugees from Palestine, Iraq, and Syria—a nationally practiced effort of care despite its own limited resources. Family and tribal structures remain central social institutions that support one another through weddings, illness, and financial hardship. The Bedouin culture of the Wadi Rum desert relies to this day on an ethos of mutual care, which was historically necessary for survival under sparse conditions.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Jordan shows a very pronouncedly social Two: hospitality is nearly sacred in Bedouin culture—traditionally even a stranger entering the tent must be hosted for three days before being asked their purpose—and taking in millions of refugees from Palestine, Iraq, and Syria over decades shows care as a nationally lived, institutionalized practice. The shared \"Mansaf\" dish, eaten from one large bowl, symbolizes shared hospitality at festivals. A self-preservation share shows in family and tribal structures that support each other at weddings, illness, and financial hardship. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, nationally lived practice of care." },
    { name: "United Arab Emirates", iso: "AE", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Dubai, once a modest fishing and pearl-diving village, deliberately transformed itself within a few decades into a global symbol of luxury, height, and superlatives—the Burj Khalifa, the tallest building in the world, stands as an emblem for this narrative of advancement. The Emirates target investments in spectacular, internationally visible mega-projects—artificial islands, the tallest hotels, spectacular World Expos—to generate global attention and status. Tax exemptions and targeted economic development attract international companies and professionals, with the stated goal of being perceived as a global business and status hub. Cultural visibility is sought as well: the Louvre Abu Dhabi and spectacular museum projects are intended to demonstrate international prestige. The rapid ascent from a traditional desert region to one of the wealthiest places in the world is actively cultivated and marketed as a national success narrative.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "The United Arab Emirates shows a very pronouncedly social Three: Dubai deliberately transformed itself into a global symbol of luxury and superlatives, and the targeted investments in spectacular, internationally visible megaprojects—artificial islands, the tallest hotels, world expos—explicitly aim to generate global attention and status before an international audience. The Louvre Abu Dhabi and similar museum projects demonstrate the same striving for international prestige. A self-preservation share shows in deliberate tax exemption to attract international talent. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, publicly marketed national success narrative." },
    { name: "Iraq", iso: "IQ", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Iraq, historic Mesopotamia and one of the cradles of civilization, carries its thousands-of-years-old history as a source of national pride despite decades of war and occupation. Tribal structures, with their own armed defense capabilities and codes of honor, have historically often possessed more real power and legitimacy than the respective central government. Following the overthrow of Saddam Hussein in 2003, the US occupation, and the subsequent fight against the \"Islamic State,\" a culture has developed where assertiveness and defensive readiness are held in high regard. Militias and armed groups, whether Shia, Sunni, or Kurdish, often view themselves explicitly as protectors of their respective communities against external threats. Direct, confrontational conflict resolution, whether political or societal, is chosen more frequently than lengthy diplomatic searches for compromise.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Iraq shows a clearly self-preservation Eight: tribal structures with their own armed defense capabilities and honor codes historically often hold more real power and legitimacy than the respective central government—protection and territory concretely in one's own hands rather than the state's. Militias, whether Shia, Sunni, or Kurdish, explicitly see themselves as protectors of their respective community against outside threat, aiming at material protection of one's own environment. A social share shows in collective pride in Mesopotamia's millennia-old history. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad resilience oriented toward tribal and community protection." },
    { name: "Qatar", iso: "QA", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Qatar, once one of the poorest regions in the Gulf, utilized enormous natural gas reserves to develop into one of the world's richest countries per capita within a few decades—a consciously staged, rapid ascent. The 2022 FIFA World Cup, featuring billions invested in stadiums and infrastructure, was staged explicitly as an international statement of global importance and visibility. Al Jazeera, the Qatari-funded international news network, granted the small country a media reach far beyond its actual size. Education City in Doha, with branch campuses of elite Western universities, is designed to bring international academic prestige to the country. Similar to the Emirates, Qatar deliberately relies on spectacular, internationally visible projects as an expression of its economic and political advancement.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Qatar shows a very pronouncedly social Three: the 2022 FIFA World Cup, with billions invested, was explicitly staged as an international statement of global significance, and Al Jazeera gave the small country a media reach far beyond its actual size—status deliberately generated for an international audience. Education City in Doha, with branches of elite Western universities, aims to bring international academic prestige to the country. A self-preservation share shows in using gas reserves to economically secure the rapid rise. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, media-staged status building." },
    { name: "Yemen", iso: "YE", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Yemen, shaped by one of the longest-running armed conflicts of present times, has a deeply rooted tribal culture where belonging to one's own clan provides protection and identity across generations. Firearm ownership is traditionally widespread and is understood not primarily as aggression, but as a necessary means of self-defense and family protection. Historical tribal feuds and their traditional settlement by councils of elders reveal a cultural system based on caution and negotiated compromise between armed groups. The humanitarian crisis of recent years, with famine and displacement, has made family solidarity networks even more vital than state institutions, which are often barely functional. Mistrust toward external powers, whether regional neighbors or international actors, is historically deeply grounded and shapes political decisions to this day.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Yemen shows a clearly self-preservation Six: gun ownership is traditionally widespread and understood not primarily as aggression but as a necessary means of self-defense and family safeguarding, and the humanitarian crisis has made family solidarity networks even more important than often barely functioning state institutions. The deeply rooted tribal culture, in which belonging to one's clan provides protection and identity across generations, reinforces this picture of concrete, family-organized safeguarding. A social share shows in the traditional settlement of tribal feuds by councils of elders. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, family-anchored caution strategy." },
    { name: "Afghanistan", iso: "AF", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "After decades of war—against the Soviet Union, in civil war, against the US and NATO forces—vigilance in Afghanistan is not an exaggerated caution, but the lived reality of multiple generations. The tribal and clan structure, along with the Pashtun code of honor \"Pashtunwali,\" continues to govern hospitality, conflict resolution, and mutual protection often more effectively than state institutions. Loya Jirgas, traditional grand councils of tribal elders, have been used for centuries to collectively seek security and consensus during times of crisis. Families traditionally keep weapons and supplies across generations as a precautionary measure against recurring instability. Extreme geographical fragmentation into isolated mountain valleys has historically favored independent, mistrustful, and cautious local communities that rarely submitted fully to a central authority.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Afghanistan shows a clearly self-preservation Six: families traditionally keep weapons and provisions across generations as a concrete precaution against recurring instability, and the extreme geographic fragmentation into isolated mountain valleys has favored self-contained, distrustfully cautious local communities that rarely fully submitted to central power. The Pashtun honor code \"Pashtunwali,\" which governs hospitality, conflict resolution, and mutual protection, reinforces this family-local security orientation. A social share shows in the Loya Jirgas, traditional councils seeking collective consensus in times of crisis. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, family-and-locally anchored vigilance." },
    { name: "Georgia", iso: "GE", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Ground down for centuries between Persian, Ottoman, Mongol, and finally Russian and Soviet great powers, Georgia has developed a deeply rooted awareness of its own vulnerability between powerful neighbors—an awareness kept painfully current by the 2008 war over South Ossetia and Abkhazia. The Georgian Orthodox Church, with roots reaching back to the 4th century, became the central anchor of national identity and continuity through centuries of Muslim foreign rule—faith as a refuge, not merely piety. The traditional \"supra,\" a ritually structured feast led by a \"tamada\" as toastmaster, celebrates loyalty to family, friends, ancestors, and homeland again and again through elaborate toasts—loyalty as a repeatedly reaffirmed pledge. The rugged mountain landscape of the Caucasus historically favored independent, defensively organized mountain communities and clan structures that rarely submitted fully to a central authority. To this day, Georgia navigates warily between its desire for EU and NATO integration and its constant concern about Russian influence—the caution of a small country in the shadow of great powers.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Georgia shows a pronouncedly social Six: the traditional \"Supra,\" a ritually structured feast with a \"Tamada\" as toastmaster, repeatedly celebrates loyalty to family, friends, ancestors, and homeland through elaborate toasts—loyalty as a repeatedly reaffirmed, communal pledge. The Georgian Orthodox Church became the central anchor of collective national identity through centuries of Muslim foreign rule. A self-preservation share shows in the wary navigation between EU integration and concern over Russian influence. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, ritually cultivated culture of loyalty." },
    { name: "Kazakhstan", iso: "KZ", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Within a single generation, Kazakhstan transformed from a Soviet steppe republic into a confidently staged rising power, complete with skyscrapers, a World Expo (2017), and an entirely rebuilt capital, Astana, as a showcase of national ascent. The renaming and construction of the capital itself — a prestige project built from scratch — is widely seen as a deliberate performance of modernity and global arrival. Oil and resource wealth are channeled purposefully into visible megaprojects, international sporting events, and a polished national image. The \"World Nomad Games,\" a purpose-built international sporting event, reveal the desire to present traditional roots as a global brand. Navigating between Russia and China, Kazakhstan deliberately pursues a multi-vector foreign policy that visibly underscores its own significance on the world stage.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Kazakhstan shows a very pronouncedly social Three: the completely newly built capital Astana as a showcase of national advancement and hosting Expo 2017 show deliberate staging of modernity for an international audience, not private wealth. The specially created \"Nomad Games\" show the desire to present traditional roots as a global brand. A self-preservation share shows in the targeted investment of oil and resource wealth into visible megaprojects. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad advancement staged for international recognition." },
    { name: "Armenia", iso: "AM", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "As the first state in the world to adopt Christianity as its state religion in 301 AD, Armenia carries a deep sense of cultural uniqueness amid predominantly Muslim neighbors. The 1915 genocide, still unrecognized by Turkey, has become the central, generation-spanning trauma of national identity — remembrance as an act of survival. The Armenian diaspora, larger than the population within the country itself, maintains an intense, often painfully longing connection to the lost homeland. The loss of Nagorno-Karabakh in 2020 and 2023 was experienced not merely as a military defeat but as a deeply identity-shaking loss, mourned publicly. The unique Armenian script, centuries-old churches built from volcanic tuff, and a rich tradition of melancholic poetry keep alive a cultural self-image that consciously sets itself apart from its surroundings.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Armenia shows a pronouncedly social Four: as the first state in the world to adopt Christianity as its state religion in 301 CE, Armenia carries a collectively shared awareness of cultural uniqueness amid predominantly Muslim neighbors—distinctiveness as national self-understanding, not individual distancing. The 1915 genocide, still unrecognized, has become the central, cross-generational trauma of national identity, carried together by the diaspora. A self-preservation share shows in the diaspora's painful connection to a lost homeland. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively cultivated cultural distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Oman", iso: "OM", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Until 1970, under Sultan Said bin Taimur, Oman was almost entirely sealed off from the outside world — radios, eyeglasses, and even umbrellas were at times banned, out of deep mistrust of foreign influence. Unlike its glittering Gulf neighbors, Oman deliberately forgoes spectacular prestige projects, favoring restrained, resource-conscious development instead. The country has a long tradition of playing a discreet, often secret mediating role in international conflicts — between Iran and the West, in US hostage negotiations — preferring the background to the spotlight. Water is considered the region's scarcest resource; the centuries-old falaj irrigation system reflects a cultural trait of extreme frugality in handling limited resources. Omanis are widely regarded throughout the Arab world as especially reserved, observant, and sparing with words — a collective withdrawal that observes rather than displays itself.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Oman shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Five: unlike its glamorous Gulf neighbors, Oman deliberately forgoes spectacular prestige projects in favor of restrained, resource-conscious development, and the centuries-old Falaj irrigation system shows a cultural trait of extreme frugality in handling limited resources. Near-total isolation from the outside world until 1970 reinforces this picture of deliberate withdrawal. A social share shows in its discreet, often secret mediator role in international conflicts. Sexual Five recedes behind this broad, resource-conscious restraint." },
    { name: "Uzbekistan", iso: "UZ", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "Uzbekistan has invested massively for years in the meticulous restoration of its Silk Road cities Samarkand, Bukhara, and Khiva — order and perfection as national legacy, not mere heritage preservation. Under decades of authoritarian rule (Karimov, later Mirziyoyev), a tightly woven system of state control still runs through administration, press, and public life. Strict, often unyielding regulations governing religious practice — regulated down to the smallest detail — reveal a deep need to enforce order even in matters of faith. The cult around Timur (Tamerlane), now revered as a national founder of order, deliberately supplants the Soviet past in favor of a disciplined, orderly national narrative. Recent economic reforms are implemented with the same top-down, systematic precision historically used to enforce religious and state order.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Uzbekistan shows a very pronouncedly social One: the meticulous restoration of the Silk Road cities of Samarkand, Bukhara, and Khiva shows order and perfection as national legacy, and the tight-knit system of state control pervading administration, press, and public life enforces collective rule-following top-down. Strict, minutely regulated rules for religious practice show the same need to enforce order even in faith. A self-preservation share shows in the cult around Timur as national founder of order. Sexual One recedes behind this broad, systematically enforced public order." },
    { name: "Kuwait", iso: "KW", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The 1990 Iraqi invasion and seven-month occupation remain Kuwait's central national trauma — a collective awareness of permanent vulnerability toward more powerful neighbors. As the only Gulf state with a genuinely elected parliament holding real oversight power, Kuwait shows a deep need for institutional safeguards against abuse of power. Close military ties to the US and Western allies are explicitly understood as a life insurance policy against future threats, not merely alliance politics. Kuwaiti families traditionally maintain close, dependable networks of mutual support that proved life-saving during the occupation and other crises. \"Liberation Day\" is marked each year with great public effort — remembrance of liberation as a constant reminder to stay vigilant.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Kuwait shows a clearly self-preservation Six: close military ties to the US and Western allies are explicitly understood as a life insurance policy against future threats, and Kuwaiti families traditionally cultivate close, reliable networks of mutual safeguarding that proved life-saving during the 1990 occupation—concrete, material protection front and center. As the only Gulf state with a genuinely elected parliament holding real oversight power, an additional institutional need for safeguarding shows through. A social share shows in the annual \"Liberation Day\" as a collective ritual of vigilance. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad caution strategy oriented toward concrete safeguarding." },
    { name: "Bahrain", iso: "BH", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "As the first Gulf state to produce oil, already in the 1930s, Bahrain early on positioned itself as a regional pioneer and deliberately built that head start into a banking and finance hub for the Middle East. The Sakhir race track, hosting the annual Bahrain Grand Prix since 2004, exists explicitly to grant international visibility to a country barely larger than a mid-sized city. Despite limited oil reserves of its own compared to its neighbors, Bahrain invests specifically in diversification to maintain its status as a modern, rising economic power. Its connection to larger, more powerful Saudi Arabia via the King Fahd Causeway is framed as a bridge to prosperity and status, not a sign of dependence. International conferences, bank branches, and a deliberately cosmopolitan image aim to make a tiny kingdom appear as a significant player on the world stage.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Bahrain shows a very pronouncedly social Three: as the first Gulf state to produce oil, Bahrain positioned itself early as a regional pioneer and deliberately built that status edge into a financial and banking center, and the Sakhir Formula 1 circuit serves explicitly for international visibility before a global audience. International conferences and a deliberately cosmopolitan image aim to make the small kingdom appear as a significant actor on the world stage. A self-preservation share shows in targeted economic diversification despite limited own oil reserves. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad status-building aimed at international recognition." },
    { name: "Turkmenistan", iso: "TM", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Former president Saparmurat Niyazov had golden statues of himself erected, renamed months and bread after himself and his mother, and wrote his own spiritual national book, the \"Ruhnama\" — self-staging as a state principle. The capital Ashgabat has more white marble buildings per capita than almost any other city in the world — a deliberately staged image of wealth and modernity, funded by vast gas reserves. The \"Gates of Hell\" at Darvaza, a natural-gas crater that has been burning for decades, is deliberately marketed as a tourist attraction and symbol of national greatness rather than extinguished. Vast, often nearly empty boulevards and monumental buildings aim to convey an image of success and progress to international visitors, regardless of actual economic reality. At the same time, the country ranks among the most sealed-off states from the outside world — success is staged, but rarely exposed to international scrutiny.", subtyp: "sx3", subtypText: "Turkmenistan shows a very pronouncedly sexual Three: former President Niyazov had golden statues of himself erected, renamed months and bread after himself and his mother, and wrote his own spiritual national book—self-staging as a personal, almost narcissistic cult of personality rather than collective achievement. The vast, often empty grand avenues and monumental buildings aim to convey success regardless of actual economic reality. A self-preservation share shows in the enormous gas reserves that finance this staging. Social Three, which would show as internationally visible recognition, recedes behind this personal, largely closed-off leader cult." },
    { name: "Kyrgyzstan", iso: "KG", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "As the most democratic yet also most politically unstable republic in Central Asia, Kyrgyzstan has experienced three revolutions since independence in 1991 (2005, 2010, 2020) — each time with comparatively little bloodshed and a swift return to everyday normality. The traditional nomadic yurt culture, in which hospitality and community spirit are central values, contrasts with the political instability in the cities. The epic poem \"Manas,\" one of the longest orally transmitted epics in the world, is cultivated as a cultural bond that provides continuity across political upheavals. Unlike its more authoritarian neighbors Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan, Kyrgyzstan preserves a remarkably open civil society and free press despite the chaos. The country pragmatically navigates between Russian, Chinese, and Western influence without entering open confrontation with any of the powers.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Kyrgyzstan shows a pronouncedly social Nine: after each of its three revolutions since 1991, the country returned comparatively quickly to everyday normalcy rather than sustained confrontation, and traditional nomadic yurt culture, in which hospitality and community spirit are central values, shapes the social baseline. The epic poem \"Manas\" is cultivated as a cultural bond that provides continuity across political upheavals—shared narrative as an anchor of stability. A self-preservation share shows in pragmatically navigating between great powers without open confrontation. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, communally carried normalizing force." },
    { name: "Tajikistan", iso: "TJ", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The civil war (1992–1997), one of the bloodiest post-Soviet conflicts with around 100,000 deaths, left behind a country still marked by deep mistrust between regions and clans. President Emomali Rahmon, uninterruptedly in power since the war's end, explicitly justifies his authoritarian control as preventing a renewed civil war. The remote Pamir region, almost entirely isolated geographically, traditionally preserves independent, wary communities that rarely trusted central authority. The long, porous border with Afghanistan is perceived as a constant source of threat, secured by a massive military presence. Checkpoints, ID controls, and a dense security network run through daily life as a visible legacy of postwar vigilance.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Tajikistan shows a clearly self-preservation Six: checkpoints, ID controls, and a dense security net pervade daily life as a visible, concrete legacy of postwar vigilance, and the long, porous border with Afghanistan is perceived as a constant source of threat, with massive military presence for safeguarding. President Rahmon explicitly justifies his authoritarian control with preventing renewed civil war—security as the top, personally owned state goal. A social share shows in the deep distrust between regions and clans after the civil war. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, materially visible security architecture." },
    { name: "Azerbaijan", iso: "AZ", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "Azerbaijan has deliberately invested its oil wealth in spectacular prestige projects — from the futuristic Flame Towers in Baku to hosting the 2012 Eurovision Song Contest and Formula 1 since 2016. President Ilham Aliyev, son of his predecessor, consistently stages the country internationally as a modern, rising success story on the Caspian Sea. Victory in the second Nagorno-Karabakh war in 2020 was celebrated nationwide as a triumphant national success, complete with military parades and a purpose-built \"trophy park\" of captured Armenian equipment. Within a few decades, Baku was transformed from a Soviet industrial city into a gleaming metropolis of skyscrapers and international conferences — visible progress as reason of state. Hosting the UN Climate Conference COP29 in 2024 likewise explicitly served international recognition and image-building for the country as a significant global player.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "Azerbaijan shows a very pronouncedly social Three: President Aliyev consistently stages the country internationally as a modern, rising success story, and the deliberate hosting of Eurovision, Formula 1, and the COP29 UN climate conference explicitly serves international recognition before a global audience. Victory in the second Nagorno-Karabakh war in 2020 was celebrated nationwide with military parades as a collective, public triumph. A self-preservation share shows in the targeted investment of oil wealth into the country's own economic safeguarding. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, media-staged national success narrative." },
    { name: "Syria", iso: "SY", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Syria was ruled from 1970 until its overthrow in 2024 by the Assad family — first Hafez al-Assad, then his son Bashar — whose rule for decades relied on one of the region's most brutal intelligence and security apparatuses. The civil war beginning in 2011, triggered by the brutal crackdown on civilian protests, escalated into one of the most devastating armed power struggles of the 21st century, with barrel bombs, chemical weapons attacks, and hundreds of thousands killed. Rival power centers — government forces, various rebel groups, ISIS, Kurdish forces — fought for over a decade for territorial control, backed by competing foreign powers (Russia, Iran, Turkey, the US). Assad's surprisingly rapid fall in late 2024 through an offensive by Islamist rebel groups again showed that in Syria, territory and rule are ultimately decided by military effectiveness, not negotiation. The new leadership under Ahmed al-Sharaa has since faced the task of bringing a country of rival armed factions and militias under any shared authority at all.", subtyp: "so8", subtypText: "Syria shows a pronouncedly social Eight: rival power centers—government troops, various rebel groups, ISIS, Kurdish forces—fought for over a decade for territorial control, each backed by competing foreign powers—organized group confrontation rather than solo combat. The surprisingly swift fall of Assad in late 2024 through the offensive of Islamist rebel groups again showed that territory is decided by collective military assertiveness. A self-preservation share shows in the Assad family's decades-long security apparatus built on control and surveillance. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad, cross-factional power struggle." },
    { name: "Palestine", iso: "PS", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Palestine, listed as a UN \"non-member observer state\" since 2012 but without full sovereignty over its claimed territory, carries a national identity deeply shaped by the 1948 Nakba (the expulsion and flight of hundreds of thousands of Palestinians) and decades of occupation. The geographic and political rift between the West Bank (Palestinian Authority) and the Gaza Strip (Hamas), often marked by open rivalry between the two power centers, reinforces an awareness of unique, complex national fragmentation. The concept of \"sumud\" (steadfast perseverance, rootedness in one's own land despite all adversity) is cultivated as a central, distinctive cultural value that sets the Palestinian experience apart from that of any other people. The Palestinian diaspora, scattered worldwide and living in refugee camps in neighboring countries, maintains an intense, often painful connection to a lost or inaccessible homeland as a central marker of identity. The conflict over the same historical landscape, the same cities, and the same history with Israel remains the defining, all-overshadowing reference point of Palestinian national self-understanding.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Palestine shows a pronouncedly social Four: national identity, deeply shaped by the 1948 \"Nakba\" and decades of occupation, is understood as a collectively shared, unique experience that sets it apart from that of any other people—distinctiveness as a shared national self-understanding. The concept of \"Sumud,\" steadfast endurance and rootedness in one's own land, is collectively cultivated as a central, distinctive cultural value. A self-preservation share shows in the painful connection of the globally scattered diaspora to a lost or inaccessible homeland. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, nationally shared sense of identity." },
  ]},
  { region: "Asia", laender: [
    { name: "Japan", iso: "JP", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Hardly any word is as untranslatably Japanese as \"Mono no Aware\"—the wistful, deeply felt awareness of the impermanence of things, celebrated, for instance, in the annual cherry blossom season that deliberately lasts only a few days. \"Wabi-Sabi,\" the aesthetic of the imperfect and transient, is found in the ceramic art of Kintsugi, where broken porcelain is repaired with gold lacquer—the fracture is visibly celebrated, not hidden. Haiku poetry, with its concise, emotionally intense capture of a fleeting moment, has been a highly respected art form for centuries. The tea ceremony, celebrated in utter silence and precision, ultimately serves to consciously honor a unique, never-recurring moment of shared experience—\"Ichi-go ichi-e,\" one encounter, one moment. Yet this deep emotional culture is not expressed in an unbridled way; rather, it is poured into highly disciplined forms—order as a vessel for deep emotion, not its opposite.", subtyp: "se4", subtypText: "Japan shows a clearly self-preservation Four: \"Mono no Aware\" and \"Wabi-Sabi\" are not lived out unrestrained but poured into highly disciplined, private forms—the tea ceremony, celebrated in complete silence, honors a unique moment within strict, ritualized form. Kintsugi, in which broken porcelain is repaired with gold lacquer, shows melancholy as something cultivated concretely, by hand, in one's own home. A social share shows in haiku poetry, revered for centuries as a shared art form. Sexual Four recedes behind this quiet, disciplined, contained emotional culture." },
    { name: "China", iso: "CN", typ: 3, unsicher: false, text: "The rise from a land of bicycles in the 1980s to the world's second-largest economy within a single generation is one of the most dramatic economic advancement narratives in history—and is actively celebrated as such. The \"Gaokao,\" China's national university entrance exam, is considered one of the most competitive educational systems in the world—families invest enormous resources so that their children can advance socially. Status symbols—from international luxury brands to apartment size in megacities—are displayed openly as proof of success. Millions of migrant workers left rural regions for the cities, driven by the promise of economic advancement, often at significant personal sacrifice. At the same time, ancient China carried a completely different, more introverted note over millennia: the Confucian and Daoist scholarly traditions, calligraphy, and meditation as paths to quiet insight. This tension between the historic ideal of retreat and the present striving for success is itself the true insight into China's cultural shift.", subtyp: "so3", subtypText: "China shows a pronouncedly social Three: the rise from a bicycle-nation to the world's second-largest economy is actively celebrated as a collective national success narrative, and the \"Gaokao,\" one of the world's most competitive education systems, shows families investing enormous resources so children can rise socially—success as a shared, socially recognized goal. Status symbols are openly displayed as visible proof of success before the community. A self-preservation share shows in the millions of migrant workers who make personal sacrifices for economic advancement. Sexual Three recedes behind this broad, collectively celebrated advancement narrative." },
    { name: "India", iso: "IN", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "With 22 official languages, hundreds of additional dialects, and practically all world religions united on a single subcontinent, India functions only because there is a deep cultural capacity to let contradictions coexist rather than resolving them. Hinduism itself has no central dogma, but rather an enormous spectrum of deities, philosophies, and practices revered alongside one another as equals. On crowded streets, in packed trains, and amid chaotic traffic, a remarkable collective composure toward tight spaces and apparent disarray is displayed—\"Adjust kar lo,\" \"just adjust,\" is an everyday phrase. Yoga and meditation, originally spiritual practices for inner equilibrium, are so deeply rooted in daily culture that they long ago became global exports. Despite massive social tensions and conflicts, India has maintained the world's largest democracy since independence in 1947—an ongoing, often chaotic, but rarely violently escalating struggle for balance between extremely diverse interests.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "India shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: with 22 official languages and virtually every world religion on one subcontinent, the country only functions because a deep cultural capacity exists to let contradictions coexist collectively rather than resolve them—\"Adjust kar lo\" as an everyday, shared phrase. The world's largest democracy has, since 1947, sustained an ongoing, rarely violently escalating struggle for balance between extremely different interests. A self-preservation share shows in yoga and meditation as deeply rooted practices of inner balance. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, socially lived composure." },
    { name: "South Korea", iso: "KR", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "\"Jeong,\" a concept of deep emotional bond and belonging that goes beyond mere friendship, is considered the central glue of South Korean relationships—people feel responsible for one another, often across decades. Enormous educational pressure, complete with cram schools (Hagwon) late into the night, is understood by parents not primarily as individual striving for success, but as an investment in the honor and future of the entire family. K-Pop bands, strictly trained and choreographed, cultivate a relationship culture with their fans based on mutual emotional care and loyalty rather than simple consumption. Eating together from a shared platter, accompanied by drinking rituals where participants actively pour for each other, symbolizes everyday mutual care. Companies are traditionally understood as quasi-family communities where superiors are expected to look after employees' well-being in exchange for loyalty—a relational contract that extends beyond basic wage labor.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "South Korea shows a very pronouncedly social Two: \"Jeong,\" a concept of deep emotional connection beyond mere friendship, is considered the central glue of all relationships, and companies are traditionally understood as quasi-family communities in which superiors are expected to look after employees' well-being—care as an institutionalized relationship principle. K-Pop bands cultivate a relationship culture of mutual emotional care and loyalty with their fans. A self-preservation share shows in enormous educational pressure as an investment in the whole family's honor. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, institutionally anchored culture of care." },
    { name: "Thailand", iso: "TH", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "\"Land of Smiles\" is no mere marketing slogan: The smile fulfills many social functions in Thailand—it apologizes, comforts, greets, and actively shows care far beyond simple politeness. \"Kreng Jai,\" the consideration given to avoid imposing a burden or inconvenience on others, shapes daily social interactions down to the smallest detail. Street vendors and neighbors naturally share food with one another without any special occasion—care as an everyday, unspectacular practice. Buddhism, practiced by over 90 percent of the population, emphasizes actively accumulating merit through good deeds for others rather than passive meditation alone. Family care traditionally extends to elderly parents, who are naturally cared for by their children at home rather than placed in care facilities—active, lived affection as a core cultural value.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Thailand shows a very pronouncedly social Two: the smile serves many social functions—it apologizes, comforts, greets, and actively shows care—and \"Kreng Jai,\" consideration for not burdening others, shapes daily social interactions down to the smallest detail. Buddhism, practiced by over 90 percent, emphasizes actively earning merit through good deeds for others, not just passive meditation. A self-preservation share shows in the family care of elderly parents at home rather than in institutions. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, everyday-lived culture of care." },
    { name: "Vietnam", iso: "VN", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Following centuries of Chinese rule, French colonization, and the devastating Vietnam War resulting in millions of deaths, vigilance regarding external interference is deeply anchored in the national consciousness. The spirit of resistance against overwhelming adversaries—whether China, France, or the USA—is taught in schools and public monuments as a central national narrative. Family and village communities, bound by tight mutual obligations, historically served as the most reliable protection during wartime when central authority was absent or unreliable. Ancestor worship, featuring home altars in nearly every Vietnamese household, closely links earthly security with the care and protection provided by deceased ancestors. Even amidst today's economic boom, a cultural caution toward rapid, unverified promises remains evident—trust must prove itself over time.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Vietnam shows a clearly self-preservation Six: family and village communities with close mutual obligations historically served as the most reliable protection in wartime, when central power was absent, and ancestor worship, with household altars in nearly every home, closely links security in this life with care from deceased ancestors—concrete, family-anchored protection. Even in today's economic boom, a cultural caution toward unverified promises remains recognizable. A social share shows in the nationally taught narrative of resistance against overpowering opponents. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, family-anchored vigilance." },
    { name: "Indonesia", iso: "ID", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "With over 17,000 islands, hundreds of ethnic groups and languages, and the world's largest Muslim population alongside significant Hindu, Christian, and Buddhist minorities, Indonesia consciously holds its diversity together through the national motto \"Bhinneka Tunggal Ika\"—Unity in Diversity. \"Gotong Royong,\" the principle of mutual, unbureaucratic neighborly assistance, is enshrined as a core value from the village level to the constitution. Balinese culture, for example, integrates Hindu, animist, and later Islamic influences into an independent, peaceful synthesis rather than mutual displacement. Despite occasional religious tensions, Indonesian society remains remarkably stable and unescalating in regional comparison. The pronounced principle of \"Musyawarah\"—finding consensus through joint deliberation until all can agree—shapes decision-making processes from village assemblies to national politics.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Indonesia shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the national motto \"Bhinneka Tunggal Ika\"—unity in diversity—deliberately holds enormous diversity together collectively, and \"Gotong Royong,\" the principle of mutual, unbureaucratic neighborly help, is anchored as a core value from the village level up to the constitution. The principle of \"Musyawarah\"—consensus-building through joint deliberation until all can agree—shapes decision-making at every level. A self-preservation share shows in Balinese culture, which integrates various religious influences into a peaceful synthesis. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, constitutionally anchored ideal of unity." },
    { name: "Philippines", iso: "PH", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "\"Bayanihan,\" literally the communal carrying of an entire house during a move by neighbors, has become the symbol of Philippine solidarity—mutual practical assistance without expecting anything in return. Large family gatherings, often involving dozens of relatives, are a central component of social life, marked by clear mutual obligations across generations. Millions of Filipino workers abroad—\"Overseas Filipino Workers\"—send a significant portion of their income back home, representing a lived, often sacrificial care for the family left behind. Catholic religious practice, with intense observances like Holy Week, emphasizes active charity and mutual care within the community. Even during natural disasters, which strike the Philippines frequently, a remarkably rapid, unbureaucratic neighborly solidarity emerges during reconstruction.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "The Philippines shows a very pronouncedly social Two: \"Bayanihan,\" the communal carrying of an entire house through the neighborhood during a move, has become a symbol of shared, practical help without expectation of return, and the fast, unbureaucratic neighborhood solidarity in rebuilding after natural disasters confirms this pattern of collective care. Catholic religious practice emphasizes active charity within the community. A self-preservation share shows in the millions of Overseas Filipino Workers who send a significant part of their income home to family. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, communally lived culture of solidarity." },
    { name: "Pakistan", iso: "PK", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Since the often bloody partition of India in 1947, which resulted in millions displaced and lost lives, alongside several wars with neighboring India, a deep collective need for security forms part of the national identity. Clan and extended family structures, guided by the honor code \"Izzat,\" govern protection, loyalty, and mutual obligation more strongly than state institutions do in many regions. In the tribal areas along the Afghan border, the Pashtun honor code applies, regarding hospitality and the protection of one's community as a sacred duty. The military and security apparatus traditionally enjoy high societal prestige as guarantors of national security within what is perceived as a threatening geopolitical situation. Family marriage and kinship networks, often arranged within the extended family, also serve to secure trust and mutual assistance.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Pakistan shows a clearly self-preservation Six: clan and extended-family structures with the honor code \"Izzat\" still regulate protection, loyalty, and mutual obligation more strongly than state institutions in many regions, and family marriage and kinship networks, often arranged within the extended family, serve the concrete safeguarding of trust and mutual support. The Pashtun honor code in the tribal areas regards hospitality and protecting one's own community as sacred duty. A social share shows in the high social esteem held by the military and security apparatus as guarantors of national security. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, family-anchored security strategy." },
    { name: "Nepal", iso: "NP", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "As the birthplace of Buddha and featuring a coexistence of Hinduism and Buddhism developed over centuries, Nepal practices a remarkably peaceful religious diversity—many Nepalese naturally visit both Hindu temples and Buddhist stupas. The country's more than one hundred ethnic groups, from the Sherpas in the Himalayas to the Newar in the Kathmandu Valley, live in predominantly peaceful neighborhood relations despite differing languages and customs. The ten-year Maoist civil war (1996–2006) ended, unlike many comparable conflicts, in a negotiated peace process and a new, inclusive constitution instead of permanent division. The Sherpa culture in the Everest region is known for its composure and undramatic approach to the extreme dangers of high-altitude mountaineering. Buddhist prayer flags and mani stones, found everywhere across the country, are meant to actively spread peace and goodwill across the landscape.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Nepal shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the country's more than one hundred ethnic groups live in largely peaceful neighborliness despite different languages and customs, and the ten-year Maoist civil war ended in a negotiated peace process and a new, inclusive constitution rather than lasting division. The centuries-old peaceful coexistence of Hinduism and Buddhism confirms this collective capacity for balance. A self-preservation share shows in the composure of Sherpa culture in dealing with the dangers of high-altitude mountaineering. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, socially lived ideal of peace." },
    { name: "Mongolia", iso: "MN", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "Across one of the most sparsely populated landmasses on Earth—roughly two inhabitants per square kilometer—vastness and solitude form the daily reality for traditionally nomadic herding families. The Ger, the traditional circular tent, enables a life lived at a deliberate distance from larger settlements, often days of travel away from the nearest town. Nomadic culture teaches self-reliance and self-sufficiency from childhood—families frequently move with their herds several times a year, independent of fixed communities. Shamanic and Buddhist traditions emphasize an individual's quiet, introspective connection with nature and the spirit world rather than loud, collective ceremonies. Despite the modern capital of Ulaanbaatar, cultural identity remains strongly shaped by the image of the lonely rider on the endless steppe—retreat and self-reliance as core ideals.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Mongolia shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Five: the ger, the traditional round tent, enables a life at deliberate distance from larger settlements, and nomadic culture teaches self-sufficiency and independence from childhood—families move with their herds independent of fixed communities. In one of the most sparsely populated areas on Earth, vastness and solitude are everyday, practical reality. A social share shows in shamanistic and Buddhist traditions, which nonetheless tend to emphasize the individual's introspective connection to the spirit world. Sexual Five recedes behind this broad, resource-conscious ideal of withdrawal." },
    { name: "Sri Lanka", iso: "LK", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Following a 26-year civil war between the Sinhalese-Buddhist majority and the Tamil minority that ended in 2009, Sri Lanka entered a phase of conscious, if incomplete, reconciliation. Theravada Buddhism, practiced by the majority, emphasizes composure and letting go of resentment as the path to inner peace—an attitude invoked repeatedly in political contexts as well. The famed hospitality extended to travelers, regardless of their origin, reflects a culture that remains remarkably open to the outside world despite internal conflicts. Traditional village communities maintain shared religious festivals that feature overlapping Sinhalese and Tamil elements. The tea plantations in the highlands, with their tranquil, seasonally paced work rhythm, symbolize a culture that maintains a serene continuity despite hardships.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Sri Lanka shows a pronouncedly social Nine: after the 26-year civil war, the country entered a phase of deliberate, collective reconciliation, and Theravada Buddhism, practiced by the majority, emphasizes composure and letting go of resentment as a path to inner peace repeatedly invoked politically. Traditional village communities hold shared religious festivals that partly overlap Sinhalese and Tamil elements. A self-preservation share shows in the calm, seasonally paced work rhythm of the highland tea plantations. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, socially cultivated reconciliation process." },
    { name: "Bangladesh", iso: "BD", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "As one of the most densely populated countries in the world, frequently hit by devastating floods and cyclones, Bangladesh has developed a pronounced culture of family and neighborly precaution. Early-warning systems and cyclone evacuation plans form part of the everyday knowledge of many coastal residents, often passed down through generations. The 1971 War of Independence against (West) Pakistan, with estimated deaths reaching up to three million, serves as a central, painful point of reference for national identity and vigilance. Extended families, often with multiple generations living under one roof, form the most critical social safety net against economic and climate uncertainty. Microcredit systems like those pioneered by Grameen Bank, which originally grew out of local self-help groups, demonstrate a cultural reflex to build financial security collectively against uncertain external circumstances.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Bangladesh shows a clearly self-preservation Six: early warning systems and evacuation plans for cyclones are everyday knowledge for many coastal residents, often passed down through generations, and extended families, often with several generations under one roof, form the most important social safety net against economic and climatic uncertainty—concrete, family-organized precaution. The 1971 independence war remains the central, painful reference point of national vigilance. A social share shows in microcredit systems like the Grameen Bank, which collectively build financial security. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, family- and economically anchored culture of precaution." },
    { name: "Myanmar", iso: "MM", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Theravada Buddhism continues to deeply shape daily life in Myanmar even now—young men traditionally spend time as monks in monasteries to cultivate serenity and inner peace. Pagodas like the golden Shwedagon in Yangon serve as central hubs of daily, quiet spiritual practice for millions. At the same time, the ongoing conflict—the 2021 military coup, decades of civil war with ethnic groups such as the Karen or Kachin, and the Rohingya crisis—is itself an expression of a core Type Nine theme: the difficult struggle for peace and cohesion in a country full of internal fragmentation that struggles to achieve lasting stability. Despite political instability, traditional village communities often maintain a remarkably peaceful, cooperative everyday life. The widespread practice of meditation, even outside monastic life, underscores a cultural longing for inner calm amidst external turbulence.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Myanmar shows a pronouncedly social Nine: Theravada Buddhism deeply shapes daily life—young men traditionally spend time as monks in monasteries to cultivate composure and inner peace—and pagodas like the Shwedagon are central sites of daily, shared spiritual practice for millions. The ongoing conflict itself is an expression of a deeply Nine-like core theme: the difficult struggle for peace and cohesion in a country full of inner division. A self-preservation share shows in the remarkably peaceful, cooperative daily life of village communities despite political instability. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad longing for collective inner calm." },
    { name: "Cambodia", iso: "KH", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Following the terror regime of the Khmer Rouge (1975–1979), which claimed an estimated two million lives—about a quarter of the population at the time—a strikingly reconciliatory everyday culture has developed in Cambodia, surprising many observers. Buddhist temples, deliberately destroyed during the reign of terror, were rebuilt with great social commitment and are once again centers of community life. The Khmer Rouge Trials, which held perpetrators accountable, proceeded in a deliberately orderly fashion and without major acts of revenge against the population. Angkor Wat, the monumental temple heritage of the Khmer empires, is nurtured as a source of proud, peaceful national identity beyond the traumas of the 20th century. Everyday smiles and hospitality toward visitors remain remarkably intact despite the recent, extremely violent history—a conscious collective continuation of life in peace.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Cambodia shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: after the Khmer Rouge terror regime, a strikingly reconciliatory everyday culture developed, and Buddhist temples, deliberately destroyed during the reign of terror, were rebuilt with great communal commitment and are once again centers of community life. The Khmer Rouge tribunals proceeded deliberately in an orderly manner without major acts of revenge against the population—collective reconciliation rather than individual retaliation. A self-preservation share shows in the everyday smile and hospitality despite the extremely violent recent history. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, deliberately cultivated collective peace." },
    { name: "Taiwan", iso: "TW", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "Under constant geopolitical tension with the People's Republic of China, which views Taiwan as a renegade province, Taiwanese society lives in permanent, daily present vigilance regarding a potential military escalation. Civil defense drills, featuring island-wide air raid sirens and mandatory participation, are a part of annual routine. Strong social cohesion is evident in one of the world's highest vaccination rates and most disciplined responses to the COVID-19 pandemic—collective, coordinated action as a protective strategy. Family businesses, often passed down through generations, form the backbone of the Taiwanese economy, relying on strong trust within the family rather than external investors. Taiwan's democratic identity is consciously nurtured and defended as a counter-model and shield against its authoritarian Chinese neighbor.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "Taiwan shows a pronouncedly social Six: civil defense drills with nationwide air-raid sirens and mandatory participation are part of annual collective life, and strong social cohesion shows in one of the world's highest vaccination rates and most disciplined responses to the COVID pandemic—collective, coordinated action as a protective strategy. Democratic identity is deliberately cultivated and defended as a communal counter-model to the authoritarian Chinese neighbor. A self-preservation share shows in family businesses with strong trust within the family rather than external investors. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, collectively coordinated vigilance." },
    { name: "Singapore", iso: "SG", typ: 1, unsicher: false, text: "The sale of chewing gum has been largely banned in Singapore since 1992—an often-cited yet accurate example of the standard set for total public cleanliness and order. Strict laws, ranging from heavy fines for littering to precise rules for public conduct, are enforced consistently and without exception. The subway system is considered one of the most punctual and clean in the world, with strict rules of conduct naturally followed by most citizens. The education system, highly competitive and structured, prepares students from an early age for rigorous standardized tests. The government-promoted \"meritocracy\"—advancement through performance according to clear, established rules—is an official state principle and is consistently implemented in education and employment policy.", subtyp: "so1", subtypText: "Singapore shows a very pronouncedly social One: strict laws, from heavy fines for littering to precise rules for public behavior, are enforced consistently without exception, and the government-propagated \"meritocracy\"—advancement through achievement under clear, fixed rules—is an official, collectively binding state principle. The subway system, among the most punctual and cleanest in the world, with strict behavioral rules followed as a matter of course by most citizens, confirms this pattern of socially internalized order. A self-preservation share shows in the highly competitive education system that prepares students early for standardized tests. Sexual One recedes behind this broad, top-down enforced public order." },
    { name: "Malaysia", iso: "MY", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "In Malaysia, Malays, Chinese, Indians, and numerous indigenous groups with their respective religions—Islam, Buddhism, Hinduism, Christianity—have lived together in a balanced social framework for decades. Holidays of all major religions, from Hari Raya to Chinese New Year to Diwali, are celebrated together nationwide, often through \"Open House\" traditions where all Malaysians are invited regardless of their own faith. The \"Rukun Negara,\" the national state principles, explicitly emphasize unity and harmony among ethnic groups as the ultimate political goal. Street markets (Pasar Malam) featuring stalls of diverse ethnic cuisines side by side serve as a daily symbol of peaceful coexistence. Economic policy, too, such as quota regulations between ethnic groups, aims primarily at maintaining a balanced social equilibrium rather than inciting confrontation, despite occasional criticism.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Malaysia shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: Malays, Chinese, Indians, and indigenous groups with their own religions have lived together in a calibrated social coexistence for decades, and the \"Rukun Negara,\" the national state principles, explicitly emphasize unity and harmony between ethnic groups as the top political goal. Holidays of all major religions are celebrated together nationwide with \"Open House\" traditions, regardless of one's own faith. A self-preservation share shows in economic quota policy aimed at maintaining social balance. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, constitutionally anchored ideal of harmony." },
    { name: "North Korea", iso: "KP", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The North Korean state system relies on an omnipresent, mandatory loyalty toward the leadership, reinforced by permanent propaganda regarding external threats from the USA and South Korea. Mass parades and synchronized Mass Games involving tens of thousands of participants demonstrate collective discipline and unity both internally and externally. Mandatory military service, one of the longest in the world, and constant readiness for mobilization shape all of daily societal life. Bomb shelters and civil defense drills are part of everyday life like in almost no other country in the world—a forced, yet genuinely lived permanent vigilance. How deeply this loyalty is truly voluntary or enforced solely through control and lack of access to alternatives is difficult to assess from the outside—a country that deliberately isolates itself from external observation.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "North Korea shows a very pronouncedly social Six: mass parades and synchronized mass games with tens of thousands of participants demonstrate collective discipline and unity both outward and inward, and mandatory military service along with permanent mobilization readiness shape the whole of society as organized group vigilance. Omnipresent, mandatory loyalty to the leadership is reinforced by permanent propaganda about outside threat. A self-preservation share shows in bunker facilities and civil defense drills as concrete, everyday precaution. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, state-organized collective vigilance." },
    { name: "Bhutan", iso: "BT", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Bhutan officially replaced Gross Domestic Product with \"Gross National Happiness\" as its central state measure — contentment as a declared national goal rather than growth at any price. Deliberately closed off from the outside world until the 1970s, the country opened only cautiously and at its own pace, without subordinating itself to foreign models. Buddhism shapes daily life as a quiet, all-pervading undertone rather than a loud profession of faith. Environmental protection is constitutionally enshrined — at least 60 percent of the country's land must remain forested forever, a unique commitment to balance over exploitation. Television and the internet were only permitted in 1999, out of a deliberate concern that the country's inner calm might be disturbed by outside stimulation.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Bhutan shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Nine: television and internet were only permitted in 1999, out of a deliberate concern that the country's inner calm could be disturbed by outside stimuli, and its opening up through the 1970s occurred only carefully and at its own pace, without submitting to foreign models—protecting its own inner balance from outside influence. Environmental protection is constitutionally anchored, with at least 60 percent of land permanently forested as a commitment to balance rather than exploitation. A social share shows in \"Gross National Happiness\" as an official, collective state goal. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, resource-conscious protective stance." },
    { name: "Laos", iso: "LA", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "As the most heavily bombed country per capita in history — the US dropped more bombs on Laos during the Vietnam War than in all of World War II — the country carries a deep but publicly barely articulated war trauma. Despite this past, Laos to the present day deliberately maintains a reserved, neutral foreign policy that consistently avoids confrontation with more powerful neighbors China, Vietnam, and Thailand. Theravada Buddhism shapes daily life as a calm, composed baseline mood — the capital Vientiane is considered one of the quietest and least hectic capitals in Asia. The communist one-party system deliberately maintains a low international profile, without adopting the loud ideological tone of some neighboring countries. Unexploded ordnance from the war still claims victims every year — a quiet, ongoing legacy the country continues to live with, without great public complaint.", subtyp: "se9", subtypText: "Laos shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Nine: as the most heavily bombed country per capita in history, Laos carries a deep but publicly barely articulated war trauma, and unexploded ordnance from the war still claims victims every year—a quiet, ongoing legacy the country lives with without major public complaint. Its deliberately restrained, neutral foreign policy, which consistently avoids confrontation with more powerful neighbors, and the communist one-party system's low international visibility confirm this pattern of quiet restraint. A social share shows in Theravada Buddhism as a calm, composed everyday tone in Vientiane. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, quietly endured restraint." },
    { name: "Brunei", iso: "BN", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The small, oil-rich sultanate of Brunei pursues a deliberately reserved foreign policy focused on stability and security, consistently avoiding confrontation with its more powerful neighbors Malaysia and Indonesia. The enormous sovereign wealth and oil fund is explicitly built up as a safeguard for future generations after the oil reserves run out, not for short-term consumption. Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah, uninterruptedly in power since 1967, guarantees his citizens comprehensive social security — free education, healthcare, no income tax — in exchange for political stability and loyalty. The strict implementation of Sharia law since 2014 is officially justified in part by the preservation of social order and security, not primarily by religious expansionism. Brunei's consistently isolationist, predictability-focused position within the ASEAN region differs markedly from its more dynamic, risk-taking neighboring states.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Brunei shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Six: the enormous sovereign and oil fund is explicitly built as a safeguard for future generations after oil reserves run out, and Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah guarantees citizens comprehensive social security—free education, healthcare, no income tax—in exchange for stability and loyalty, with concrete material provision at the center. Its deliberately restrained, stability-oriented foreign policy avoids any confrontation with more powerful neighbors. A social share shows in strict Sharia implementation, officially justified by maintaining social order. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, materially safeguarded stability strategy." },
    { name: "Timor-Leste", iso: "TL", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Timor-Leste, Asia's youngest country (independent only since 2002), carries a deep national identity substantially shaped by decades of suffering — 24 years of Indonesian occupation (1975–1999) with an estimated 100,000 to 200,000 deaths have formed a collective consciousness of a deep, unique historical wound. The resistance struggle, often romanticized as a David-versus-Goliath story against an overpowering neighbor, is cultivated as a central, identity-forming national myth that distinguishes Timor-Leste from all its neighbors. The unique blend of Portuguese colonial heritage, Catholic faith (unique in a predominantly Muslim neighborhood), and Austronesian-Melanesian roots makes the country culturally unmistakable in the region. The Nobel Peace Prize for independence leaders Ramos-Horta and Bishop Belo in 1996 was felt by Timorese people as international recognition of their unique, incomparable path of suffering. The ongoing culture of remembrance around the Santa Cruz massacre and other atrocities is deliberately preserved as an unmistakable national trauma, not repressed.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Timor-Leste shows a pronouncedly social Four: the resistance struggle against an overpowering neighbor is cultivated as a central, identity-forming national myth that deliberately sets Timor-Leste apart from all its neighbors, and the ongoing culture of remembrance around the Santa Cruz massacre is preserved as a distinctive collective trauma, not repressed. The 1996 Nobel Peace Prize was felt as international recognition of a uniquely incomparable national path of suffering. A self-preservation share shows in the blend of Portuguese, Catholic, and Austronesian-Melanesian heritage as a distinctive cultural foundation. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively cultivated sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Maldives", iso: "MV", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "The Maldives, an archipelago of more than 1,000 coral islands, have deliberately positioned themselves internationally as the ultimate stage for exclusive, sensory vacation pleasure — overwater bungalows, private island resorts, and turquoise water as the trademark of boundless enjoyment. The \"one island, one resort\" tourism strategy deliberately creates secluded experiential worlds where every stay is staged as a unique, unforgettable event. Despite the real threat of rising sea levels, the government simultaneously relies on spectacular media stunts, such as an underwater cabinet meeting in 2009, to generate international attention rather than merely warn. Diving safaris to some of the most species-rich reefs in the world are marketed as adventure and discovery experiences that go beyond mere relaxation. The culture of \"bodu beru\" (large-format drum music) and exuberant island festivals shows a deeply rooted joy in communal, sensory celebration.", subtyp: "sx7", subtypText: "The Maldives shows a very pronouncedly sexual Seven: positioning itself as the ultimate stage for exclusive, sensual vacation pleasure—overwater bungalows, private island resorts, turquoise water as a hallmark of boundless indulgence—shows intensity and pleasure as a central, personally experienced principle of success. The \"one island, one resort\" strategy deliberately creates secluded worlds of experience in which every stay is staged as a unique, unforgettable event. A social share shows in spectacular, media-staged actions like the 2009 underwater cabinet meeting to generate international attention. Self-preservation Seven recedes behind this broad principle of pleasure centered on intensity and exclusivity." },
  ]},
  { region: "Oceania", laender: [
    { name: "Australia", iso: "AU", typ: 7, unsicher: false, text: "\"No worries, mate\" is likely the most frequently heard expression in Australia—a knee-jerk rejection of drama and tension in everyday life, even when real problems arise. The \"Sunday Roast\" at the beach, barbecues (barbies) with neighbors, and informal, spontaneous interactions shape a leisure culture explicitly focused on lightness. Surfing, hiking, and camping in the \"Outback\" are not luxuries, but daily, often weekly balances to professional life—the enormous geographical expanse actively invites exploration rather than retreat. The informal, often irreverently humorous style of interaction, even toward authority, is seen as uniquely Australian and is culturally valued rather than penalized. Despite occasional natural disasters such as bushfires, the Australian public maintains a strikingly calm, optimistic baseline tone—freedom and zest for experience as national priorities.", subtyp: "so7", subtypText: "Australia shows a pronouncedly social Seven: \"No worries, mate\" is the country's most-heard phrase—a reflexive, shared rejection of drama in everyday life—and the beach \"Sunday Roast\" and backyard barbecues with neighbors shape a leisure life explicitly oriented toward shared ease. The informal, often irreverently humorous manner, even toward authority, is culturally valued rather than sanctioned—a shared national tone. A self-preservation share shows in actively exploring the Outback and nature as a weekly counterbalance. Sexual Seven recedes behind this broad, publicly lived optimism." },
    { name: "New Zealand", iso: "NZ", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "New Zealand was the first country in the world to grant women the right to vote in 1893—an early, undramatic step toward greater social balance rather than forced, conflict-ridden change. The Treaty of Waitangi (1840) between the British Crown and the Māori remains to this day the foundation of an ongoing, consciously nurtured reconciliation process between the indigenous and colonial populations. The Māori concept of \"Kaitiakitanga,\" responsible stewardship of land and nature as a communal duty, increasingly shapes state environmental policy as well. New Zealand is consistently recognized internationally as one of the most politically stable and least corrupt countries—minimal social friction as a recognized national hallmark. Its remote geographical location, far removed from major global conflicts, is culturally felt as a blessing—a consciously cultivated self-understanding as a calm, untroubled haven away from global turmoil.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "New Zealand shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the Treaty of Waitangi remains to this day the basis of an ongoing, deliberately cultivated reconciliation process between indigenous and colonial populations, and the Māori concept of \"Kaitiakitanga,\" responsible stewardship of land and nature as a communal task, increasingly shapes state environmental policy. The country is consistently recognized internationally as one of the most politically stable and least corrupt—low social friction as a recognized national trademark. A self-preservation share shows in its valued self-image as a calm place removed from global turbulence. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, socially cultivated ideal of balance." },
    { name: "Fiji", iso: "FJ", typ: 2, unsicher: false, text: "The \"Bula\" greeting, with which visitors are warmly received everywhere on the islands, is far more than a formality—it expresses an actively lived, warm-hearted culture of welcome cultivated both in tourism and in daily life. The traditional Kava ceremony, where a mildly soothing root mixture is shared from a single bowl, serves explicitly to build and nurture community and trust between hosts and guests. Village communities (Koro) operate on the principle of collective responsibility for one another, led by chiefs who traditionally care for the well-being of all villagers. Extended family networks often span multiple islands, with expectations of mutual financial and practical support during difficult times. Shared meals and celebrations, often involving the entire village community, are regular, frequent features of social life, not rare exceptions.", subtyp: "so2", subtypText: "Fiji shows a very pronouncedly social Two: the \"Bula\" greeting expresses an actively lived, warm-hearted welcoming culture, and the traditional kava ceremony explicitly serves to build and maintain community and trust between hosts and guests. Village communities (Koro) function on the principle of collective responsibility for one another, with chiefs traditionally caring for the welfare of all villagers. A self-preservation share shows in extended-family networks with mutual financial support across multiple islands. Sexual Two recedes behind this broad, institutionalized culture of hospitality." },
    { name: "Papua New Guinea", iso: "PG", typ: 5, unsicher: false, text: "With over 800 distinct languages across a relatively small landmass, Papua New Guinea is the most linguistically diverse country in the world—a result of millennia of geographical isolation among individual mountain valleys and island communities. Many communities in the rugged highlands lived virtually independent of one another well into the 20th century, with no significant contact with neighboring valleys. Traditional \"Sing-Sing\" festivals, where individual tribes display their own distinct cultural identity in elaborate adornment, continue to highlight strong local rather than national belonging. Dense rainforest and mountainous terrain continue to make infrastructure and travel between regions severely difficult today, favoring distinct, mutually independent local cultures. This extreme cultural fragmentation makes generalizing statements about \"the\" culture of Papua New Guinea particularly challenging—yet the introverted independence of individual communities remains a recurring pattern.", subtyp: "se5", subtypText: "Papua New Guinea shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Five: many communities in the rugged highlands lived practically independent of one another well into the 20th century, without meaningful contact with neighboring valleys, and the dense rainforest and mountainous terrain continue to favor self-contained, mutually independent local cultures. With over 800 languages across a comparatively small area, introverted self-containment of individual communities is a recurring, geographically driven pattern. A social share shows in \"Sing-Sing\" festivals, where tribes communally display their cultural identity. Sexual Five recedes behind this broad, isolation-driven self-containment." },
    { name: "Samoa", iso: "WS", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Pacific island nation of Samoa is culturally shaped above all by \"Fa'a Samoa\" (the Samoan way) — a deeply rooted system of community, mutual respect, and consensus-building older than any Western administrative structure. The traditional matai (chief) order, in which disputes are resolved through discussion and balance rather than court rulings, remains alive alongside the state legal system even now. As one of the first Pacific countries to become independent in 1962, Samoa made the transition from New Zealand administration remarkably smoothly and undramatically. The attitude of respect (\"fa'aaloalo\") toward elders, family, and community structures almost every social interaction in daily life. Despite modernization and migration to Western countries, the Samoan diaspora preserves its community-oriented values worldwide as a unifying cultural foundation.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Samoa shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: \"Fa'a Samoa,\" the Samoan way, is a deeply rooted system of community, mutual respect, and consensus-building, and the traditional Matai council of elders resolves disputes through discussion and balance rather than court rulings. The remarkably smooth transition from New Zealand administration to independence in 1962 confirms this priority on social harmony. A self-preservation share shows in the worldwide diaspora preserving its community-oriented values as a cultural foundation. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, deeply rooted ideal of consensus." },
    { name: "Tonga", iso: "TO", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Kingdom of Tonga is the only Pacific island nation never formally colonized by a European power — a fact Tongans today proudly attribute to the skillful diplomatic safeguarding of their monarchy in the 19th century. King George Tupou I secured independence at the time through early constitution-making (1875) and treaties with Britain that guaranteed formal sovereignty, rather than risking military resistance. The monarchy, one of the last remaining in Polynesia, is valued by most Tongans as a stabilizing anchor securing continuity, despite occasional calls for more democracy. The deeply religious, Methodist-shaped society upholds strict Sunday rest and traditional values as protection against culturally perceived threatening change from outside. After the devastating volcanic eruption and tsunami of 2022, the country responded with an immediate, well-organized return to traditional self-sufficiency structures rather than external dependence.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "Tonga shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Six: King George Tupou I secured independence in the 19th century through early constitution-making and treaties with Britain rather than risking military resistance—concrete, diplomatically safeguarded survival. After the 2022 volcanic eruption, the country responded with an immediate return to traditional self-sufficiency structures rather than external dependency. The deeply religious, Methodist-shaped society upholds traditional values as protection against outside change perceived as threatening. A social share shows in the monarchy as a stabilizing anchor valued by most Tongans. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad caution strategy oriented toward concrete safeguarding." },
    { name: "Vanuatu", iso: "VU", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Vanuatu, an archipelago of more than 80 islands with over 100 different languages, is considered one of the most peaceful countries on Earth despite this extraordinary diversity, regularly ranking at the top of international happiness rankings. The traditional \"kastom\" system, a network of customs, mutual obligations, and councils of elders, prefers to resolve conflicts at the village level through balance rather than formal law. Despite decades of joint British-French colonial administration (the so-called \"Condominium,\" a unique dual-government system), the transition to independence in 1980 was comparatively peaceful. The kava ceremony, celebrated communally, serves as a central social outlet for resolving tensions between communities. Recurring natural disasters such as cyclones and volcanic eruptions are traditionally handled by residents through communal adaptation and mutual support rather than panic or conflict.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Vanuatu shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the traditional \"Kastom\" system, a network of customs, mutual obligations, and councils of elders, resolves conflicts at the village level preferably through balance rather than formal law, and despite more than 100 languages the country ranks among the most peaceful on Earth. The comparatively peaceful transition to independence in 1980 after decades of joint British-French colonial administration confirms this priority on balance. A self-preservation share shows in communal adaptation to recurring natural disasters. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, consensus-based ideal of peace." },
    { name: "Marshall Islands", iso: "MH", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "The Marshall Islands carry a deep, unique national trauma: between 1946 and 1958, the US tested 67 atomic bombs here, including the hydrogen bomb \"Castle Bravo\" on Bikini Atoll, whose fallout still renders health and land uninhabitable today. This unprecedented experience as \"the most irradiated people on Earth\" shapes national identity as uniquely affected, with suffering incomparable to any other country. The displaced residents of Bikini and other test atolls still fight today for recognition, compensation, and the right to return to a forever-altered homeland. The extreme geographic dispersal across more than 1,000 islands with only about 180 km² of land area underscores the fragility and uniqueness of Marshallese existence. As one of the countries most threatened by climate change in the world (average elevation only two meters above sea level), the Marshall Islands articulate their unique, existential vulnerability internationally very consciously and urgently.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "The Marshall Islands shows a pronouncedly social Four: the experience as the \"most irradiated people on Earth\" shapes national identity as uniquely affected, its suffering incomparable to any other country—collectively shared distinctiveness as national self-understanding. Internationally, the Marshall Islands articulates its unique, existential vulnerability to climate change very consciously and urgently before a global audience. A self-preservation share shows in the ongoing struggle of displaced Bikini residents for compensation and the right to return. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively articulated sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Palau", iso: "PW", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "Palau, a tiny island nation in the western Pacific, was one of the first countries in the world to establish a comprehensive marine protected area (the \"Palau National Marine Sanctuary,\" covering about 80% of its waters) — a deliberate choice for long-term balance with nature over short-term economic exploitation. Since 2017, visitors must sign a \"Palau Pledge\" upon entry, an environmental promise in their passport that enshrines ecological consideration as a national obligation to future generations. The traditional matrilineal social order, in which women as clan elders take on central mediating roles, emphasizes balance and consensus over hierarchical confrontation. Despite centuries of changing colonial rule (Spain, Germany, Japan, the US), Palau preserved its cultural continuity through persistent, undramatic adaptation rather than resistance. The \"bul\" tradition, a temporary fishing pause to preserve stocks, shows a centuries-old cultural practice of ecological balance that serves today as a model for modern conservation policy.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Palau shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the Palau National Marine Sanctuary, protecting about 80% of its own waters, and the \"Palau Pledge,\" an environmental promise stamped in every visitor's passport, show a deliberate, collectively anchored choice for long-term balance with nature. The traditional matrilineal social order, in which women as clan elders hold central mediating roles, emphasizes balance and consensus over hierarchical confrontation. A self-preservation share shows in the \"Bul\" tradition of temporary fishing pauses to protect stocks. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, ecologically anchored ideal of balance." },
    { name: "Kiribati", iso: "KI", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Kiribati, scattered across 33 atolls straddling both the equator and the international date line, carries a unique, existential national fate: as one of the lowest-lying countries on Earth (averaging only 1.8 meters above sea level), it faces the real risk of becoming the first country in the world to become completely uninhabitable due to rising sea levels. Former president Anote Tong symbolically purchased land in Fiji in 2014 as a possible future refuge — an unprecedented, unique step by a state actively preparing for its own possible erasure. Kiribati claims to be the first country in the world to welcome the new year (thanks to the date line shifted eastward in 1995), a small but deliberately cultivated distinguishing feature. The Micronesians of Kiribati maintain a distinctive culture, \"Te Katei ni Kiribati,\" shared by no other people, preserved with particular persistence despite colonial history and looming submersion. The national consciousness of literally struggling for its own physical existence gives Kiribati an internationally unique, barely comparable voice in climate negotiations.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Kiribati shows a pronouncedly social Four: former President Anote Tong symbolically bought land in Fiji in 2014 as a possible refuge—an unprecedented, publicly visible step by a state actively preparing for its own possible extinction before a world audience. The awareness of literally struggling for its own physical existence gives Kiribati an internationally unique, scarcely comparable voice in climate negotiations. The distinctive culture of \"Te Katei ni Kiribati\" is collectively preserved with particular persistence despite the looming threat. A self-preservation share shows in the cultivated claim to be the first country to greet the new year. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, internationally articulated sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Nauru", iso: "NR", typ: 8, unsicher: false, text: "Nauru, at about 21 km² the world's third-smallest country, experienced one of the most spectacular rises to wealth in history through phosphate mining in the 1970s — at times the highest per-capita income in the world, ruthlessly exploited to the maximum without long-term provision. When phosphate reserves were exhausted in the early 2000s, this uncompromising logic of exploitation left behind a literally devastated, largely uninhabitable island landscape. To survive economically, Nauru then pursued aggressive, controversial strategies: offshore banking with lax regulation, selling diplomatic recognition to Taiwan or China for payments, and from 2001 the controversial hosting of Australian refugee detention centers in exchange for substantial financial compensation. This willingness to uncompromisingly deploy its own sovereignty as a negotiable instrument of power to secure income shapes Nauru to the present day as a politically unpredictable, power-conscious actor despite its tiny size. The country thus shows a pattern of uncompromising, often exploitative pursuit of its own interests toward both nature and international partners.", subtyp: "se8", subtypText: "Nauru shows a very pronouncedly self-preservation Eight: phosphate mining was exploited ruthlessly and without long-term precaution to its maximum, leaving behind a literally destroyed island landscape—concrete material exploitation without regard for the future. To survive economically, Nauru then pursued aggressive strategies such as offshore banking, selling diplomatic recognition, and hosting Australian refugee camps for financial compensation—sovereignty as an uncompromising instrument of survival. A social share shows in its shifting diplomatic alignment with Taiwan or China. Sexual Eight recedes behind this broad pattern of assertiveness aimed at material survival." },
    { name: "Micronesia", iso: "FM", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Federated States of Micronesia, a loose association of four very different island groups (Chuuk, Kosrae, Pohnpei, Yap), maintain a remarkably low-conflict, balancing cohesion despite considerable cultural and geographic differences between the states. The 1979 federal constitution was deliberately designed so that each state retains extensive autonomy — a compromise model that prevents tensions through power-sharing rather than centralization. Yap's traditional stone money culture, giant limestone discs (\"rai\") that are never moved but whose change of ownership is only orally acknowledged, shows a value system based on trust and social consensus, undramatic in nature. The special status with the US known as the \"Compact of Free Association\" secures economic support and security without giving up its own cultural distinctiveness — a deliberately balanced middle path. Geographic fragmentation across more than 600 islands fosters a cultural baseline of patiently making do with distance and difference rather than forced uniformity.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "Micronesia shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: the 1979 federal constitution was deliberately designed so each state retains broad autonomy—a compromise model that prevents tension through power-sharing rather than centralization. Yap's traditional stone-money culture, whose ownership changes are recognized only orally, shows an undramatic value system based on trust and social consensus. The \"Compact of Free Association\" with the US secures support without giving up its own cultural distinctiveness—a calibrated middle path. A self-preservation share shows in the patient accommodation of geographic distance. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, federally anchored ideal of balance." },
    { name: "Tuvalu", iso: "TV", typ: 4, unsicher: false, text: "Tuvalu, with only about 26 km² of land area one of the smallest and lowest-lying countries on Earth, is running the world's first \"digital twin project\": the government is creating a complete virtual copy of the nation in the metaverse so that, should it be completely submerged by rising sea levels, the state can continue to exist digitally — a globally unique, unprecedented step. The country, which has profitably licensed its top-level domain \".tv\" to international media companies, shows a creative, distinctive response to its own economic marginality. Tuvalu's prime minister gave an internationally noted speech at the COP26 climate conference in 2021 while literally standing knee-deep in the sea — a symbolic, distinctive staging of its own unique existential situation. The tiny population of only about 11,000 people, spread across nine atolls, maintains a strong awareness of cultural distinctiveness and uniqueness despite international marginality. The prospect of possibly losing its physical homeland completely has produced a deep, collective engagement with identity, memory, and cultural continuity beyond territory.", subtyp: "so4", subtypText: "Tuvalu shows a very pronouncedly social Four: as the first country in the world, the government is creating a complete virtual copy of the nation in the metaverse to continue existing digitally as a state should it be fully submerged—a globally unique, publicly staged step. The prime minister's 2021 COP26 speech, delivered while standing knee-deep in the sea, was a symbolic, distinctive staging of the country's own existential plight before an international audience. The prospect of possibly losing its physical homeland has produced a deep, collective grappling with identity beyond territory. A self-preservation share shows in the creative licensing of the \".tv\" top-level domain. Sexual Four recedes behind this broad, collectively articulated sense of distinctiveness." },
    { name: "Solomon Islands", iso: "SB", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "The Solomon Islands, site of some of the bloodiest battles of the Pacific War (including Guadalcanal), still carry a collective awareness of military vulnerability today, reinforced by internal ethnic unrest between 1998 and 2003 (\"The Tensions\") that necessitated an international peacekeeping mission (RAMSI). Since then, the country has relied on a carefully balanced network of international security guarantees, most recently visible in the internationally controversial 2022 security agreement signed with China as additional protection alongside traditional Australian partnerships. Traditional \"wantok\" solidarity — mutual obligation within language groups and clans — still serves today as an informal social safety net supplementing state institutions where these are perceived as inadequate. Extreme geographic fragmentation across nearly 1,000 islands complicates central control and fosters an awareness of local vulnerability, compensated for by close clan and island ties. Recurring natural disasters (earthquakes, tsunamis, cyclones) reinforce a cultural baseline of vigilance and precautionary safeguarding against unpredictable external threats.", subtyp: "se6", subtypText: "The Solomon Islands shows a clearly self-preservation Six: traditional \"Wantok\" solidarity—mutual obligation within language groups and clans—still serves as an informal, concrete social safety net supplementing state institutions, and recurring natural disasters reinforce a cultural attitude of vigilance and precautionary safeguarding. The country additionally relies on a carefully balanced network of international security agreements, most recently with China alongside traditional Australian partnerships. A social share shows in the collective awareness of military vulnerability since the Pacific War. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, clan- and alliance-based safeguarding strategy." },
    { name: "Cook Islands", iso: "CK", typ: 9, unsicher: false, text: "The Cook Islands, in free association with New Zealand, deliberately cultivate a political model that reconciles independence with a close, tension-free bond to a larger partner, rather than choosing one over the other. The concept of the \"Cook Islands Way of Life,\" blending traditional Polynesian values with modern administration, is deliberately marketed as a harmonious middle path between tradition and progress. The 15 scattered islands, culturally and geographically quite different from one another, are held together through a carefully balanced, consensus-oriented political system that deliberately avoids rivalry between islands. The traditional \"tivaevae\" craft, made communally at sociable sewing gatherings, symbolizes the islands' close, low-conflict community culture. The predominantly Christian, relaxed way of life on the Cook Islands is described by locals and visitors alike as especially peaceful and balanced.", subtyp: "so9", subtypText: "The Cook Islands shows a very pronouncedly social Nine: its political model of free association with New Zealand deliberately reconciles independence with a close, tension-free bond to a larger partner, rather than choosing one or the other. The 15 scattered, culturally very different islands are held together through a calibrated, consensus-oriented political system that deliberately avoids rivalries between islands. The communally crafted \"Tivaevae\" needlework symbolizes the close, low-conflict community culture. A self-preservation share shows in the relaxed, balanced way of life. Sexual Nine recedes behind this broad, consensus-oriented model of harmony." },
    { name: "New Caledonia", iso: "NC", typ: 6, unsicher: false, text: "New Caledonia, a French overseas territory in the South Pacific with a unique special status negotiated over decades, has chosen a deliberately cautious, step-by-step path to political self-determination through three independence referendums (2018, 2020, 2021) rather than an abrupt break. The deeply divided society between indigenous Kanak people and descendants of French settlers has lived since the violent conflicts of the 1980s (\"Les Événements\") under a carefully balanced, institutionally secured power-sharing system (the 1998 Nouméa Accord). The violent unrest of 2024, triggered by planned electoral reforms, once again showed how fragile this security balance remains and how vigilantly both sides defend their hard-won guarantees. The significant nickel mining industry, the island's economic backbone, is regarded by the population as a shared asset to be safeguarded, over which reliable participation models have been contested for years. France's continued military and administrative presence is valued by many residents as a guarantor of stability, criticized by others as a continuation of colonial paternalism.", subtyp: "so6", subtypText: "New Caledonia shows a pronouncedly social Six: the deeply divided society between indigenous Kanak and descendants of French settlers has lived since the 1980s in a carefully calibrated, institutionally safeguarded power-sharing system (the 1998 Nouméa Accord)—security through a collectively negotiated group system. The three independence referendums show a deliberately cautious, step-by-step path to self-determination rather than an abrupt break. The violent unrest of 2024 again showed how vigilantly both sides defend their hard-won guarantees. A self-preservation share shows in nickel mining regarded as a shared asset requiring safeguarding. Sexual Six recedes behind this broad, institutionally negotiated security balance." },
  ]},
];
